:bust_in_silhouette: **lahoda** &nbsp; :clock8: Sep 29, 2009 at 10:38 UTC

## Consider this code:

package a;

import java.io.IOException;
import lombok.SneakyThrows;

public class Main {

```
public static void main(String[] args) {
    new Runnable() {
        @ SneakyThrows(IOException.class)
        public void run() {
            throw new IOException();
        }
    };
}
```

## }

When building this code with javac, the attached exception occurs.

This is (AFAIK) caused by the fact that bodies of methods are inspected in
the attribute phase, but annotation processing is performed after the enter
phase. Synthetic &quot;value=&quot; assignment is generated during enter phase for
annotations on members/classes, but is generated during attribute phase for
the annotation on a method inside anonymous innerclass (see
MemberEnter.visitMethodDef/MemberEnter.enterAnnotations/Annotate.enterAnnotation).

