:bust_in_silhouette: **reinierz** &nbsp; :clock8: Oct 16, 2010 at 20:49 UTC

Wow. I was typing a reply to you and I think I stumbled on the answer! (Self = type name of class where the code is generated)

We change @ EQAHC to also add the following method (this is the biggest stumbling block - folks will see this method and go: Huh? Where the heck is this coming from?!?):

protected java.lang.Class<?> equalityLevel() {
    return Self.class; // note: _NOT_ this.getClass();!
}

The type equivalency test in equals() is then redefined. We currently use:

if (!this.getClass().equals(other.getClass()) return false;

We'd replace this with:

if (!(other instanceof Self)) return false;
if (this.equalityLevel() != other.equalityLevel()) return false;

This has the exact same effect _IF_ this class and all subclasses use @ Data or @ EqualsAndHashCode to generate their equals and hashCode methods - lombok will end up writing an override equalityLevel() method with a new type in it, and thus all equality tests between different subtypes will all return false, which is the correct default.

However, generated subtypes, such as JPA proxies, will NOT override equalityLevel (nor will they override equals and hashCode), and thus equality between the base and the proxy can be true.

Now we get to the second stumbling block:

Subclasses written without the use of @ Data or @ EQAHC need to make sure they override equalityLevel() if they also overwrite equals and hashCode. Preferably equals and hashCode would have been final UNLESS the class also overrides equalityLevel but we cannot enforce this. There's some precedent for this, of course (if you override hashCode or equals(), you must also override the other one, but the java language has no way of enforcing this. We're just adding a third method to the set). At best we can emit a warning if we notice this happening, but that warning will obviously only show up if lombok is installed. Lombok also isn't a style checker, so that would be diversifying a bit too much (for those that ALSO use a style checker, it would be rather annoying if lombok also told them about the equals/hashCode issue, together with their style checker, and lombok has no settings area to turn it off).

