It's pretty common to have a situation where an api is imposed on you which only can throw a single exception, but the implementation of the api can throw many more.  You end up having to do something like this:

public void foo() throws A {
  try {
    ...
  } catch (Throwable t) {
    if (t instance of A) 
      throw t;
    else
      throw new A(t);
  }
}

It would be wonderful to have an annotation do this boilerplate for you.  Maybe just this?

@ WrapExceptions(wrapper=A.class)
public void foo() throws A {
  ...
}

Thanks again for this project and allowing me to hack out much of the annoying boilerplate from my code.

