:bust_in_silhouette: **Maaartinus** &nbsp; :clock8: Jul 18, 2011 at 20:02 UTC

IMHO, by default also Errors shouldn't be wrapped, since Errors also don't need to be declared. I'm not sure if optionally specifying the exception to be wrapped is the way to go; doing it the other way round may be better:

public @ interface WrapperException {
    Class<? extends Throwable> wrapper();
    Class<? extends Throwable>[] exclude() default {RuntimeException.class, Error.class};
    // Class<? extends Throwable>[] include() default {Throwable.class};
}

Note that by using "include" (i.e., specifying the exceptions to be wrapped) there's AFAIK no way to express the default behavior. Maybe both "include" and "exclude" may be useful. Conflicts could be ideally solved in favor of the more specific exception, but this would require resolution, and maybe simply giving precedence to "exclude" could do.

