Unfortunately, we can only modify syntactically correct java files. That means, the parser must understand it (it doesn't have to work though) and only then hands the AST to Lombok. So we cannot use the try () {} syntax for java<7.

We did investigate throwing the "right" exception. But it turned out that we cannot make it work because it messes with the definite assignment rules or we cannot ensure the compiler knows what exceptions are thrown either by using the variable or calling the close method. 

See http://java.sun.com/docs/books/jls/third_edition/html/defAssign.html for more on definite assignment.

public void foo() throws IOException {
  @ Cleanup final InputStream in = create();
  // Some code throwing IOException
}

Would need to generate something like this:

public void foo() throws IOException {
  @ Cleanup final InputStream in = create();
  Throwable thrownInBlock = null;
  try {
    // Some code throwing IOException
  }
  catch (Throwable t1) {
    thrownInBlock = t1;
  }
  finally {
    Throwable thrownInClose = null;
    if (in != null) {
      try {
        in.close();
      }
      catch (Throwable t2) {
        thrownInClose = t2;
      }
    }
    if (thrownInBlock != null) {
      // ignore thrownInClose? 
      // How do we make this IOException?
      // We cannot throw Throwable, the method signature does not allow us
      throw lombok.Lombok.sneakyThrow(thrownInBlock); 
    }
    if (thrownInClose != null) {
      // How do we make this IOException?
      // We cannot throw Throwable, the method signature does not allow us
      throw lombok.Lombok.sneakyThrow(thrownInBlock); 
    }
  }
}

