I think there is not really a reason to do this cast. Instead of having code like this: 

Foo other = (Foo) obj;
if (bar == null) {
    if (other.bar != null)
        return false;
} else if (!bar.equals(other.bar))
    return false;

you should do this: 

Foo other = (Foo) obj;
Object _bar = this.bar;
if (_bar == null) {
    if (other.bar != null)
        return false;
} else if (!_bar.equals(other.bar))
    return false;

The compiler than will generate calls to Object﹟equals(Object) no matter whether there is an equals method with SelfType argument. 

In large software projects with a lot of developers like ours we made the experience that developers are not impressed by comitting files with compile warnings. Either they do ignore them or in worst case they place the @ SuppressWarnings annotation without considering why the compiler is blaming them. 
So we decided to define most of the code-sytles warnings as errors. 
I don't want to slacken these settings for a bunch of projects with some hundred thousands of handwritten lines of code just because I'd like to allow the usage of lombok there. 

So I would be very happy if this issue is fixed.

