:bust_in_silhouette: **adambrewster** &nbsp; :clock8: Nov 28, 2012 at 21:46 UTC

You're right, that didn't fix the problem.

I'll add that another workaround for this problem is to add the "missing" methods to an interface implemented by the intermediate class.

In other words, the following works:

public class DelegateBug {
    public void test() {
        new A().c();
    }
}

class A {
    @ Delegate
    private final B b = new B();
}

class B implements I { // <---
    @ Delegate
    private final C c = new C();
}

class C {
    public void c() {
    }
}

interface I {
    void c();
}

In practice this has not been much of a burden to me.  The nuisance level was so low that I had believed this bug to be fixed for several months.  I always delegate to interfaces, and I always implement the interface to which I delegate.  In that case transitive delegation just works.

I will suggest, however, that if transitive delegation can't be make to work in javac that it be turned off in eclipse as well.  While I think that transitive delegation is the nicest (and most expected behavior), I find it very bothersome when eclipse disagrees with my build system on what constitutes valid input.

