I would appreciate `@NonBlank` and `@NonEmpty` as well. For anyone looking for a workaround with not much boilerplate you may give a try to a _Tiny Type_ pattern. Consider following examples.

Tiny type ensuring fail fast on a blank or null value.
```java
import static org.apache.commons.lang3.Validate.notBlank;

@EqualsAndHashCode
public class NonBlankString {
    private final String value;

    public NonBlankString(String value) {
        this.value = notBlank(value);
    }
}
```

Any class at your codebase utilising the above.
```java
@Builder
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class Foo {
    @NonNull
    private final NonBlankString alias;
    @NonNull
    private final NonBlankString key;
    @NonNull
    private final NonBlankString id;
}
```

Not ideal as it may require some extra mappers/handlers/serializers elsewhere. Those are usually done once as a framework/lib config whilst your model and logic classes may remain concise and blank safe at the same time.

There is yet some hassle with building an object as it would go like `Foo.builder().id(new NonEmptyString("123"))...` which is noisy. However if you let serialise/deserialise data to such tiny types only at the boundaries of your system and later pass them around, you would not face that issue in prod code at all.

In tests, on the other hand, you may extend builder and add convenience overloaded setter methods, something like:

```java
public class TestFooBuilder extends FooBuilder {
    TestFooBuilder id(String value) {
        this.id(new NonBlankString(value));
        return this;
    }
}
```
That's a bit of a pain but pays back quickly when re-used a lot. Also such test builder may become a fixture with pre-set all the necessary values.

Of course an overloaded method may be added straight away in the Lombok's generated builder too if that's the preference, but this gets more noisy in prod code so we're almost back to the former issue.

You may also go more specific with tiny types which actually is their real purpose and (following the example above) create classes like:
- `public class Id extends NonBlankString { /* ... */ }`
- `public class Key extends NonBlankString { /* ... */ }`
- `public class Alias extends NonBlankString { /* ... */ }`

It seems like an overhead at first but you may quickly change your mind when you search for usages of i.e. `Key` where `String` would list you more than half of the codebase :trollface:, or when i.e. it appears that `Id` is not only non-blank but needs to match a certain length or pattern - a change in one place at the `Id` class constructor and voila!

`String` is final but it may get more funky and hasslefree with collections if you are willing to use experimental Lombok feature.
```java
import static org.apache.commons.lang3.Validate.notEmpty

@EqualsAndHashCode
public class NonEmptyList<E> implements List<E> {
    @Delegate
    List<E> source;

    public NonEmptyList(List<E> source) {
        this.source = notEmpty(source);
    }
}
```

Having written all of that, it would be great to have the `@NonEmpty` for collection and `@NonBlank` for string annotations which would cover like 80-90% of use cases :roll_eyes:

I'd like to offer the candid initial reaction perspective of a casual Java programmer coming into this discussion cold.  

There appear to be deep explanations of whether this feature "should" be supported or if it's a code smell.  But if we're going to get pedantic, the Lombok itself probably is a bastardization and should be avoided.  From my myopic view, Lombok exists only to reduce boilerplate Java and make my life easier.  By using it, I'm signing a waiver that I might fall into traps or develop bad habits.

I maintain shitty legacy code that has unsanitized inputs and am often finding myself doing:

```
public void doSomething(String input){
      if (StringUtils.isBlank(input)){
         throw new RuntimeException("Bad input yada yada")
    }
}
```

Seems like the perfect candidate for annotation, and that's about where my brain shuts off.  As the library maintainers, obviously, you must consider the problem more deeply.  But if your hangup is that this violates some nuanced idioms of Java, or that this is a code smell and we probably should rethink the code... then ... we don't care and just want something to reduce lines.  

Thanks for a great library.

I want to support this feature request, especially when handling String data types. 

There are often implementation cases where there is a String member variable, which should be not null (already handled via @NonNull), but is also required to have content. Sometimes whitespaces as the only content are allowed (trim=false), sometimes there has to be content left after trimming it (trim=true).

My proposal for syntax:
```
//content is not null and not empty after trimming
@NonBlank
String s;
```

```
//content is not null and not empty
@NonBlank( trim=false )
String s;
```

The situations I need this kind of validation is frequently and I have to interfere with the lombok handling to somehow add this to my code, which bloats it and makes it less readable.

I really like using lombok a lot and hope you consider adding this feature, which will certainly make many developers very happy.

