:bust_in_silhouette: **reinierz** &nbsp; :clock8: Jan 14, 2014 at 20:53 UTC

That sounds simple but it isn't. The initializing expressions aren't easily copyable. "Bob" is copyable, but callToSomeMethod() or callToSomeSuperMethod() won't be. refToOtherField.foo(); isn't copyable either.

So if we can't copy these initializers, what we can try and do is create every possibly constructor variant to cover all permutations of leaving 1 or more of the fields with an initializer 'unset' so that their initializer will 'stick', but that isn't just hugely unwieldy, it is not possible; if some of your fields have the same type you get duplicate constructor signatures which won't compile. We can also just clone the entire initializer straight into the constructor but that suffers from the same problem: Not all initializers are copyable like that.

That's one side of the equation. The other side is how the builder communicates these; one way is to treat the default builder initialization (which is always null / 0 / false / etc) as a sign that the field initializer should be used instead, but now it is impossible to explicitly set them to null / 0 / false / etc. In order to support that, every field in builder should become something like AtomicReference<T> so that we can tell the difference between a set null and an unset null, OR we need to create a matching boolean field for every field which covers whether or not the field has been set. That is possible of course, but neither easy nor elegant.

Add these issues together and it's hugely complicated. Here's a practical workaround. To your sample, add the following:

private Person(String name, int age) {
    if (name == null) name = "Bob";
    if (age == 0) age = 30;
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
}

