:bust_in_silhouette: **Maaartinus** &nbsp; :clock8: May 03, 2014 at 15:35 UTC

FWIW I also don't think that final implies anything here as the builder uses its own non-final variables, which can be left unassigned or assigned any number of times (just like local variables in a constructor).

I can see that the problem of forgotten variables is real, but I can't see any good solution. Generating a compile-time error is impossible since the information is not available at the call site. A "nanny" Builder returning a different class after each assignment was already refused.

Still, you can do better than static factories:

@ Builder public class X {
    @ NonNull private final String requiredA, requiredB;
    private String optionalC, optionalD;

```
public static XBuilder builder(String requiredA, String requiredB) {
    return new XBuilder()
        .requiredA(checkNotNull(requiredA))
        .requiredB(checkNotNull(requiredB));
}
```

}

This still allows re-assignment in the Builder (which someone surely dislikes), but it surely prevents forgetting.

So I'd like to propose the following:
1. Let `requiredA(null)` throw because of the `@ NonNull` annotation. Quite often it doesn't help much as it happens close to where `build()` gets called, but (a) it's simple, (b) sometimes builders get passed around, (c) it simplifies the above example, (d) being fail-fast is a good thing, and (e) there is no downside.
2. Allow to specify the `builder` method signature somehow. I don't think that always adding all `final @ NonNull` fields to it would do. I'd personally be happy with

@ Builder(startWith=2)

or something like

@ Builder public class X {
    @ Builder.StartWith @ NonNull private final String requiredA, requiredB;
    private String optionalC, optionalD;
}

