:bust_in_silhouette: **illya.kokshenev** &nbsp; :clock8: Jun 01, 2014 at 01:18 UTC

The problem is that equals() implementation fails for generic fields when
they are represented with arrays.

Consider the following class:

@ EqualsAndHashCode
@ AllArgsConstructor
public class Item&lt;T&gt; {
    final private T value;
}

After de-lombok we got equals() implementation like that:

  public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (o == this) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Item)) return false;
        final Item other = (Item) o;
        if (!other.canEqual((Object) this)) return false;
        final Object this$value = this.value;
        final Object other$value = other.value;
        if (this$value == null ? other$value != null : !this$value.equals(other$value)) return false;
        return true;
    }

Obviously, the equals contract will fail in case of Item&lt;int[]&gt;, since
Object.equals() method will be used instead of Arrays.deepEquals(). 

My solution is to modify the the line 

if (this$value == null ? other$value != null : !this$value.equals(other$value)) return false;

with the following:

if (this$value == null ? other$value != null :
 !(
   this$value.getClass().isArray()?
   Arrays.deepEquals(new Object[]{this$value},new Object[]{other$value}):
   this$value.equals(other$value))
  )
return false;

The trick with Arrays.deepEquals(new Object[]{this$value},...) maybe is not the fastest, but it avoids painful (and maybe slow) type checks and castings for different kinds of arrays (int[], double[], Object[], etc).

A small test unit with the both, original and proposed implementations is attached.

P.S: I guess hashCode impl. should be modified as well.

