@jennierose I don't think that's a good example. I find that code deplorable - it's going to cause confusion, and given that security issues usually cannot be easily tested, confusion is _deadly_. You have a field named `password`, and it is lying to you. It doesn't contain the password at all. It contains a passhash.

That class should be written as follows:

```java
@Data
public class User {
    private static final PasswordEncoder ENCODER = new BCryptPasswordEncoder();

    // Depending on what you want, you may want to tag this with:
    // @Setter(AccessLevel.NONE)
    private String passHash;

    public void setPassword(@NonNull String password) {
        passHash = ENCODER.encode(password);
    }
}
```

After all, in your example, your User class would operate as `.setPassword("hello")` works fine, and then `.getPassword()` returns something completely different. See how that is confusing?

Once we have the above established is the proper way to write the class, we then get to the gnarly question of what the builder should look like. In this case we really don't want a `setPassHash` style method in that builder... or do we? Maybe you have a passHash instead. Should it be possible to make new user objects based on a passHash? If you're creating User objects based on JPA or any other database solution, then you surely do - the passHash is all you have. In fact, in practice, when would you ever create a user object using an actual password, via a builder? Perhaps when the user signs up, and you're about to `save()` this user object to the database? In which case it might just be smarter to leave the pass hashing separate from the builder step. Perhaps you should simply be writing:

```java
  User.builder()
    .passHash(User.hashPassword(passEnteredOnNewUserForm))
   .build();
```

Where `hashPassword` is a method you write in an appropriate context - could be a `static` method in `User` itself, or if you have some sort of `UserFactory` because the pass hash algorithm is configurable, then in that factory.

The question more generally boils down to: What if I want an alternate way to set a property and also want to eliminate the normal way to set that property. You can trivially do the alternate-set thing (write the actual builder class and include the method), but telling builder to not generate the usual setter is not currently possible. Given that I don't see an easy syntactical way to do so, I doubt we'll ever add such a thing.

I in fact adopt your case as proof that we should _not_ do this. Feels like delivering this feature is the proverbial handing the foot-bazooka to journeyman developer and watching the fireworks.

