Good point! I agree @Cleanup should preserve suppressed exceptions whenever possible.

In practice, it won't make much difference. Storing suppressed exceptions in Throwable is only available in Java 7 or later, but @Cleanup is only really useful prior to Java 7 (before try-with-resources became available). Even so, making @Cleanup fully compatible with try-with-resources in Java 7 or later is an excellent idea.

In this case, the original example:

```
public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
    @Cleanup InputStream in = new FileInputStream(args[0]);
    in.read();
    ...
}

```

would be better expanded in Lombok as follows:

```
public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
    InputStream in = new FileInputStream(args[0]);
    try {
        in.read();
        ...
        if (in != null) {
            try {
                in.close();
            } finally {
                in = null;
            }
        }
    } catch (Throwable t) {
        if (in != null) {
            try {
                in.close();
            } catch (Throwable e) {
                // t.addSuppressed(e);
                try {
                    Throwable.class.getMethod("addSuppressed", new Class[] { Throwable.class }).invoke(t, e);
                } catch (Exception e2) {
                    // ignore NoSuchMethodException, etc.
                }
            }
        }
        throw Lombok.sneakyThrow(t);
    }
}

```

