A more appropriate implementation based on Oracle's example, also taking into account arbitrary exceptions and also validating if the resource wasn't set to null, should take the original example:

```
public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
    @Cleanup InputStream in = new FileInputStream(args[0]);
    in.read();
    ...
}
```

then expand it in Lombok as follows:

```
public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
    InputStream in = new FileInputStream(args[0]);
    Throwable $t = null;
    try {
        in.read();
        ...
    } catch (Throwable t) {
        $t = t;
        throw Lombok.sneakyThrow(t);
    } finally {
        if (in != null) {
            if ($t != null) {
                try {
                    in.close();
                } catch (Throwable e) {
                    // $t.addSuppressed(e);
                    try {
                        Throwable.class.getMethod("addSuppressed", new Class[] { Throwable.class }).invoke($t, e);
                    } catch (Throwable e2) {
                        // ignore NoSuchMethodException, etc. (in case Java < 1.7)
                    }
                }
            } else {
                in.close();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

I only disagree about providing special treatment for InterruptedExceptions. Perhaps this could be somewhat convenient, but it would make this functionality have an inconsistent non-intuitive behaviour, that would differ from try-with-resources. Inconsistent behaviours make it harder for programmers to use and test their code properly in order to avoid unwanted side-effects. The drawbacks would surpass the benefits in this case, IMHO.

The example that is in my head is that I launch a FutureTask (ie, thread) which catches and handles some exceptions, and lets others propagate up and kill it. For example, the cancellation mechanism for tasks uses `thread.interrupt` and requires that InterruptedException is not caught.

Consider that in the following code, the download throws a CommunicationException which normally causes the download to be re-tried. However, while waiting to close CommunicationChannel, the thread receives an interrupt and tries to throw an InterruptedException. However, this exception is suppressed and the cancellation does not happen.

```
FutureTask task = new FutureTask (this::task);
    executorService.submit (this::task);

...

task.cancel(); // task thread will be interrupted, throwing an InterruptedException
// the InterruptedException must not be caught, or the task will not cancel


int task() throws InterruptedException
{
    boolean retry = true;
    while (retry)
    {
        try
        {
            try (CommunicationChannel channel = ...)
            {
                // perform download
                // fail and throw a CommunicationException

                retry = false;
            }
            finally
            {
                // channel.close() will wait and can throw an InterruptedException
                // which may be suppressed
            }
        }
        catch (CommuncationException e)
        {
            // ignore exception and retry
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Hence I said that when two exceptions are thrown, the path of execution should return as far up the stack as possible.

