Assume we have class:

```
@Builder
public class MyClass {
    private int a;
}
```

After processing it will became:

```
public class MyClass {
    private int a;

    MyClass(int a) {
        this.a = a;
    }

    public static MyClass.MyClassBuilder builder() {
        return new MyClass.MyClassBuilder();
    }

    public static class MyClassBuilder {
        private int a;

        MyClassBuilder() {
        }

        public MyClass.MyClassBuilder a(int a) {
            this.a = a;
            return this;
        }

        public MyClass build() {
            return new MyClass(this.a);
        }

        public String toString() {
            return "MyClass.MyClassBuilder(a=" + this.a + ")";
        }
    }
}
```

And somewhere in code it can be used:

```
MyClass.builder().a(42).build();
```

But what will happen if we add new field b to out class?

```
@Builder
public class MyClass {
    private int a;
    private int b;
}
```

Everything will compile as before. Sometimes (often?) it is not desired. `@Builder` should have option to forbid leave fields unassigned.
Also there is another problem with IDE support (a and b already assigned, but IntelliJ Idea does not understand that it should suggest something else. It is easy to forget set some fields in case of big data class):
![image](https://cloud.githubusercontent.com/assets/283980/9247410/75a4d388-41bb-11e5-9579-0580733e3b7a.png)

Possible solution is to use Staged builder. Generated code will be:

```
public class MyClass {
    private int a;
    private int b;

    MyClass(int a, int b) {
        this.a = a;
        this.b = b;
    }

    public static MyClassBuilderStage1 builder() {
        return new MyClass.MyClassBuilder();
    }

    public static class MyClassBuilder implements MyClassBuilderStage1, MyClassBuilderStage2, MyClassBuilderStage3 {
        private int a;
        private int b;

        MyClassBuilder() {
        }

        public MyClassBuilderStage2 a(int a) {
            this.a = a;
            return this;
        }

        public MyClassBuilderStage3 b(int b) {
            this.b = b;
            return this;
        }

        public MyClass build() {
            return new MyClass(this.a, this.b);
        }

        public String toString() {
            return "MyClass.MyClassBuilder(a=" + this.a + ", b=" + this.b + ")";
        }

    }


    public interface MyClassBuilderStage1{
        MyClassBuilderStage2 a(int a);
    }

    public interface MyClassBuilderStage2{
        MyClassBuilderStage3 b(int a);
    }

    public interface MyClassBuilderStage3{
        MyClass build();
    }

}
```

## Issue Summary

A constructor generated for a subclass does not include fields that are inherited from its parent. This behavior is identical with `@RequiredArgsConstructor` and `@AllArgsConstructor`.
## Example

``` java
@Data
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.JOINED)
public abstract class Person {
    @NonNull
    private String name;
}
```

``` java
@Data
@AllArgsConstructor
public class Programmer extends Person {
    @NonNull
    private String favoriteLanguage;
}
```
### Expected Constructor

``` java
public Programmer(String name, String favoriteLanguage);
```
### Actual Constructor

``` java
public Programmer(String favoriteLanguage);
```
### Additional Notes

I have also tried the following with the same result:
- Removing `abstract` keyword from parent class
- Adding `@AllArgsConstructor` to parent class

