Sometimes you may want a builder for a class, say `User`, that you can't (or won't) annotate with `@Builder`.  In such cases, the only approach I've found is to have a separate class with a method that is annotated with `@Builder` and returns a `User`.  For example:

``` java
@Getter
@Setter
public class User {
    private String firstName;
    private String lastName;
    private int age;
    private List<String> aliases = new ArrayList<>();
    private StatusType status = StatusType.ACTIVE;
}
```

``` java
public class MetaBuilder {
    @Builder(builderClassName = "UserBuilder", builderMethodName = "userBuilder")
    private static User constructUser(String firstName, String lastName, int age, List<String> aliases, StatusType status) {
        User user = new User();
        user.setFirstName(firstName);
        user.setLastName(lastName);
        user.setAge(age);
        user.setAliases(aliases);
        user.setStatus(status);
        return user;
    }

    @Builder(builderClassName = "CompanyBuilder", builderMethodName = "companyBuilder")
    private static Company constructCompany(...) {
        ...
    }
}
```

Lombok determines that every parameter in the method should be exposed fluently and you can then use it like this:

``` java
MetaBuilder.userBuilder().firstName("John").lastName("Doe").age(30).build();
```

However, there are a few problems with this approach.

First, as the `User` class grows with new attributes, so does the `constructUser` builder method with evermore parameters.  Ideally, it would be great if I could tell Lombok that I'm building a `User` and that it should automatically expose all of its attributes as setters.  Basically, this is what Lombok does today when the annotation is placed directly on the `User` class.  That means the mechanics are there, but I'd like to be able to use it from outside the `User` class.  Is there currently any way to do this?

Second, in the `constructUser` method, if either `aliases` or `status` are null, there's no way to know if it's because the caller issued `.aliases(null).status(null)` or simply did not set values.  That means that it makes impossible to keep the default values that are set in `User` at initialization without putting in some additional logic in the `constructUser` method, like this:

``` java
@Builder(builderClassName = "UserBuilder")
private User constructUser(String firstName, String lastName, int age, List<String> aliases, StatusType status) {
    ...
    if (aliases != null) {
        user.setAliases(aliases);
    }
    if (status != null) {
        user.setStatus(status);
    }
    ...
```

From past discussions (ex: https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/issues/916), I understand why this happens.  However, I think there could be a fairly straightforward enhancement that could solve the problem.  If Lombok were to track a `dirty` flag for every setter method it generates, it could tell the difference between a value that was explicitely set to `null` and one that was left untouched by the caller.  Or it could use an `Optional<T>` to differentiate between null (no value set), empty (null was explicitely provided) and a value.  In the generated constructor, these flags could be checked before deciding whether to overwrite any value.  This behaviour could be made backward compatible by requiring a new `@Builder`  configuration attribute to be set to be activated.

What do you think?

