I like the syntax of the assert statement. It's very clean. What I hate are the semantics. I would love to be able to use `assert` for actual checking of preconditions, postconditions, and invariants instead of `if` statements. Here is my proposal:

```
@BulletproofAsserts
public class Foo {
    void bar(int param) {
        assert param >= 0 : new IllegalArgumentException ("param");

        ...
    }
}
```

becomes:

```
public class Foo {
    void bar(int param) {
        if (!(param >= 0))
            throw new IllegalArgumentException ("param");

        ...
    }
}
```

The assertions become always-enabled and we can pick what exception is thrown. If the expression to the right of `:` is not a Throwable, then we should wrap that object in an AssertionError (as usual).

There is no mechanism to disable the assertions. Disabling assertions is usually a bad idea. It is critically important that invariant checks continue to be done in production. This is because perfect test coverage is impossible, and all production code will have bugs which assertions should help to diagnose and mitigate.

