Hi, we're using FindBugs to show additional warnings and noticed a somewhat illogical null check under certain conditions. Take the following code:

``` java
public class Foo {

    public Foo(@NonNull String a) {
        this(a, "");
    }


    public Foo(@NonNull String a, @NonNull String b) {
    }
}
```

This translates to: 

``` java
public class Foo {
    public Foo(@NonNull String a) {
        this(a, "");
        if(a == null) {
            throw new NullPointerException("a");
        }
    }

    public Foo(@NonNull String a, @NonNull String b) {
        if(a == null) {
            throw new NullPointerException("a");
        } else if(b == null) {
            throw new NullPointerException("b");
        }
    }
}
```

This gives a warning in FindBugs, because as you can see, the first null check is happening after the parameters is handed over in `this(a, "");` to the other constructor. Naturally, the null check couldn't happen earlier, as `this` has to come first. Thus, the null check should not be created, as it is also happening in the second constructor anyways.

(FindBug's message is "Nullcheck of a at line 15 of value previously dereferenced in new 
Foo(String) [Troubling(11), Normal  confidence]")

By the way, thanks a lot for Lombok, we use it throughout the entire company and it makes code so much more readable.

