Adding support for `<T> T lombok.Lombok.checkNotNull(T value, String message)` and rewriting the bytecode in a post-compiler isn't much harder, and it doesn't leave the classes peppered with generated methods. They do show up in the outline, code completion and reflection.

The somewhat hard part is to check if the parameter is used in the this-call or super-call and then wrap it in a checkNotNull call and not generate an explicit null check (if-statement) later.

For instance:

``` java
class Foo extends Bar
    Foo(@NonNull value, boolean toggle) {
        super(toggle ? value : null);
    }
}
```

In this case, should we also add a null-check after the super call? I think we should.

So for now, we stick to the current implementation and only have a null-check after the this-or-super-call.

If we add both, we will get other warnings.

Small addition: If you add suppresswarning to these calls, it will no longer show up in FindBugs, though it's not exactly nice looking:

```java
public class Foo {

    @SuppressFBWarnings ("RCN_REDUNDANT_NULLCHECK_WOULD_HAVE_BEEN_A_NPE") // see https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/issues/1134
    public Foo(@NonNull String a) {
        this(a, "");
    }


    public Foo(@NonNull String a, @NonNull String b) {
    }
}
```

@FeanorsCurse AFAIK, there's already such an option:
Add "lombok.extern.findbugs.addSuppressFBWarnings=true" to your "lombok.config".
It makes FB to ignore all warnings an all generated methods.

Also, RCN_REDUNDANT_NULLCHECK_OF_NONNULL_VALUE is triggered by:

```java
@Value class Foo {@NonNull String bar;}
```

Looks like superfluous null checks are injected into `equals(Object)` and `hashCode()`. Not sure if this is worthy of a distinct bug report.

