Just make field protected and keep the test in the same package as the tested class. I mean different folders (main/test, in case of maven), but same package hierarchy. Then no lombok magic is needed and you get the desired behaviour with pure Java.

P.S.: In most cases test classes SHOULD NOT extend the tested class, but "protected" still works here. Talking about accessibility, "protected" stands between "public" and "package" (default) modifiers, not between "public" and "private". So such properties are accessible while inherited and ALSO accessible from the same package.

``` java
public class MyClass {
    protected SomeType field;

    public void someMethod() {
    //...
    }
}

public class MyClassTest {
    @Test
    public void someMethod_withSomeCondition_success() {
        ...
    }

    @Test
    public void someVerySpecificTest_withFieldAccess() {
        field = ... // use it here
    }
}
```

P.P.S: Still I believe that even such level of access is mostly useful only for some utilities, which can be common between main code and test code. Tests with such a low-level access does not make much sense - you should test some atomic "subflows" which will be maintained (and may be updated) in the future.

P.P.P.S: If you need to mock something private, take a look on powermock project.

