Consider this code.

``` java
@EqualsAndHashCode
class Foo {
  public int a = 1;
}
@EqualsAndHashCode(callSuper = true) // whether callSuper is true actually doesn't really matter
class Bar extends Foo {
  public int b = 2;
}

Foo a = new Foo(), b = new Bar();
a.equals(b); // true
b.equals(a); // false
```

Without `canEqual`, `a.equals(b)` will call the `Foo.equals` method, which only checks the field `a`. OK, it returns true, because both have `a = 1`.
What about `b.equals(a)`? `b` will check if `a instanceof Bar`, which is false, so it will return false.

`a.equals(b)` returns true and `b.equals(a)` returns false. This is wrong, because `equals` must be symmetric.

`other.canEqual(this)` returns true when `this` is an instance of the class of `other`. In addition to the line you pointed out, that `other` must be an instance of the class of `this`, it basically means, `other` and `this` must be the same class.

