I figured out two ways to inject the calls into explicit written constructors:

Before:
```java
Class() {
    if (a > b) return;
    if (b > a) throw new Exception();
    b = 10;
}
```
Wrap every return statement into a block and just add the calls there:
```java
Class() {
    if (a > b) {
        postConstructor();
        return;
    }
    if (b > a) throw new Exception();
    b = 10;
    postConstructor();
}
```
Wrap the code into a `try ... catch .. finally` block and call `postConstructor()` in the `finally` block:
```java
Class() {
    boolean $callPostConstructor = true;
    try {
        if (a > b) return;
        if (b > a) throw new Exception();
        b = 10;
    } catch (Throwable t) {
        $callPostConstructor = false;
        throw t;
    } finally {
        if ($callPostConstructor) {
            postConstructor();
        }
    }
}
```
Both should work in all cases, I prefer the first one because it is shorter as long as there are only a few different exit pathes and it looks like something that could also be written by hand. 

Another way to enable modifications of explicit written constructors is a new annotation `@PostConstructor.Include`. That one gets added to the constructor. This would increase flexibility (for the 1% that might need that) and improves readability as it gets obvious that there is some post construction logic somewhere.

> Can you explain in which situation a static method is better than an instance method for this feature?

A minority styleguide opinion is that calling instance methods in constructors is not a good idea (because you can witness `final` fields being uninitialized). Of course, passing a half-baked object to a static method is just as bad. So, I guess, I can't? "To satisfy dumb linter rulers", but that's not a very good reason. I'm okay with just not allowing this in name of simplicity.

I think the `this()` thing is trivial: You can't have an endless loop of constructors (that call some `this(...)` in a circle). Or, well, you can, but, that class is not going to ever get to any post constructors so the point is moot. Hence, they will always eventually end up at an actual one. That actual one is either one generated by lombok and therefore post-constructs, or it is handwritten in which case the postconstructor feature doesn't work / you have explicitly flagged it with: Yeah yeah, I know, I'm on the hook for post-constructing here.

It belies a more general problem here (if constructor A calls constructor B, B calls C, and C is the first one in the line that actually calls `super()` and gets to work, vs. A and B, which called some `this(...)`, then post construction occurs immediately after C's statements and before B and A's. But that's not really avoidable. I can foresee a lang feature that would delay post constructors all the way until A is done.

i.e:

```java
class Test {
  Test() {
    this(true);
    System.out.println("C1");
  }
  Test(boolean a) {
    this(true, true);
    System.out.println("C2");
  }
  @PostConstructor.SkipPostConstructors
  Test(boolean a, boolean b) {
    super();
    System.out.println("C3");
  }
  @PostConstructor void test() {
    System.out.println("CP");
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {new Test(); }
}
```

Optimally the above would result in: `C3, C2, C1, CP`, but that's difficult to deliver. We can fairly easily make that work without lombok errors or warnings and print: `C3, CP, C2, C1`. Considering the fact that 'constructor shenanigans' is already a snakepit, I'm okay with tossing another snake down there.

We can scan each constructor for: Is the very first statement a `this()` call? Yes? Then nevermind - that constructor is utterly irrelevant for post construction, and can be completely skipped. It is not modified and there are no requirements that it is marked as `@SkipPostConstructors` or what not. That C3 constructor is the only one that 'counts', given that it is the only one that does _not_ start with a `this()` call.

> `@PostConstructor.InvokePostConstructors` works already

