In my eclipse project I'd like to use annotation based null-checks provided by the eclipse compiler (https://wiki.eclipse.org/JDT_Core/Null_Analysis) in conjunction with some of the lombok annotations. However, it turns out that it is virtually impossible to turn the feature to detect unchecked conversion on (`org.eclipse.jdt.core.compiler.problem.nullUncheckedConversion=error`), which from my perspective is the real bug-finder. See the following code example. The `@Nonnull` annotation is from `javax.annotation`, while the `@NonNull` annotation is from `lombok`

```
@ToString(doNotUseGetters=true)
@EqualsAndHashCode(doNotUseGetters=true)
class MyClass {
  @Setter(onParam = @__({ @Nonnull }))
  @Getter(onMethod = @__({ @Nonnull }))
  private String someString = "";
}

```

The intention is, that `MyClass` can never have a null-object for someString. However, the above code will result in an error, that the getter method needs unchecked conversion: 

> Null type safety: The expression of type String needs unchecked conversion to conform to Nonnull String

A fix for the code would be to add the annotation Nonnull to the parameter itself like that:

```
@ToString(doNotUseGetters=true)
@EqualsAndHashCode(doNotUseGetters=true)
class MyClass {
  @Setter(onParam = @__({ @Nonnull }))
  @Getter(onMethod = @__({ @Nonnull }))
  @Nonnull
  private String someString = "";
}
```

However, this will result in even more errors, because now lombok generates code (null-checks + throws), where it should not generate code. The compiler checks will make sure, that no one ever calls the setter with a null-value.

From what I can guess, the simplest approach would be to have a configuration switch to make lombok only generate null-checks + throws for its own annotations (`@lombok.NonNull`) and not for all other nonnull annotations as well.

JavaFX expands upon the JavaBean concept and uses properties for bindings (see http://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/javafx/properties-binding-tutorial/binding.htm).

When defining a property, you are instructed to write the following code:

```
// Define a variable to store the property
private DoubleProperty amountDue = new SimpleDoubleProperty();

// Define a getter for the property's value
public final double getAmountDue(){return amountDue.get();}

// Define a setter for the property's value
public final void setAmountDue(double value){amountDue.set(value);}

// Define a getter for the property itself
public DoubleProperty amountDueProperty() {return amountDue;}
```

The current @Getter and @Setter are not compatible, since

```
@Getter @Setter private DoubleProperty amountDue = new SimpleDoubleProperty();
```

results in getting and setting a DoubleProperty and not the wrapped double. The getter for the property itself should also be renamed from getXXX to XXXProperty.

Can Lombok add support for this? Ideally you would have an annotation for those 3 methods for a single field, with an option to disable the wrapped value get and set and leave only the XXXProperty since getting and setting the amount is for backwards compatibility.

Example:

`@Property DoubleProperty amountDue = new SimpleDoubleProperty();`

would generate the first code block posted.

`@Property(valueMethods=false) DoubleProperty amountDue = new SimpleDoubleProperty();`

would generate only the getter for the property itself.

Addendum:

The getter and setter methods are `final` by convention. An option `finalMethods=false` could be useful if someone wants to avoid the convention.

