@rspilker and I talked about it; feature request denied. 

Arrays are like low-level assembler. The whole point of the varargs concept is to make nice API, and arrays fundamentally clash with the notion of nice API. We'd be writing a cheque our code couldn't cash – creating the impression this is supposed to be nice API without actually having it. Sending mixed messages.

The proper way to do this is with `@Builder`.

I like it, but when we did some thinking on this, we arrived at a method call instead of an annotation. Something like:

    String version = nullCarry(computer.getSoundcard().getUSB().getVersion());

or even better:

    String version = onNull(computer.getSoundcard().getUSB().getVersion(), "1.0.0");

which produces 1.0.0 if any part of the expression is null (including the final call!). onNull is a static utility method in Lombok, and lombok magically fixes things when you call it. To include it, you'd add import static lombok.Lombok.onNull;

This lets you:

* Offer a default other than `null` which is the proper way to deal with null. See things like Map's `getOrDefault` and my article on [how to deal with null in java properly](https://www.voxxed.com/blog/2015/01/embracing-void-6-refined-tricks-dealing-nulls-java/).

* Use it anywhere, in any expression, without forcibly having to assign it to a variable just to have a place to dump the annotation.

* This does cause some pain if used in an expression in a super() call (because in java you can't put statements before the super call, and oracle in its infinite wisdom borked the ability to invoke stuff prior to the bytecode equivalent of super() in the verifier, in a minor(!) version update of the jvm); even with some classfile hackery I'm not sure we should consider that stable, so let's just instead go with: if you're inside a super() call, it's just not legal to use onNull.

We'd be _very_ interested in this feature.

Sometime ago I was looking for nice approach to handle all possible `null-safe` expressions, in old java i sorted out it with: util class which was generating proxy by reflection api which pretend to be an real object and return null when any call on real object returns null. It looked like this:
```
String name = NullUtil.safe((SomeObject)null).getNestedField1().getNestedField2().getName();
```

But since 1.8 there is more simple way to implement this by using `Function`:
```
String name = NullUtil.safe((SomeObject)null, so -> so.getNestedField1().getNestedField2().getName());
```

Where:
```
public class NullUtil
{
    public static <I, O> O safe(I input, Function<I, O> mapper)
    {
        return safe(input, mapper, null);
    }

    public static <I, O> O safe(I input, Function<I, O> mapper, O defaultValue)
    {
        try
        {
            return Optional.ofNullable(input).map(mapper::apply).orElse(defaultValue);
        }
        catch (NullPointerException e)
        {
            return defaultValue;
        }
    }
}
```

Both are quite simple to implement but both need special care for primitives (cause casting null return value to primitive variable results in `NPE`). So this `NullUtil` class to be re-used widely should define pair of methods for primitive types as well. At least for these ones: `ToIntFunction`, `ToDoubleFunction`, `ToLongFunction`, `Predicate` (instead of `ToBooleanFunction`). In best case for all primitive types.

AFAIK so far this is the simplest way to void NPE's in your code (disregarding you achieve it by hand or by generation). Solution with proxy-class also needs same special logic for primitives.

