    }
```

Get it out there, see some real-world use patterns, add more parameters later if necessary? It's much easier to add params than to take them away.

I'm gonna veto it, though @rspilker - if you want to talk me into undoing my veto, or someone wants to make one heck of a case, I'll watch this issue for a few weeks just in case.

Here's why, and it's a bit of a logical waltz.

* This feature becomes quite complicated unless we always wrap into `RuntimeException`. So we can do that and just steel ourselves to shoot down the cavalcade of requests to make it configurable that will doubtlessly appear, but, as @cchudant's comment on 16 feb 2017, 'throwing RuntimeException is bad'. I think that's bogus, but I hear that all the time. Which means if we force you into doing this (or even simply default to that behaviour), we'll get one issue every week with vehement complaints. You're asking me to deal with that for the next 10 years and I don't wanna, so, 'just wrap into RuntimeException' is flat out vetoed. __It is not about _you_ - it doesn't matter that such a feature would cover all your needs__. It's not even about covering the needs of a significant crowd. It's about engendering confusion in a sufficiently large group (which I think is nasty all by itself, and is also really bad for our issue tracker).
* A way to salvage this is to re-imagine the point of this feature specifically as fixing spring. So, make it `@lombok.extern.spring.FixIdioticCheckedExceptionTransactionRules`. I think it's hard to name this feature, let alone name it in a way that won't get the spring apology tour brigading our issue tracker, and to be fair, before I start casting aspersions at the apparently dubious choices spring made here, I haven't used either spring or ORMs enough to feel certain that I know what I'm talking about. So before Project Lombok starts insinuating that spring has bad designs that need lombok annotations to fix em, I'd.. like to, I guess, ask spring for permission first, or some such. Very complicated. I don't think I want to do that. Hence, this strategy also ends in a veto.
* That leaves actually letting you (nay, forcing you) to _say_ which exception type is the wrapper.

But, no, this is too complex. Here are 4 issues nobody seems to have mentioned yet:

1. There is no guarantee that the `MyException(Throwable cause)` constructor exists. We can instead do:

```java
WrapperException $e = new WrapperException();
$e.initCause(ex);
throw $e;
```

But, there is also no guarantee the args-less constructor exists. I think 'no args constructor' is a lot easier to explain in the docs ('your exception wrapper type must have a no-args constructor'), and custom exceptions with no no-args constructor are in my experience far more rare than ones that have the solo 'cause' constructor.

2. Presumably one might want a message, but because part of the point is to fire-and-forget, the message would be something one wants to configure in `lombok.config`. Can do, but, adds complexity.

As covered, this feature is vetoed unless you specify the wrap type. But, we can't resolve, so we have no idea if the wrapped type is checked or unchecked, and I veto on basis of surprising complexity if we try to make a rule that lombok cannot detect if you try to wrap in a checked ex, but lombok won't work properly if you do that. Thus, it has to work if you choose to wrap in a checked exception type.

This gets us to the next set of I think _showstopping_ problems in delivering this feature:

3. Should we `throws` it? Yes is bad. No is bad.

Lombok is a boilerplate buster library. It would be nuts if rethrowing as checked requires you to write:

```
@Rethrows(from = SQLException.class, to = MyCheckedEx.class)
public void foo() throws MyCheckedEx {}
```

That `throws` clause is idiotic, no? So we should just generate it: Lombok will add it for you. Except that raises a new issue: That also means any javadoc generated for this _will_ mention that exception. An API design may not want that. Linter tools will complain (that's because the linter is dumb, but if delivering this feature involves asking me to go cajole a bevy of linting tool maintainers that their ideas about clean code are wrong, I assume you see why this feature is not going to happen then, right?) – I can handwave away linter tool complaints (hey, we stuck `@Generated` on it, or at least you can ask lombok to do that, it's on the linter tool to figure it out at that point), but it's not helping.

4. True veto territory now: Clashing needs to not needless wrap, and satisfy javac.

We want to not needlessly wrap exceptions. That means if you do this:

```java
@Rethrows(from=GeneralSecurityException.class, to = MyCheckedEx.class) throws InvalidKeySizeException {}
```

then I think that needs to act as: If this method throws `IKSEx` (which is a subtype of `GSEx`!), then that's just thrown, not wrapped. Any other GSEx is wrapped, though.

We can deliver on that premise rather easily - just:

```java
catch (RuntimeException | InvalidKeySizeException | AnyOtherExceptionMentionedInThrowsClause e) {
}
```

Except this runs into trouble. You can legally write throws clauses that cannot be legally turned into catch clauses:

```java
void foo() throws RuntimeException, NumberFormatException { // legal
try {
 ..
} catch (RuntimeException | NumberFormatException e) {} // nope
}
```

That catch block gets you: `Example.java:10: error: Alternatives in a multi-catch statement cannot be related by subclassing`

Without resolution we can't solve that, so yet another knot in the noose: We need to spec that you can't write your `catch` clauses like that, and if you fail to adhere to this, that you get a confusing error from your compiler, yich.

__But it gets worse!__

What if you actually _throw_ your (checked) wrapping exception from within the block? We'd.. rewrap it. In itself. That's silly. Imagine this:

```java
@WrappedThrows(from=Exception.class, to=MyCheckedEx.class)
public void foo() {
  if (cond) throw new IOException();
  if (foobar) throw new MyCheckedEx();
}

// becomes:

public void foo() throws MyCheckedEx {
  try {
    if (cond) throw new IOException();
    if (foobar) throw new MyCheckedEx();
  } catch (RuntimeException e) {
    throw e;
  } catch (Exception e) {
    MyCheckedEx $w = new MyCheckedEx();
    $w.initCause(e);
    throw $w;
  }
}
```

Which therefore wraps `MyCheckedEx` in... `MyCheckedEx`, which is bad. The user could attempt to fix this by writing:

```java
@WrappedThrows(from=Exception.class, to=MyCheckedEx.class)
public void foo() throws MyCheckedEx {
  if (cond) throw new IOException();
  if (foobar) throw new MyCheckedEx();
}
```

and now we've reached the veto point, because that means that one of these three points __has__ to be true, and they are all veto-level bad:

* If you manually write `throws MyWrapperEx` on the signature, just because you think that was perhaps required, and that wrapper is checked, well, we try to catch it, and javac won't let you as your body (usually) doesn't throw it. You get a compiler error on the wrong line with the wrong message and are hopelessly confused. Feature vetoed.
* We work around that by _not_ catching the wrapper type, __even if__ you explicitly added it to your `throws` clause. Except now that means the above workaround is no longer available and we'll rewrap the wrapping exception in itself. Vetoed.
* We give you a cavalcade of settings to work around all this; `@WrappedThrows` ends up with like 5 esoteric parameters that immediately come across as over-engineered crazy unless the user reads this dostoyevski novel of an issue. Vetoed.
* We detect that you are attempting to `throws` your own wrapper and just error out, telling you to not do that, avoiding the issue. Except this means we still wrap your own exception in itself. Best option so far, but compounded with all the other issues, I still say veto.

Sorry folks. I get it, I do. I recently wrote in another issue about sneaky throws for lambdas that lombok ought to have a rethrows feature. I want it too.

But we can't deliver it in a way that I can live with.

I see one way to snatch victory from the jaws of defeat on this: Ditch the plan of using multiple catch blocks and write it all with `instanceof` checks. But, I bet this will run into unsolvable problems too. I think right now only lombok maintainers (@Rawi01 and I) appear to be truly thinking through the complexities, and everybody else is just talking about what would serve their specific needs without thinking of the bigger picture. Which is fine of course, but I'm spent about all the time I was planning to spend on attempting to suss out a way to make this work, but I don't get the feeling anybody else sees the bears on the road I'm seeing.

But, maybe I misjudged the situation and someone is willing to try to work through alllll these concerns and have another go at it with `instanceof` based code. Cover all the exotic corner cases, find a template strategy that doesn't require resolution, apply what lombok would be doing to all the corner cases, run it through javac, check that javac accepts it. Then propose it, and maybe this feature has a future.

Parked - awaiting genius proposals or objective errors in the above reasoning. Can be closed 2022-03-14.

