@rzwitserloot sounds like a good plan for a first approximation.

Two things:

1. I'd rather have support a to-constructor with a single cause, and `initCause` only as a fall-back when a single-arg-constructor cannot be found, as e.g.
```java
@WrappedThrows(from = IOException.class, to = UncheckedIOException.class)
public void foo() {
```
wouldn't work otherwise. But that's just a minor detail and neglible if too much effort.


2. I like the "copy the message from the cause" approach (despite the duplication) but wonder how you'd like to do it (without reflection), as there's no public `Throwable.setMessage()` method.

I still think you're all overthinking this. I'll make one last plea.

There are two discrete camps in the Java world, "use checked exceptions" and "don't use checked exceptions". @rzwitserloot, you are obviously in the former camp, yet you added `@SneakyThrows` to lombok so I'm a bit puzzled about your motivations. Yes, Java has checked exceptions, but there is a large and growing body of people who don't use them. You must have some intuition about this or you wouldn't have added `@SneakyThrows` in the first place.

`@SneakyThrows` targets the "don't use checked exceptions" crowd. It has a couple quirks that make it imperfect, so this camp ends up doing a lot of this anyway:

```java
try {
    blahblah();
} catch (RuntimeException e) {
    throw e;
} catch (Exception e) {
    throw new RuntimeExcepiton(e);
}
```

Since 95% of the users of `@WrappedThrows` will be the "don't use checked exceptions" camp, why not give them what they want?

Nope. If `MyWrapperEx` and `Exception1` are type siblings (neither is a subtype of the other), that does not compile.

> What kind of study would suffice your needs?

Once (there is no point until we've decided on where we want to go with this) we have a proposed syntax, for every major linter tool: How it deals with it, and what settings you need to turn on to make it complain about what we generate. If there is a way to get it there - an alternate style that doesn't cause any linter tool to complain about it, or at least - no complaints unless you turn on rarely used settings.

