**Additional information**: the class is also annotated with `@UtilityClass`. It seems to work as expected under 1.16.14.

This could be related to #1254. Apparently `@UtilityClass` seems to behave differently than explicitly marking a field as `static`. An example (under 1.16.14), where I am seeing this discrepancy to the above example:

```
@FieldDefaults(makeFinal = true, level = AccessLevel.PRIVATE)
public final class Beep implements Serializable {
	static long serialVersionUID = -3930941707841545900L;
	...
}
```

In previous versions of Lombok, the `serialVersionUID` was marked as `final` and `private`. Now, the `static` modifier prevents this. This is very inconvenient! Also, it's inconsistent, because when annotating the class with `@UtilityClass`, the field is correctly set to be `final` and `private`.

I'm also experiencing weird `@UtilityClass` behavior I think is related to this:

```java
// Variant A: @UtilityClass alone
@UtilityClass
class Constants {
  public final int ZERO = 0;
  public final int ONE = 1;
}
```

```java
// Variant B: @UtilityClass + @FieldDefaults
@FieldDefaults(makeFinal = true, level = AccessLevel.PUBLIC)
@UtilityClass
class Constants {
  int ZERO = 0;
  int ONE = 1;
}
```

```java
// Client code:
switch (value) {
  case Constant.ZERO: // works for A, errors for B ("not a constant")
  …
}
```

@rzwitserloot mentioned in https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/issues/1287#issuecomment-279550745 that non-static non-final fields should not be converted into static final fields using `@UtilityClass` and `@FieldDefaults(makeFinal = true)`. 

Right now `@UtilityClass` runs first and convert the fields to static fields. `@FieldDefaults` runs after it and only handle instance fields. Thats why the field is only static and not final and public.

It might be possible to fix this bug by switching the order of the handlers, at least all tests pass and this example works too.

