The name for feature annotation is totally under construction - I'll use `@Fluent` for example's case.

# Use case
 
Quite often if happens that you're providing API methods that should be easy to use, without too much concern on abstraction.

> Obviously, you should structurize your codebase so that logic should be separated and have thought-through constructors, accessors, etc.
> I'm talking about classes that are supposed to be used by other programmers, making it easy
> to use your code.

Good example may be:

```
@Value
public class FileSink implements Sink {
    File file; //point is - FileSink has single FileSink(File) constructor
    ...
} 

public class SinkFactory {
    public Sink fileSink(File file){...} 
    //this is an interesing method
    public Sink fileSink(String path){ return fileSink(new File(path)); } 
}
```

Most interesting here is `fileSink(String)` method - it's only an utility that makes it easier for end user to create `fileSink` without bothering to create `File` manually. I can imagine methods like `fileSink(File parent, String child)` and `fileSink(URI)` - both matching `File` constructors.

One could argue that this is a really specific `File`-related case, but imagine more business-enterprise-another_keyword case:

```
@Entity
@Data
public class User {
    long id;
    //point is there is a User(String username) constructor
    @NonNull String username;
    float balance;
    ...
}

public class UserRepository ... {
    public User register(User entityToPersists){...}

    public User register(String username){ return register(new User(username)); } 
}
```

Most basic idea would be an annotation `@Fluent` which could be used on a single-arg method and generate overloads of that method, delegating to it, but having argument lists taken from that single args type constructors.

## Simple lombok vs vanilla example

### lombok

```
@Value
public class FileSink implements Sink {
    File file; //point is - FileSink has single FileSink(File) constructor
    ...
} 

public class SinkFactory {
    @Fluent
    public Sink fileSink(File file){...}
}
```

### Vanilla Java

```
@Value
public class FileSink implements Sink {
    File file; //point is - FileSink has single FileSink(File) constructor
    ...
} 

// @Generated and similiar annotations ommited, to specifically show the idea itself
public class SinkFactory {
    public Sink fileSink(File file){...}
    public Sink fileSink(String pathname){ return fileSink(new File(pathname)); }
    public Sink fileSink(File parent, String child){ return fileSink(new File(parent, child)); }
    public Sink fileSink(String parent, String child){ return fileSink(new File(parent, child)); }
    public Sink fileSink(URI uri){ return fileSink(new File(uri)); }
}
```

## Extensions and edge cases

### Conflicts with by-hand written implementations

First of all - written-by hand overloads should take priority (this should be configurable with lombok.config, I guess). That means that:

```
@Value
public class FileSink implements Sink {
    File file; //point is - FileSink has single FileSink(File) constructor
    ...
} 

public class SinkFactory {
    @Fluent
    public Sink fileSink(File file){...}
    //for examples sake, it doesn't make much sense
    public Sink fileSink(String root, String path){ return fileSink(root, path+"/results.txt"); }
}
```

effects in following vanilla Java (some methods omitted for brevity):

```
(...)
public class SinkFactory {
    public Sink fileSink(File file){...}
    (...)
    public Sink fileSink(String root, String path){ return fileSink(root, path+"/results.txt"); }
}
```

### Conflicts between `@Fluent` overloads

If 2 overloads of the same methods would generate clashing fluent implementations, it should result in compilation errors. 

I can't think of a good annotation-driven interface to control which should take precedence or how to mark defaults.

Following example is a bad code smell, but it states the idea nontheless.

```
public class SinkFactory {
    @Fluent
    public Sink sink(File file){...}

    // compilation error - File and URI both has constructor this(String), so we can't choose
    // what should sink(String) do
    @Fluent 
    public Sink sink(URI uri){...}
}
```

### Obviously, class-level annotation

The same as with `@Getter` and family, we should be able to annotate the whole class and it should be equivalent to annotating any method of that class that is compatible with `@Fluent`. This means that methods with more than one arg should be ignored and methods that would be conflicting (see "Conflicts between `@Fluent` overloads") should be ignored too (though in that case, I think warning is adequate).

It would be good to provide `@Fluent.Ignore` annotation too, to provide possibility to turn off utility implementations.

```
@Value
public class A {
    String a;
}

@Value
public class B {
    String b;
}

@Fluent
public class API {
    public foo(A a){...}
    public bar(A a){...}
    public bar(B b){...}
    @Fluent.Ignore
    public baz(A a){...}
}
```

effects in vanilla Java:

```
(...)
public class API {
    public foo(A a){...}
    public foo(String a){return foo(new A(a));}
    public bar(A a){...}
    public bar(B b){...}
    // warning generated, because bar(String) would be ambiguous
    public baz(A a){...}
    // nothing generated for baz, due to @Fluent.Ignore
}
```


### Static methods

Static methods should be supported out-of-the-box and be treated the same way. In my opinion, class-level annotation should also cover static methods, though this needs discussion.

### Custom factories

This is an idea for another feature issue, that makes sense once this feature is implemented.

Constructors are just simplest, language-supported ways of obtaining new instances of classes. We can obtain them in other ways too - through factory classes and methods.

The idea is that the `@Fluent` annotation should be customizable to tell in details where to look for translation methods. 

A translation methods is just a callable that takes some arguments and translates them to another type. For example `File:new(String)` is `String -> File` translation methods and `File:new(File, String)` is `(File, String) -> File` translation method.

The general idea of feature describes in this issue is that when we're annotating a method `foo(T)` , we're browsing for translation methods `X -> T` and generating methods `foo(X)` for each translation method. 

Constructors aren't always best way of translating one type to another, besides they are hardly customizable and may make it harder to resolve conflicts. Instead we may define a number of ways to look for translation methods:

- argument type constructors (default way, as it was shown in previous examples),
- static methods of some class (
- instance methods of result of some method of current class.

First possibility was already covered. `@Fluent(translationClasses = {Clazz.class})` would mean "browse Clazz for static methods, if you find static single-arg methods that are translation methods, use them".
This would require us to decide what to do in case of conflicts.

Another possible extension here is registering translation classes only for some target types - for example something like `@Fluent @Fluent.FactoryBinding(target=File.class, factories={Clazz.class})` could mean "browse `Clazz` only for method that translate anything to `File`". This can be useful then annotating the whole class, to customize how should different types be handled.

Last case is more interesting 

Idea is mark some methods in the same scope as method that is exploded with `@Fluent.Factory` (possibly  with argument marking what is the result of translation, e.g. `@Fluent.Factory(File.class)` should only mark factories used to translate to `File` type). Those methods should return a type that has instance methods that can be used as translation methods. This way translation can be stateful too.

In the end, API should look like:

```
// Animal is an interface, Dog and Cat implement it, Dog has String name and Color color, Cat has String name, bool isSneakyBastard and Color color.

public class AnimalFactory {
    Color defaultColor; // matching constructor

    public Animal doggo(String name){
         return new Dog(name, defaultColor);
    }

    public Animal kitty(String name, bool isSneakyBastard){
         return new Cat(name, isSneakyBastard, defaultColor);
    }
}

public class FileFactory {
    static File ensuredFile(String path){
        File out = new File(path);
        out.getParentFile().mkdirs();
        out.createNewFile();
        return out;
    }
}

@Fluent
@Fluent.FactoryBinding(target = File.class, factories = { FileFactory.class })
public class MyAPI {
    public void foo(File file){...}
    public String bar(Animal animal){...}

    @Fluent.Factory
    public AnimalFactory animalFactory(){
        return new AnimalFactory(Color.GREEN); // welcome to Prypiet
    }
}
```

which turns MyAPI to vanilla Java:

```
(...)

public class MyAPI {
    public void foo(File file){...}
    public void foo(String path){ return foo(FileFactory.ensuredFile(path)); }
    public String bar(Animal animal){...}
    public String bar(String name){ return bar(animalFactory().doggo(name)); }
    public String bar(String name, bool isSneakyBastard){ return bar(animalFactory().kitty(name, isSneakyBastard)); }

    public AnimalFactory animalFactory(){
        return new AnimalFactory(Color.GREEN); // welcome to Prypiet
    }
}
```

Possible change may be memoization of result of `animalFactory()`, so that it's built once only and then used for translation.

Obviously, there are some questions here - factory memoization, conflicts, requirements on Factory classes (e.g. `animalFactory()` may return subclass of `AnimalFactory`, which in turn can be interface or may have less methods than result of the method - in the end translation methods would be only looked up in `AnimalFactory`, not its subclasses). 

### Invocation builders

This is another idea for distinct issue, just stating it here for further thinkings.

We could provide `@Fluent.InvocationBuilder` annotation for methods that take single argument which in turn is annotated with `@Builder`. It will be the best to show it on an example and proposed de-lomboking and to figure out minor details in another issue:

Given lombok code (access modifiers ommited for brevity):

```
@Builder
public class Credentials {
    String username;
    String password;
    String domain;
} 

public class SessionService {
    @Fluent.InvocationBuilder(invokeMethod = "perform")
    public Session login(Credentials credentials){...}
}
```

we could use it as:

```
SessionService service = ...
service.login().
        username("elvis.presley").
        password("takingCareOfBusiness").
        domain("in.the.ghetto").
        perform();
```

and which would be implemented as:

```
@Builder
public class Credentials {
    String username;
    String password;
    String domain;
} 

public class SessionService {
    @Fluent.InvocationBuilder(invokeMethod = "perform")
    public Session login(Credentials credentials){...}

    public SessionService$login$InvocationBuilder login(){ return new SessionService$login$InvocationBuilder(); }

    class SessionService$login$InvocationBuilder {
        Credentials.CredentialsBuilder credentialsBuilder = Credentials.builder();
        
        SessionService$login$InvocationBuilder username(String username){
            credentialsBuilder.username(username);
            return this;     
        }
        //other builder delegates
        
        // name of this method is customized in annotation argument
        Session perform(){ return login(credentialsBuilder.build()); }
    }
}
```

There is an issue with what would happen when we'd declare out own arg-less login() method, but I guess this can be solved with adding `builderMethodSuffix` annotation attribute that would default to empty string, and when e.g. used as `builderMethodSuffix = "Builder"` would allow for `session.loginBuilder().username(...)...`.

The Build.Default annotation enables default values when using builder, but those default values are ignored with using the NoArgConstructor.

    @Builder
    @NoArgsConstructor
    @AllArgsConstructor
    public static class Model {
        @Builder.Default
        private String value1 = "testA";
        private String value2 = "testB";
    }

    @Test
    public void test() {
        final Model model1 = new Model();
        assertNull(model1.value1); // FIXME should not be null
        assertEquals(model1.value2, "testB"); // Correct
        final Model model2 = Model.builder().build();
        assertEquals(model2.value1, "testA"); // Correct
        assertNull(model2.value2); // Correct
    }

