Here's a setup:
```
public interface Work {

	public void doA();
	public void doB();
	public void doC();
}

public class WorkAdapter implements Work {

	public void doA() {
		System.out.println("adapter: doA");
	}

	public void doB() {
		System.out.println("adapter: doB");
	}

	public void doC() {
		System.out.println("adapter: doC");
	}
}

public class WorkWrapper implements Work {

	protected static interface Handled {

		public void doA();
	}

	@Delegate(excludes = Handled.class)
	private final Work delegate = new WorkAdapter();

	@Override
	public void doA() {
		System.out.println("wrapper: doA");
	}
}

public class AdvancedWorkWrapper extends WorkWrapper implements Work {

	protected static interface Handled {

		public void doB();
	}

	@Delegate(excludes = Handled.class)
	private final Work delegate = new WorkAdapter();

	@Override
	public void doB() {
		System.out.println("advanced wrapper: doB");
	}
}

AdvancedWorkWrapper aww = new AdvancedWorkWrapper();
aww.doA();
aww.doB();
aww.doC();
```

Now, `aww.doC()` is delegated, so it will print: `adapter: doC`.  

`AdvanceWorkWrapper`, obviously, handles `doB()` by itself.  
So, `aww.doB()` will print `advanced wrapper: doB`.  

But, what about `doA()`?!  
It appears, that in the situation above, `doA()` doesn't survive the inheritance, and gets overridden by AdvancedWorkWrapper's `@Delegate`.  
So, `aww.doA()` will print `adapter: doA` (and NOT `wrapper: doA`, as expected!)

---
Here's a rather simple workaround:
```
public class AdvancedWorkWrapper extends WorkWrapper implements Work {

	protected static interface Handled extends WorkWrapper.Handled {

		public void doB();
	}

	@Delegate(excludes = Handled.class)
	private final Work delegate = new WorkAdapter();

	@Override
	public void doB() {
		System.out.println("wrapper: doB");
	}
}
```  
(Notice the additional `extends WorkWrapper.Handled`.)  
This time `aww.doA()` will print `wrapper: doA`, as expected.

Despite the workaround, I believe this `@Delegate` behaviour should be fixed.

