Consider this code:

```java
class X {
    public X(String a) {
        System.out.println(a);
    }
}
```

```java
class Y extends X {
    public Y(@NonNull String a) { // Case 1
        super(a.trim());
    }

    public Y(@NonNull String a, int b) { // Case 2
        this(a.trim() + b);
    }

    public Y(@NonNull StringBuilder c) { // Case 3
        this("x");
    }
}
```

Lombok will add a null check after the call to the base constructor. However, at this point, a `NullPointerException` would already be generated. This might not be very problematic since the null checks added by lombok would also throw a `NullPointerException`. However, the `NullPointerException` thrown in this case has no message and it also makes FindBugs complain.

Also, this means that the invoked `this` or `super` constructors that do not use some of the `@NonNull` parameters still does run before the constructor parameters are validated. This makes sense, but it is undesired and surprising.

A simple solution would be lombok generating a `java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(T, String)` on the first occurrence of each `@NonNull`-annotated parameter in the constructor if it happens inside a `super` or `this` constructor call. If the parameter is not used at all in those calls, then emit a test for the parameter after the call. Although this don't fix the problem completely (base constructors still runs before unrelated parameters are checked), it fixes the problem for parameters directly used at base constructor calls and should also make FindBugs happy.

A complete solution (let's call the class `K`) which do not mangles with callers of the constructor, do not breaks reflection (for sane uses of reflection at least), do not rely on bytecode manipulation and works even if the base constructor call is parameterless would be to:

- Add an uninstantiable static inner class (lets call it `N`) inside class `K`.
- Duplicate each constructor having some `@NonNull`-annotated parameter of `K`. Each duplicate would be a private constructor with the same parameters of the original constructor prepended by a `N`-typed parameter.
- Move the code of each of the original constructors to the corresponding newly produced ones.
- Replace the body of the original constructors by `this(...)` constructor calls with the same signature, but passing `null` as the first `N`-typed parameter. The remaining parameters are passed in the same order as received, but those annotated with `@NonNull` are wrapped inside a `java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(T, String)` call.

This ensures that the base constructors won't run before the parameters are validated even if the base constructor call happens to be a parameterless `this()` or `super()`.

Also, if passing a bunch of `null`s as the first parameters is for some reason undesirable, an alternative would be to replace the uninstantiable static inner class `N` by a single-element `enum` and pass that element as the parameter.

The code above is a testcase for that. Calling any of the `Y` constructors by passing a `null` parameter on any `@NonNull` annotated parameter should produce a `NullPointerException` with the expected message and without anything being outputted to `System.out`.

nodes.body:
> A simple solution would be lombok generating a java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(T, String) on the first occurrence of each @NonNull-annotated parameter in the constructor if it happens inside a super or this constructor call. If the parameter is not used at all in those calls, then emit a test for the parameter after the call. Although this don't fix the problem completely (base constructors still runs before unrelated parameters are checked), it fixes the problem for parameters directly used at base constructor calls and should also make FindBugs happy.

Not so easy:

    class Base {
        public Base(Object o) {…;}
        …;
    }
    class Descendant extends Base {
        public Descendant(boolean useA, @NonNull Object a, @NonNull Object b) {
            super(useA ? a : b);
            …;
        }
        …;
    }

Just replacing `super(useA ? a : b)` with `super(useA ? Objects.requireNonNull(a) : Objects.requireNonNull(b))` would leave some (actually, both) of the parameters possibly-unchecked, so we'll need to check them again after the `super(…)` call anyway.
&nbsp;

> but passing `null` as the first `N`-typed parameter…
> 
> if passing a bunch of `null`s as the first parameters is for some reason undesirable, an alternative would be to replace the uninstantiable static inner class `N` by a single-element `enum` and pass that element as the parameter

Again, it seems to be not so easy for me:

    class Descendant extends Base {
        public Descendant(@NonNull Object arg) {
            super(arg);
            …;
        }
        public Descendant(Object arg1, Object arg2) {…;}
        …;
    }
    
In such case:
  - Simply calling `this(null, Objects.requireNonNull(arg))` seems to be be ambiguous between our `Descendant(N, Object)` and existing `Descendant(Object, Object)`.
  - Typecasting like `this((N)null, Objects.requireNonNull(arg))` won't help too, nor declaring the `N` as enum and calling `this(N.Value, Objects.requireNonNull(arg))` will. Because `Descendant` may have the following method:

        public static Descendant create() {return new Descendant(null, null);}

    Which will be broken by our new `private Descendant(N, Object) {…;}` declaration.

We can't use `returnFirstAndCheckAllOthers` too, because an original invocation of `super` may look like this:

    super(f1(a, b), f2(a, b));

If we turn it into:

    super(returnFirstAndCheckAllOthers(f1(a, b), a, b), f2(a, b));

— then `f1(a, b)` would be evaluated **before** `a` and `b` would be checked for being not nulls.
&nbsp;

Ways I see:

 1. Pass a closure to the helper-function:

        private static <T> checkAndCall(A a, B b, Supplier<T> supplier) {
            Objects.requireNonNull(a, "a");
            Objects.requireNonNull(b, "b");
            return supplier.get();
        }

        // Then we replace `super(f1(a, b), f2(a, b))` with:
        super(
            checkAndCall(a, b, ()->f1(a, b)),
            f2(a, b)
        );

    The problem with this way is that `a` and `b` now need to be `final` (or effectively final since Java 8) to be allowed for use in the closure's body. Though, we can insert `A copyOfA = a`, `B copyOfB = b` right after the `super(…)` invocation and replace all following usages of `a` and `b` with usages of `copyOfA` and `copyOfB` to guarantee original arguments immutability.

 2. Rely on [function argument evaluation order](//stackoverflow.com/a/2201729):

        private static <T> ignoreAllButLast(A a, B b, T result) {return result;}

        // Then:
        super(
            ignoreAllButLast(
                Objects.requireNonNull(a, "a"),
                Objects.requireNonNull(b, "b"),
                f1(a, b)
            ),
            f2(a, b)
        );

 3. Rely on any other operator with defined evaluation order, e.g.:

        super(
            Objects.requireNonNull(a, "a") != null
                && Objects.requireNonNull(b, "b") != null
                    ? f1(a, b) : f1(a, b),
            f2(a, b)
        );

    The `!= null`s are here actually only to make results of `requireNonNull` usable as operands for `&&`. If no exception occurs, the `?:` always evaluates its first branch. Or a cleaner way:

        private static boolean checkAndSuccess(A a, B b) {
            Objects.requireNonNull(a, "a");
            Objects.requireNonNull(b, "b");
            return true;
        }

        // Then:
        super(
            checkAndSuccess(a, b) ? f1(a, b) : f1(a, b),
            f2(a, b)
        );

&nbsp;
Still I have no idea what to do when nothing is passed to a `super()`. It may look as if no arguments of the current constructor are passed to a super-constructor (or if super-constructor isn't even invoked explicitly), then we don't need to check anything before calling `super()`. But that's not true: super-constructor may appear a dirty (context-dependent) routine. E.g.:

    class Base {
        public static Object context;
        public Base() {
            if (context == null)
                startGreatCatastrophe();
        }
        …;
    }

    class Descendant extends Base {
        public Descendant(@NonNull Object argument) {
            super();
            …;
        }
        …;
    }

    Object value = getSomeObject();
    Base.context = value;
    new Descendant(value); // <-- will start great catastrophe
                           //     if the `value` isn't checked before calling `super()`,
                           //     but won't start if it is.

The only thing that we could do in the case of a parameterless `super()` or `this()` is to replace it with a call to another constructor. So, I think that this is the way to go.

Further, synthetic constructors may introduce problems only with uses within the same class, enclosing classes or inner classes. Those could be fixed by adding proper casts in the same class file. For exemple, the following code compiles because the potentially ambiguous call happens in an unrelated class that can't see the private constructor:

```java
class Foo {
    public Foo() {}

    private Foo(Goo x) {
        System.out.println("x");
    }

    public Foo(Integer x) {
        System.out.println("y");
    }

    private static enum Goo {
        GOO;
    }
}

class Muck {
    public Foo f() {
        return new Foo(null);
    }
}
```

If you move the `f()` method to the `Foo` class, you'll get a compiler error. With all of that in mind, let's consider this:

```java
class X {

    public final String z;

    public X(String a) {
        this.z = a;
    }

    public X() {
        this.z = "nothing";
    }
}

class Y extends X {
    public Y(@NonNull String a) { // Case 1
        super(a.trim());
    }

    public Y(@NonNull String b, int c) { // Case 2
        super(b.trim() + c);
    }

    public Y(@NonNull StringBuilder d) { // Case 3
        this("x");
    }

    public Y(@NonNull Object... stuff) { // Case 4
        super();
    }

    public Y(@NonNull Integer e) { // Case 5
        super("e");
    }

    public Y(@NonNull Integer f, @NonNull Integer g, boolean h) { // Case 6
        super("" + (h ? f : g));
    }

    public Y(@NonNull Long i, @NonNull Long j) { // Case 7
        this(mungle(i, j), 77);
    }

    public Y(char k) { // Case 8
        super("u");
    }

    public Y(Runnable r) { // Case 9
        super("run");
    }

    public static Y create() {
        return new Y(null, null);
    }

    private static String mungle(long x, long y) {
        return x + y + "";
    }

    public String dup(@NonNull String test) { // Keep testing parameters on methods.
        return test + test;
    }
}
```

I think that lombok should transform it on something very similar to this:

```java
class X {

    public final String z;

    public X(String a) {
        this.z = a;
    }

    public X() {
        this.z = "nothing";
    }
}

class Y extends X {
    public Y(String a) { // Case 1
        this($Lombok$ParameterHack.hack(java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(a, "a")), a.trim());
    }

    private Y($Lombok$ParameterHack hack, String a) { // Case 1
        super(a.trim());
    }

    public Y(String b, int c) { // Case 2
        this($Lombok$ParameterHack.hack(java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(b, "b")), b, c);
    }

    private Y($Lombok$ParameterHack hack, String b, int c) { // Case 2
        super(b.trim() + c);
    }

    public Y(StringBuilder d) { // Case 3
        this($Lombok$ParameterHack.hack(java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(d, "d")), d);
    }

    private Y($Lombok$ParameterHack hack, StringBuilder d) { // Case 3
        this("x");
    }

    public Y(Object... stuff) { // Case 4
        this($Lombok$ParameterHack.hack(java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(stuff, "stuff")), stuff);
    }

    private Y($Lombok$ParameterHack hack, Object... stuff) { // Case 4
        super();
    }

    public Y(Integer e) { // Case 5
        this($Lombok$ParameterHack.hack(java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(e, "e")), e);
    }

    private Y($Lombok$ParameterHack hack, Integer e) { // Case 5
        super("e");
    }

    public Y(Integer f, Integer g, boolean h) { // Case 6
        this($Lombok$ParameterHack.hack(java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(f, "f"), java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(g, "g")), f, g, h);
    }

    private Y($Lombok$ParameterHack hack, Integer f, Integer g, boolean h) { // Case 6
        super("" + (h ? f : g));
    }

    public Y(Long i, Long j) { // Case 7
        this($Lombok$ParameterHack.hack(java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(i, "i"), java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(j, "j")), i, j);
    }

    private Y($Lombok$ParameterHack hack, Long i, Long j) { // Case 7
        this(mungle(i, j), 77);
    }

    public Y(char k) { // Case 8
        super("u");
    }

    public Y(Runnable r) { // Case 9
        super("run");
    }

    public static Y create() {
        return new Y((Long) null, (Long) null);
    }

    private static String mungle(long x, long y) {
        return x + y + "";
    }

    public String dup(String test) { // Keep testing parameters on methods.
        if (test == null) throw new java.lang.NullPointerException("test");
        return test + test;
    }

    private static enum $Lombok$ParameterHack {
        HACK;

        static $Lombok$ParameterHack hack(Object... ignored) {
            return $Lombok$ParameterHack.HACK;
        }
    }
}
```

What lombok should do to achieve that:

* Each constructor with at least one `@NonNull` is duplicated (all except 8 and 9 in the example). An inner class parameter typed `$Lombok$ParameterHack` is added as the first parameter of that constructor.

* The private inner enum `$Lombok$ParameterHack` is added with a sole element. It features a single method `hack` that ignore all the parameters (which are things already evaluated with `java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(T, String)` and returns the sole element.

* The body of the original constructors are moved to the correspondingly new constructors.

* We rely on parameter evaluation order.

* The original constructor consists of a call to the new correspondingly constructor passing as the first parameter, the result of the `hack` method called with a bunch of `java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(T, String)` as parameters, one for each `@NonNull`-annotated parameter on the original constructor. The remaining parameters of the overloaded constructor are exactly the same parameters received.

* Places that are able to see the class private constructors (and could get ambiguities due to added new private constructors) should get casts added on null parameters for calls on those. Luckily, those are just the class itself, enclosing classes and inner classes, so they are always inside the same compilation unit.

I think that this should cover all the edge cases. Finally, this test should pass printing "Ok" in the end:

```java
public class TestLombok {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (!"foo".equals(new Y("foo").z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"foo2".equals(new Y("foo", 2).z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"x".equals(new Y(new StringBuilder()).z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"nothing".equals(new Y(4, 5, 6, 7, 8).z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"e".equals(new Y(7).z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"42".equals(new Y(42, 5, true).z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"5".equals(new Y(42, 5, false).z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"4977".equals(new Y(42L, 7L).z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"u".equals(new Y('*').z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"run".equals(new Y(() -> {}).z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"run".equals(new Y((Runnable) null).z)) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        if (!"kkkk".equals(new Y("foo").dup("kk"))) throw new AssertionError("NO!");

        try {
            new Y(null, 2);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"b".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y((String) null);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"a".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y((StringBuilder) null);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"d".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y((Integer) null);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"e".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y((Integer) null, 42, false);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"f".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y(42, (Integer) null, true);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"g".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y((Long) null, 42L);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"i".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y(42L, (Long) null);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"j".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y(null, null); // Look, no ambiguity compile error!
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"i".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y((Object[]) null);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"stuff".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        try {
            new Y("foo").dup(null);
            throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            if (!"test".equals(e.getMessage())) throw new AssertionError("NO!");
        }

        System.out.println("Ok");
    }
}
```

