When in one class in a project a compilation error occurs, lombok doesn't generate methods for ANY other class, resulting in many misleading "cannot find symbol" errors in the whole project.

Only specific compilation errors cause this problem, the ones I discovered are:

1. Method with identical signature defined twice in same class, or variable with identical name defined twice in same class
2. Name of public class doesn't match name of file it is defined in
3. Issue #1434 seems to be related

Sample code for a variable defined twice in class:
```
// Main.java:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        A a = new A();
        a.setA("x");
        System.out.println(a.getA());
    }
}

// A.java:
@Data
public class A {
    String a;
}

// Other.java:
public class Other {
    public int test;
    public int test; // compilation error
}
```

Build result:

```
D:\infa\Java\sandbox\lombok-test\src\main\java\com\gk\Other.java:8: error: variable test is already defined in class Other
    public int test;
               ^
D:\infa\Java\sandbox\lombok-test\src\main\java\com\gk\Main.java:9: error: cannot find symbol
        a.setA("x");
         ^
  symbol:   method setA(String)
  location: variable a of type A
D:\infa\Java\sandbox\lombok-test\src\main\java\com\gk\Main.java:10: error: cannot find symbol
        System.out.println(a.getA());
                            ^
  symbol:   method getA()
  location: variable a of type A
3 errors
```

Class A is affected, even though the compilation error occurred in Other class, unrelated to A. If the double definition is removed, "cannot find symbol" errors disappear.

A similar thing happens when there's a name mismatch between class name and file name:
```
// Other.java:
public class NameMismatch {
    public int test;
}
```

Expected behaviour is to show compilation error for the real problem, but not "cannot find symbol" errors related to methods generated by Lombok. It would be consistent with all compilation errors other than listed above, e.g. assigning a value to a non-existing variable.

This behaviour is confusing since in big projects it's hard to notice the real error and find the reason why methods aren't generated.

