@alvint Lombok in general can't work well with superclasses as it works in a compile stage when the information is not yet available.

It can't see the superclass field. Even it if could, there's no way of initializing it besides calling the super constructor (which you forgot to declare). So far it can't work, even if Lombok were perfect.

And now, another problems comes in: Lombok wouldn't see the super constructor either. This may get fixed one day... or not as it's a tough problem.

@alvint, my understanding is that `@NonNull` modifies the behavior of _other_ helpers in Lombok when they encounter a field or variable annotated as such.  The `@Setter` on the abstract class modifies its behavior to generate a method that throws NPE on receiving null input.  Since the setter is the only way for that field to be mutated from within its enclosing class, that's all Lombok needs to do.

@Maaartinus pointed out that `Parent` is declared without a constructor. That means, whether it happened to be `abstract` or not, it has an initial state where `someProperty == null`.  The `@NonNull` does not modify the default field initialization, nor would it enforce itself on a handwritten constructor.  If you have Lombok generate a constructor for you, using `@RequiredArgsConstructor`, then that helper will honor the annotation and force a null check.  (Aside: I thought to be "required" the fields would also need to be declared `final`?  I may be wrong on this part.) Also as a result, any subclass could no longer depend on a zero argument constructor and would be forced to call `super(Integer)`, and `Child` would fail to compile without it.

From the Child's perspective, which does use `@RequiredArgsConstructor`, at best it could look for _all_ of the parent constructors.  Is it possible to automatically limit to only a subset?  How?  There could be many; does it generate a suitable child constructor for _every_ constructor in Parent?

```
@Getter
@Setter
abstract class Parent2 {
