It has nothing to do with nested and/or abstract classes.  The `@...Constructor` annotations on subclasses don't support parent classes _without_ an accessible no-argument constructor.  The error is the same whether or not the child is nested and whether or not the parent is abstract.

The cause is the `@RequiredArgsConstructor` on `Animal` which gives the class the single, following constructor:

```
public Animal(String name) {
    this.name = name;
}
```

It doesn't make much sense in this example, but if there were a sensible default value for `name` then adding the following constructor to `Animal` would resolve the error.  (But all `Eagle` instances would then share the same default name.):

```
protected Animal() {
    this("default name");
}
```

> It has nothing to do with nested and/or abstract classes. The @...Constructor annotations on subclasses don't support parent classes without an accessible no-argument constructor. 

