@publicocean0 I'm afraid, this is much too hacky. Moreover, the read-write lock has a rather big overhead, so it's inefficient for low contention. At the same time, monitor-enter-exit gets heavily optimized. You're surely right that the context switch is very costly, but you don't know upfront, if it happens often enough. You surely shouldn't replace monitors with rw-locks blindly as the gain/loss depends on usage.

A half-baked analysis of what gets done inside a block is looking for troubles. Lombok can't access other classes, so you must give up on any method call on anything but `this`. I'm afraid, this has to be done manually or by some static analysis tool.

I can imagine annotations like `@ReplaceSynchronizedByReadLock` and `@ReplaceSynchronizedByWriteLock`, but if you'd have to insert it into a legacy class, you can rewrite it instead (it's more verbose, but I'm afraid, you need to think hard about any change).

