`@Builder.Default` works fine in case of class itself annotated as `@Builder` i.e.

```
@Builder
class X {
  @Builder.Default
  private int y = 1;
}
```

but it doesn't taken into consideration once one put `@Builder` on a constructor which might be very useful in case of hierarchy:

```
class X extends AbstractX {
  @Builder.Default
  private int y = 1;
  
  @Builder
  public X(int a, inb b, int y) {
    super(a, b);
    this.y = y;
  } 
}
```
here `y` won't have a default value of 1.
The idea is to have more generic (but maybe a bit ugly) way of specifying the default value via annotation itself: `@Builder.DefaultValue(1)` or `@Builder.DefaultValue(true)` or `@Builder.DefaultValue("string")` This way the constructor would look:

```
@Builder
public X(int a, inb b, @Builder.DefaultValue(1) int y) {
  super(a, b);
  this.y = y;
} 
```

What do you think about it?

