I've been thinking about this since your initial proposal (#1645).  For the record, I tend to prefer writing my interface APIs by hand, immutable or not, and then use Lombok for easing the implementations that are concrete or partially abstract.  What I'm about to add to this proposal, I'm not fully endorsing myself, but I put it out there for the community.

Creating an immutable interface from an implementation is a specific use case, and a good one, but I think this could be made in a way that supports broader use cases _including_ immutable interfaces, assuming it's ever added to Lombok at all.  Consider the following possible annotations:

```
@Target(TYPE)
public @interface ExtractInterface {

    /**
     * Required; name of the interface that will be created.
     * Must be a legal Java Identifier not already in use within the same
     * package as the annotated type; otherwise, an unused yet legal
     * fully-qualified class name (which may be in a different package).
     */
    String name();

    /**
     * Controls the visibility of the extracted interface.
     * Since the annotated type will automatically implement the extracted
     * interface, it is an error to set this {@code false} if the {@link #name}
     * uses a fully-qualified class name outside of the annotated type's package.
     */
    boolean isPublic() default true;    // see note 1

    /**
     * When enabled, the extracted interface will include all {@code public }, non-static
     * method signatures declared directly within the annotated type unless explicitly
     * {@link Exclude excluded}.  This behavior is enabled by default.
     *
     * Note that methods inherited from a superclass or another implemented interface
     * _will not_ be included unless this annotated type locally overrides those methods.
     * Public methods declared by {@code java.lang.Object} will never be automatically
     * included, even when locally overridden; for these methods, use an explicit
     * {@link Include @Include} if desired.
     */
    boolean memberMethods() default true;

    /**
     * When enabled, the extracted interface will include copies of all {@code public static}
     * methods declared in the annotated type unless explicitly {@link Exclude excluded}.
     * This behavior not supported for versions prior to Java 8.
     */
    boolean staticMethods() default false;

    /**
     * When enabled, the extracted interface will include all {@code public static final}
     * fields found in the annotated type unless explicitly {@link Exclude excluded}.
     */
    boolean staticFields() default false;

    /**
     * Prevents a field or method from appearing in the extracted interface, but only
     * when its inclusion is based on the settings of {@link #memberMethods},
     * {@link #staticMethods}, or {@link #staticFields}.  Methods or fields which are
     * explicitly {@link Include included} will ignore this annotation (or generate a
     * compile-time warning/error?).  Since non-public fields and methods are never
     * included by default, placing this annotation on such items is useless and may
     * generate a warning but is always harmless.
     */
    @Target({FIELD, METHOD})
    public @interface Exclude { }

    /**
     * Explicitly includes a field or method in the extracted interface.
     * It is a compile-time error to use this annotation on a field declared anything
     * other than {@code public static final}.  It is a compile-time error to use this
     * annotation on a method declared anything other than {@code public} or,
     * beginning with Java 9, {@code private}.  It is a compile-time error to use this
     * annotation on a {@code static} method prior to Java 8.
     *
     * When the annotated method is either {@code static} (Java 8+) or
     * {@code private} (Java 9+), its body will be copied into the generated
     * interface.  To opt-in to this behavior for non-static, {@code public} methods
     * on Java 8 and later, see {@link Default @Default}.
     */
    @Target({FIELD, METHOD})
    public @interface Include { }

    /**
     * This annotation is valid only on non-static, non-abstract, public methods on
     * Java 8+.  When placed on such a method in a type annotated by
     * {@link ExtractInterface @ExtractInterface}, _and_ that method is also included
     * in the extracted interface, then the original method's body is copied into the
     * generated interface as a {@code default} implementation for its method signature.
     * Placing this annotation on a method _does not_, on its own, cause the method
     * to be included in the extracted interface.  It should be a compile-time warning
     * if this annotation is detected on a method which will not appear in the extracted
     * interface.
     */
    @Target(METHOD)
    public @interface Default { }
}
```

So to borrow the example from @OndraZizka, to extract an immutable interface from an implementation declared using Lombok, I would write:

```
@ExtractInterface(name="Cheese", memberMethods=false) // only explicit inclusions
@Setter
@Getter(onMethod_={@ExtractInterface.Include})   // see note 2
class MutableCheese {
    private String name;
    private String taste;
}
```

Lombok would generate:

```
/** Javadoc copied from MutableCheese class description? */
public interface Cheese {
    /** Javadoc copied from MutableCheese#getName() */
    String getName();
    /** Javadoc copied from MutableCheese#getTaset() */
    String getTaste();
}

class MutableCheese implements Cheese {
    private String name;
    private String taste;

    @Override
    @ExtractInterface.Include
    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
    public void setName(String val) {
        name = val;
    }

    @Override
    @ExtractInterface.Include
    public String getTaste() {
        return taste;
    }
    public void setTaste(String val) {
        taste= val;
    }
}
```

And then I could use in my program like:

```
public static void main(String ... args) {
    Cheese cheese = makeCousteron();
    assert "Cousteron".equals(cheese.getName()); // OK
    cheese.setTaste("icky"); // NoSuchMethod
}

static Cheese makeCousteron() {
    MutableCheese mc = new MutableCheese();
    mc.setName("Cousteron");
    mc.setTaste("delicious");
    return mc;
}
```

The previous annotated code looks a little messy, but that's because the mutable class had public setters.  If I can make the setters non-public, it looks a little cleaner while generating an _identical_ interface:

```
@Getter
@Setter(AccessLevel.PROTECTED)
@ExtractInterface(name="Cheese")
class MutableCheese {
    private String name;
    private String taste;
}
```

Notes:

1. I considered using `AccessLevel`, but really the only usable options for top-level interfaces are `public` or default (package) access, so it's better to not imply that the other `AccessLevel` constants are valid.  Then again, if this were implemented to support extracting as a nested interface (e.g. extract `Outer$Interface` from `Outer$Inner`) then it'd need to go back to AccessLevel.
1. Requires #1653, otherwise `@Getter(onMethod_=...)` must be copied onto each field instead of at the class level in the case where `memberMethods=false`.

