Lombok's `@FieldDefaults` may turn all fields `final` or give'em some access modifier, reducing the boilerplate of having to write all those repeatedly. Those could be prevented with `@NonFinal` and `@PackagePrivate`.

So, sometimes I see cases where a group of annotations shuld be added to all the fields. `@NonNull` being the most obvious case (think of #1619).

So I suggest to add an `onField` value in `FieldDefaults`, its return type would be `FieldDefaults.AnyAnnotation[]`.

To mark exceptions to the applied annotations, `FieldDefaults` would also have an `exceptIf` field also typed as `FieldDefaults.AnyAnnotation[]`. If not empty, this field musst have the same elements as the `onField`. A new annotation `@Dummy` can be used to fill the gaps in `exceptIf`.

Finally, an `@FieldTolerate` in any field would prevent it from being touched by either `onField` or `exceptIf`.

For example:

```java
@FieldDefaults(
    makeFinal = true,
    level = AccessLevel.PRIVATE,
    onField_ = {@NonNull, @NonEmpty, @Autowired},
    exceptIf_ = {@Nullable, @Dummy, @MyOwnCustomAnnotationForNotAutowired}
)
public class Email {
    String sender;
    String recipient;
    List<String> cc;
    List<String> cco;
    String subject;
    String content;

    @MyOwnCustomAnnotationForNotAutowired
    boolean alreadySent;

    @FieldTolerate
    @NonFinal int retryCount;
}
```

