I can try to re-word the request, since it's clear that people encounter this need and are forced to override setters to implement it, which defeats the convenience of using @Setter in the first place. Here goes:

Please provide an optional boolean property to the `@Setter` annotation that, when set to `true`, will include null-safe trimming of the passed-in value in the generated setter method when the field is of type `String`. A suggestion for the property name is `autoTrim` and the default value would be `false` (to maintain backwards compatibility).
When `autoTrim` is `true`, the generated setter method would look like this:
```java
public void setName(String value) {
    if (value == null) {
        this.name = null;
    } else {
        this.name = value.trim();
    }
}
```

As an alternative, a new annotation, suggested name `@StringSetter`, could be introduced that implements trimming (and potentially other String-specific options) in the generated setter(s).

