     Survival.getEntityEffectiveDifficulty(null);

When you assign `entity` in the constructor, then it's too late as the initializer expressions get evaluated *before* the constructor (which I find stupid as it's strictly less usable than the other way round, but Sun failed to ask me :laughing:).

> my expectation is that if the builder is supplied a value, it uses that value, else it executes the statement provided to default to calculate the default.

I'm afraid, that's too optimistic. AFAIK Lombok just copies the expressions to the builder. So they get *always* executed and this happens before you can set any fields manually.

This basically restrict its usefulness to constant expressions plus some rare cases like `private long start = System.nanoTime()`. Note that this is exactly like without using `@Builder`.

