Imagine a situation
```java
public void updateName(final String name, final Player player) {
    // some operation with `player` object setting it's name from `name`, for example
    player.setName(name);
    someManager = MyManagers.getSomeManager();
    someManager.updateNameOfPlayer(player, name);
    someManager.savePlayer(player);
    someManager.storeChanges();
}
```
but a vararg equivalent is also expected
```java
public void updateName(final String name, final Player... players) {
    for (val player : players) updateName(name, player);
}
```
iterable one
```java
public void updateName(final String name, final Iterable<Player> players) {
    for (val player : players) updateName(name, player);
}
```
and an iterator one
```java
public void updateName(final String name, final Iterator<Player> players) {
    while (players.hasNext()) updateName(name, players.next());
}
```

It is not a problem but when having multiple similar cases in one class it makes it hard to maintain.
The suggestion is to add `@IteratorEquivalent` annotation which will be placed on method parameter or on the method itself (applying this functionality to the last paramter) which will lead to vararg `T...`, `Iterable<T>` and `Iterator<T>` variants of this method be generated.
```java
public void updateName(final String name, @IteratorEquivalent final Player player) {
    // some operation with `player` object setting it's name from `name`, for example
    player.setName(name);
    someManager = MyManagers.getSomeManager();
    someManager.updateNameOfPlayer(player, name);
    someManager.storeChanges();
}
```
A recommended parameter would be `ignoreNull` (`true` by default) which would simply check iterations in case the array, Iterable or Iterator were `null` instead of throwing NPE.

Additionally it would be great if there would be an ablity to set annotation's parameter `inline` to `true` (better `true` by default) in order to make iteration statements not simply call to base method, but copy it's content adding iteration where needed
So, for the example given the generated methods would be
```java
public void updateName(final String name, final Player... players) {
    // some operation with `player` object setting it's name from `name`, for example
    for (val player : players) player.setName(name);
    someManager = MyManagers.getSomeManager();
    for (val player : players) someManager.updateNameOfPlayer(player, name);
    for (val player : players) someManager.savePlayer(player);
    someManager.storeChanges();
}
// and the same with Iterable<Player> and Iterator<Player>
```
In this case it would also be great to have a `compress` parameter in annotation (better `true` by default) which would put adjoining statements under one iteration:
```java
public void updateName(final String name, final Player... players) {
    // some operation with `player` object setting it's name from `name`, for example
    for (val player : players) player.setName(name);
    someManager = MyManagers.getSomeManager();
    for (val player : players) {
        someManager.updateNameOfPlayer(player, name);
        someManager.savePlayer(player);
    }
    someManager.storeChanges();
}
// and the same with Iterable<Player> and Iterator<Player>
```

Thanks in advance and waiting for any more ideas and enhancements <3

