First of all: If you need that many variants of the same method, somebody
is doing something wrong in terms of architecture.

As an example: How about variants for List<Player>, Set<Player>,
Stream<Player>, and plain Collection<Player>? And then add Vavr collections
for even more fun? ;-)
The number of ways people can pass around collections of objects in methods
is basically endless, are we going to accomodate all of them?

The idea of lombok is to make it easier to create boilerplate, but the goal
of that is that you end up having to maintain less code, making it easier
to keep your code DRY.
This is doing the opposite: repeating yourself over and over again in order
to accomodate multiple users, then automating away the problem so that you
don't see it.
Why not just have those users deal with having a single contract instead?
This way lies insanity, really.




