@jkaipa There's no such thing, but you can use
```
@ToString.Exclude // Not needed as the equally named method "overrides" the field.
private Calendar calendar;

@ToString.Include
private Calender calendar() {
    return MyFancyFormatter.format(calendar);
}
```

This is more flexible, but also more verbose than a general formatting applicable to all fields of type `Calendar`.

---

I could imagine something like
```
@ToString(transform=MyFancyFormatter::format)
```
but method references are not allowed in annotations. There could be
```
@ToString(transform=MyFancyFormatter.class)
```
which would require a class implementing `Function<?, ?>`. It'd require Java 8 and this may be a blocker as AFAIK Lombok so far works with Java 8.

Would it be better for you?

@axiopisty What Jackson does is interesting, but AFICT not directly applicable here. Note also that Jackson uses reflection for its magic, which is orders of magnitude simpler than what Lombok has to do. I guess, my

     @ToString(transform=MyFancyFormatter.class)

would cover most needs (maybe even all of them for the price of writing a few formatter classes). Together with a possibility to [define own aggregate annotations](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/issues/602) (another long wanted feature), it'd be pretty flexible.

However, I'm not a member of the team and I currently don't have the time to work on it. So you need an approval from a Lombok team member and a voluteer.

---

Note also that

    @ToString.Include private Whatever whatever() {return MyFancyFormatter.format(whatever);}

is sufficient (no need for `String.Exclude` as this happens automatically because of the method name being equal to the field name) for everything (depending on what you implemented in the formatter class).

