nodes.body:
What else should it do? The `toBuilder` option is meant for creating a builder instance from the fields of the current object. Without `a` and `b`, it can't work.

Though it could: The `toBuilder` method could get all the arguments missing in the class, e.g., like
```
@Value class C {
    int x, y, z;

    @Builder(toBuilder=true)
    someMethod(int a, int x, int b, int y) {
    }
}
```

could generate
```
    public toSomeMethodBuilder(int a, int b) {
        return new SomeMethodBuilder(a, this.x, b, this.y);
    }
```

Is this what you wanted?

In your last example, there's no reason for wanting an automatically generated `toBuilder` (unless you expect Lombok to be able to assemble datetimes from the parts :rofl: ). You don't start with an instance of your class, which is what `toBuilder` is for.

All you need is a plain builder, with possibly added methods. The standard builder will accept `dateTime(ZonedDateTime)`, and you only need to write
```
@Value
@Builder
class Xyz {
    private final ZonedDateTime dateTime;

    class XyzBuilder {
        public Builder dateTime(ZonedDate date, LocalTime time) {
           return dateTime(ZonedDateTime.of(date, time));
        }
    }
}
```

I hope the existence of the manually written `dateTime` does not prevent the normally generated `dateTime`; otherwise, try placing `Tolerate` on your method.

