On the command line the project compiles properly. Shouldn't the generated no-arg constructor simply skip the initialized, final field?

Hi, consider the following code:

```java
//import edu.umd.cs.findbugs.annotations.SuppressFBWarnings;
import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Getter;
import lombok.Value;
import lombok.experimental.Wither;

@Value
@Wither
@Getter
@AllArgsConstructor
//@SuppressFBWarnings({"ES_COMPARING_PARAMETER_STRING_WITH_EQ", "RC_REF_COMPARISON"})
public class Example {
    String element1;
    Integer element2;
}
```

Currently (both with Lombok 1.18.2 and edge), [SpotBugs](https://github.com/spotbugs/spotbugs) complains about the wither methods, which accordingly to delombok are that:

```java
    @java.lang.SuppressWarnings("all")
    public Example withElement1(final String element1) {
        return this.element1 == element1 ? this : new Example(element1, this.element2);
    }

    @java.lang.SuppressWarnings("all")
    public Example withElement2(final Integer element2) {
        return this.element2 == element2 ? this : new Example(this.element1, element2);
    }
```

The reasons of the complaint are using `==` to compare `String`s (which SpotBugs complains as `ES_COMPARING_PARAMETER_STRING_WITH_EQ`) and using `==` to compare primitive wrappers (which SpotBugs complains as `RC_REF_COMPARISON`).

It's truth that those methods are annotated with `@java.lang.SuppressWarnings("all")`. However, since `@SuppressWarnings` have source-retention and SpotBugs works on bytecode, it can't be aware of it.

This is not a critical issue, since it can be easily workarounded by adding a few `@SuppressFBWarnings` on the class (those commented-out in the class above). However, this have the downside that it would disable some SpotBugs' analysis in the entire class instead of just in lombok generated methods.

I've created a minimal, complete and verifiable test case for that using gradle [**here**](https://github.com/victorwss/lombok-bug-tests/tree/master/equals-spotbugs-complains).

So, I'm looking for ideas of how could Lombok make SpotBugs happy without having to rely in the user adding `@SuppressFBWarnings`. And I have one:

* In those `@Wither` methods, `this.element1 == element1` could be replaced with `(this.element1 == null ? element1 == null : this.element1.equals(element1))`.

This approach also have the advantage that it avoids that `exampleInstance.withElement1("X").withElement1("X")` creates a new instance twice.

However, it have the downside that we don't know about the implementation of `element1`'s `equals` method, so using it might be is risky. There is several approaches about that:

* **A**: Lombok could simply assume the risk and call `equals` anyway. If the object does not have a safe and sane `equals` implementation, it would be not Lombok's problem. Most of the classes have sane and safe `equals` implementation, including those that inherits it directly from `java.lang.Object`. However, a few types like `java.net.URL` are notable for not having a safe and sane `equals` implementation. Also, using `equals` on mutable types may lead to some bad surprises.

* **B**: Selectively special-case the use of `equals` or `==`. The `equals` method would be used if `element1` is of some type that have a known safe and sane implementation of `equals` and is worth of it. This is the case of `String`, primitive wrappers, `java.time.*` types, `BigInteger`, `BigDecimal`, `OptionalInt`, `OptionalDouble`, `OptionalLong` and a few other types. The case of `Optional<X>` when `X` also have a safe and sane implementation of `equals` sould theoretically also use `equals`, but this is probably very hard to implement (possibly even undecidible), so it might be better to just fallback to `==`.

* **C**: Add some sort of field in the `@Wither` annotation to allow the programmer specify what to do: (1) Always create a new object regardless of anything; (2) avoid the creation of a new object if possible by testing the value with `==`; (3) avoid the creation of a new object if possible by testing the value with `equals`; (4) guess the best implementation using approach **B** and fallback to `==` and perhaps give a warning if it is too hard to determine.

