	}
	in.read();

Thus, allowing `@Cleanup` in for-each loop variable definitions would not introduce a new kind of problem. So I withdraw my objection in this regard.

(I am @m-kuklinski on my personal account)

As per @janrieke, though, are we this doesn't really introduce a new problem, nor protect us from one.

You can mimic the same behavior, as said, by just doing:

`
for (val _stream : streams) {
	@Cleanup val stream = _stream;
	...
}
`

Arguably, that's _worse_ than it originally was, whereas the syntax:

`
for (@Cleanup val stream : streams) {
	...
}
`

... is clearer as to its intent, as it doesn't have two references to the object floating around in scope.

I'm just not sure why the library is prohibiting users from doing it - whether it's bad practice or not is subjective, but it seems odd to me to prevent a user from doing something that the library author(s) consider bad practice - it is usually best to defer to the user, and not require the user to use odd syntax to do it. That _could_ just be the C++-programmer in me, though.

This also isn't an unusual concept in other languages. Both C++ and C# handle this pattern fine - C++, at least, via requiring a thin wrapper class and RAII semantics (RAII semantics being what `@Cleanup` effectively mimics)... so to me, _not_ allowing `@Cleanup` on _any_ variable definition is unusual and sort of violates RAII concepts, as it means that certain variables are excluded for some reason, whereas if the user is explicitly saying "I want a cleanup method called when this variable goes out of scope", that should be honored...

A case where that could be useful is if someone is using `@Cleanup(...)` with a custom method, and is using it just to call something when that variable exits scope. It seems odd to exclude the functionality in specific circumstances.

