    private final StampedLock s;
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, StampedLock> m = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final Inner2 i2;
    public Inner1(String s2, Inner2 i2) {
      this.i2 = i2;
      this.s = m.computeIfAbsent(s2, k -> new StampedLock());
    }
  }
  static class Inner2 {
    Inner2(Map<String,String> m) {}
  }
}
```

In this case, I suspect that adding generic parameters to the ImmutableMap factory method fixes your problems.

`new Inner1(getId(), new Inner2(ImmutableMap.<String,String>of("fff", getId())));`

My suggestion for lazy getters that cause problems is to move the initialization to a private (if possible static) method.


```java
public abstract class Test implements HasId {
  @Getter(lazy = true)
  private final Inner1 i1 = initializeI1();

  private Inner1 initializeI1() {
    return new Inner1(getId(), new Inner2(ImmutableMap.of("fff", getId())));
  }

  static class Inner1 {
    private final StampedLock s;
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, StampedLock> m = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final Inner2 i2;
    public Inner1(String s2, Inner2 i2) {
      this.i2 = i2;
      this.s = m.computeIfAbsent(s2, k -> new StampedLock());
    }
  }
  static class Inner2 {
    Inner2(Map<String,String> m) {}
  }
}
```

