Actually, `@Wither` does not seem a good choice for creating marshalling datastructures, e.g., for JSON for a few reasons:
1. The parsing will create as many objects as fields to set, but actually we want only the outcome of the parsing to be immutable
2. `@Wither` seems to ignore `@Singular`, which makes for pretty awkward dealing with collections: the entire collection needs to be set, which causes the risk of side-effects when people forget to pass to `withXXX` a deep copy: `@Singular` would be actually much safer (assuming the initialization uses a sane default), as the only possible source of side-effects would be the values to be stored in the collection
3. `@Wither` does not seem to work properly with `@NoArgsConstructor` (which seems to me to be needed when using `@JsonCreator`), as the `@NoArgsConstructor` does not properly initialize the `@Wither` fields. For example, on a JDK 8, the following ends up in a compilation error for the `value` variable not being initialized:

<pre>
@Value
@NoArgsConstructor(onConstructor_ = {@JsonCreator})
public class MyObject {

  @Wither private final String value;

}
</pre>

