This is essentially _almost_ solvable by the "types"-subfunction of `@Delegate`. However this requires the interface to be non-generic, which is a major drawback.
If this restriction could be addressed, then it would be possible to specify precisely the methods to be exposed (only adders, stream, getters....) by specifying some general Collections-related interfaces, which could be provided by lombok itself.
The feature would then look something like:
```
@Delegate(types = { Adders.class, Getters.class })
private final ArrayList<String> m_ids = new ArrayList<>();
```

While this seems more bulky, it actually gives _much greater flexibility_ and allows for the exposition of precisely the fields intended to be exposed. I know too little of the injection-mechanic to tell, if the type resolution would be possible, or if a placeholder-class (e.g. something blank just called 'Generic') could be used. in the following way, when specifying the interface:

```
public interface Adders {
    public void add(Generic object);
    public void add(int index, Generic object);
    public void addAll(Collection<? extends Generic> objects);
    public void addAll(int index, Collection<? extends Generic> objects);
}
```

If the above could be for example matched to `ArrayList`'s adder methods (which are in fact type parameterized by one type parameter), then the requested feature could be built (with slightly more syntax, but much more flexibility).

The only thing that need to be added to the above is the ability to postfix the interface methods and a `@Plural` annotation for pluralizing the postfix, so 
```
@Delegate(types = { Adders.class }, postfix = "name")
private final ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
```
with
```
public interface Adders {
    public void add(Generic object);
    public void add(int index, Generic object);
	@Plural
    public void addAll(Collection<? extends Generic> objects);
	@Plural
    public void addAll(int index, Collection<? extends Generic> objects);
}
```
