    private B b;
    private String string;
}

@Builder
public class B {
    private C c;
    private int i;
}
```
I would like to use a builder like this:
```java
ABuilder aBuilder = A.builder()
    .b(b->b
        .c(new C("initial value"))
        .i(1)
    )
    .string("extra");

A a1 = aBuilder.build();
A a2 = aBuilder
    .b(b->b
        .c(new C("allowing to replace a.b.c"))
        .i(2)
    )
    .build();
A a3 = aBuilder
    .b(B.builder()
        .c("allowing to use sub-builders"))
    .build();
A a4 = aBuilder
    .b(null) // allowing to set to a concrete value (backwards compatible)
    .build();
```
The `.b()` method would accept a `Consumer<BBuilder>` used to update the `BBuilder`.
Only at the time that `A` is built, the builders for its fields would build their objects as well.

