```
import lombok.Value;
import lombok.experimental.Wither;

import java.util.Optional;

@Value
class Example {

    @Wither
    Optional<String> field;
}
```

This will trigger [OptionalEquality](
http://errorprone.info/bugpattern/OptionalEquality) rule. Culprit code is (after delomboking):

```
   public Example withField(Optional<String> field) {
        return this.field == field ? this : new Example(field);
    }
```
In addition error prone itself will crash due to IllegalArgumentException. But this is more on them I think, it is tracked here: https://github.com/google/error-prone/issues/447

So far only workaround for that is to `@SuppressWarnings("OptionalEquality")` every time `@Wither` is used for `Optional` field or to disable rule all together.

It would be nice if we could automatically add `@SuppressWarnings("OptionalEquality")` to all wither methods (or just ones for `Optional` fields).

 **Proposal**
Add a hook method after Constructor.

It can help developer to validate/cache/compute.

**Hope Implementations**
```
  public class SomeClass {
  private final LocalDate birthDate;
  private int age;

  @AfterConstructor
  private void afterConstructor() {
    compute();
    valid();
    cache();
  }

  private void compute() {
    this.age = LocalDate.now().getYear()-birthDate.getYear();
  }

  private void valid() {
    Preconditions.checkArgument(age<150);
  }

  private void cache() {
    //do cache...
  }
}
```

Notes:
1.**I hope the feature is a new method annotation.**
Because i guess that developer hope the after constructor method excute after all generated constructors will covers 90% of cases.
In this case,a new method annotation is better than @XArgsConstructor attribute.

2.**Ignore or not allow explicitly written constructor.Of course,add an option in lombok.config is perfect practice.**
Explicitly written constructor can do anything without lombok.
Lombok should not interfere explicitly written code and inject explicitly written constructor is very difficult.

3.For some cases that method only need excute after some constructors,@AfterConstructor can have a attribute such as excludeConstructs,param type is @XArgsConstructor annotation class array.

I think this is the most important feature that lombok should have, but it does not have.

I use Lombok's `@Getter` whenever possible. Almost all of the pure getters I find myself writing these days are for `java.util.Optional`s.

```
private final String foo;
    
public Optional<String> getFoo()
{
    return Optional.ofNullable(foo);
}
```
Changing the field declaration to `Optional<String>` is not a great solution because any setters would expect an `Optional`. It's also frowned upon to use `Optional` as a field value (IntelliJ will give you a warning, for example).

It would be nice if `@Getter` had an option which allowed me to generate this automatically. For example:

```
@Getter(optional = true) private final String foo;
```

This would obviously default to false.

