I am finding myself having to write a small amount of boilerplate in order to provide convenient interfaces for classes that contain lots of collection-type methods.

**Describe the feature**

What I would like to do is provide a var-args version of any method which has a single parameter of type Collection<T>. What this ends up meaning in practice is a lot of trivial method overloading which, in my view, could benefit from Lombokification.

*Lomboked:*
```
    @VarArgs
    public void addAll(Collection<T> allTheThings) {
        // do stuff here
    }
```

*Delomboked:*
```
    public void addAll(Collection<T> allTheThings) {
        // do stuff here
    }

    public void add(T... allTheThings) {   // this method is just routine boilerplate...
        this.addAll(Arrays.asList(allTheThings));
    }
```

In the above, I have removed "All" in the lombokified method name, since this seems to be the convention used by the Java libraries. My proposed logic for this is to just detect and remove "All" if it is at the end of the method name, otherwise just overload the method name as-is.

**Describe the target audience**
This could be useful for anyone who frequently delegates to wrapped collections in order to define an interface. It is most valuable in context of a class which has lots of `xAll` methods, where `x` is a verb for taking action on every object in a Collection.

