@Maaartinus `List` and `Set` both implement `Collection`, I guess you mean they should also work if the argument is a Set or a List.

`Iterable` might be a good thing to add.

Is `name` already used on other Lombok notations? If so, please give me an example (I am only really familiar with @Getter, @Setter, @EqualsAndHashcode, and a few of the constructor annotations and compound annotations using these...)

@kalexmills 
> `List` and `Set` both implement `Collection`

Correct, but not useful (see below).

> I guess you mean they should also work if the argument is a Set or a List.

What I meant is that
```
@VarArgs public void addAll(List<T> allTheThings) {...}
```
should work, too. Inheritance won't help as
```
@VarArgs public void addAll(ArrayList<T> allTheThings) {...}
```
won't work (unless we'd decide to support `ArrayList`, which I doubt), and
```
@VarArgs public void addAll(MyOwnListImpl<T> allTheThings) {...}
```
won't work either (and you can not make it work).

I'd say that `VarArgs` should be allowed, if the last argument is *exactly* `Iterable`, `Collection`, or `List` (maybe also `Set`, though it needs a slightly different implementation).

---

> Is `name` already used on other Lombok notations?

There's `ToString.Include.name` and things like `@FieldNameConstants.innerTypeName` and two `@Builder.*Name`s. Maybe using `value` (the default annotation argument) might be better.

@Maaartinus sounds good to me.

I think I understand what you're saying now. If we can do it for `Collection` using the implementation I have proposed using `Arrays.asList`, then making `Iterable`, and `List` work also shouldn't be much extra effort, since the generated code will continue to work. (Although I guess the level of effort depends on Lombok's implementation details -- which I have not (yet) seen)).

`Set` certainly needs a different implementation. For JDK targets of 8+, one can do this.

```
    public void addAll(Set<T> allTheThings) {
        // do stuff here
    }

    public void add(T... allTheThings) {   // this method is just routine boilerplate...
        this.addAll(Set.of(allTheThings));
    }
```

For targets of JDK 7 and lower, there may be a better implementation than the one below, which is just the first thing I can think of.

```
    public void addAll(Set<T> allTheThings) {
        // do stuff here
    }

    public void add(T... allTheThings) {
        this.addAll(new HashSet<T>(allTheThings));
    }
```

EDIT: I just realized that providing separate implementations based on the JDK target API level is probably not the greatest thing to do, if Lombok is as hard to implement / maintain features for as I have heard. There's a trade-off here which project maintainers must certainly have encountered before, so thoughts are welcome.

