**Describe the bug**
inner methods and fields are auto-marked as 'static' in a `@Utility` class. However, for inner types, evidently in eclipse they are marked as static, but in javac they are not.

**To Reproduce**

```java
@lombok.Utility public class Test {
    public void example() {
        return new Foo();
     }

    public class Foo {}
}
```

The above compiles in eclipse, but in javac will fail, erroring out on the `new Foo` call, saying that `this` cannot be referenced in a static context.

lombok v1.18.4

**Expected behavior**
All inner types in `@Utility` classes should be static, there's no point having them be instance-based.

**Problem**
I use lombok's @Getter and @Builder almost everywhere. However, both of these implementations expect the property to be known during run-time. I faced a particular challenge while coding recently in which I had to map a set of object properties to a hashmap. Hence the properties could only be referenced using their names during runtime which was nothing but the key in the hashmap. 

**Solution**
I ended up writing a custom annotation and used Java Reflections to achieve something as follows.
```
 @ItemProperty(name = "flow", type = "String")
   private String flow;

  public String getValueByPropertyName(String name){
      for(Field f: Shipment.class.getDeclaredFields()){
         ItemProperty property = f.getAnnotation(ItemProperty.class);
         if(property != null && property.name().equals(name)){
            try {
               return String.valueOf(f.get(this));
            } catch (IllegalAccessException e) {
               return null;
            }
         }
      }
      return null;
   }

   public void setValueByPropertyName(String name, String value){
      for(Field f: Shipment.class.getDeclaredFields()){
         ItemProperty property = f.getAnnotation(ItemProperty.class);
         if(property != null && property.name().equals(name)){
            try{
               f.set(this, getValueOf(property.type(), value));
               break;
            } catch (IllegalAccessException e) { }
         }
      }
   }

   private Object getValueOf(String type, String value){
      switch(type){
      case "Boolean":
         return Boolean.valueOf(value);
      case "Integer":
         return Integer.valueOf(value);
      default:
         return value;
      }
   }

```
**Target audience**
I think such functionality can be inbuilt in lombok and can be used easily when certain properties can be attributes of one model but have to be modelled as a first class construct of another model

