copyable annotations end up in the class file just fine. Your example requires a ton of deps. I've tried to reduce it to a simple reproducible test case:

in dir: `ROOT/pkg`

### lombok.config

```
config.stopBubbling = true
lombok.copyableAnnotations += pkg.Ann
```

### Ann.java

```java
package pkg;

import static java.lang.annotation.ElementType.*;
import static java.lang.annotation.RetentionPolicy.*;

import java.lang.annotation.Retention;
import java.lang.annotation.Target;

@Target({METHOD, FIELD, PARAMETER})
@Retention(RUNTIME)
public @interface Ann {}
```

### Test.java

```java
package pkg;

import pkg.Ann;

@lombok.Getter @lombok.Builder
public class Test {
	@Ann
	private int foo;
}
```

### test the effect

in `ROOT/pkg`: `javac -cp lombok.jar *.java` to compile it all, then:

`javap -p -v Test` to show that the generated getter has the annotation, and `javap -p -v Test\$TestBuilder` to show that the `foo(int)` method in the generated builder has the annotation.


I suggest you run javap on the produced class files to at least ensure that the annotation is there first. Then, can you make a fully self contained and small test that shows precisely what lombok is doing wrong?

