Thanks for the reference.  I searched for "step builder" before posting this but was unable to find anything.

That said, I question the reasons listed against it in the wiki.  I think a simple step builder could be implemented very elegantly with Lombok, and I don't think "the cure is worse than the disease".

Taking a desired output from the first article (simplified a little bit):

```
Email email = Email.builder()
    .to("foo@gmail.com")
    .subject("Hello")
    .content("Test email")
    .build();
```

This could be implemented very trivially using Lombok, where you must annotate each field with the step order, and you can optionally give the method a custom name:

```
@StepBuilder
public class Email {
    @Step(1, "to")
    String emailAddress;
    @Step(2)
    String subject;
    @Step(3)
    String content;
}
```

It becomes slightly more complicated if you want to allow multiple options for, e.g., step 2, but I think that is perfectly solvable as well.

Addressing the concerns listed in the wiki:

> 1. It becomes impossible to smear the job of building out across various modules (if this method sets one thing, and that method sets another...)

I'm not sure I follow this, what's the concern here compared to a standard builder?

> 2. You are either forced to first set a, and then b, and then the rest, in that exact order, or you get a combinatorial explosion of interfaces.

That's fine, I _want_ to force an exact order.

> 3. Your autocomplete dialogs and outline views in your IDE get infected with a massive list of weird stuff.

Why does it get infected?  It won't get infected if you require the steps to be declared in specific order and there's no combinatorial explosion.

> 4. You also need to consider the notion of having a builder where you override (set 'again') a value, and the notion of multi-settable things (i.e. it must be set at least once but can be set multiple times, for example with `@Singular`).

Yes, this is slightly more complicated.  I would argue that non-collections should only be able to be set once.  `@Singular` collections could be solved by some type of `.endOfCollection()` method (probably deserving of a better name).

