The [changelog](https://projectlombok.org/changelog) for v1.18.4 contains a surprising change to the behavior of `@Getter` and `@Setter` when a nullability annotation is used.

Lombok has always had its own `@NonNull` annotation which injected runtime null checking, but you didn't _have_ to use it.  If you wanted to use a documentation annotation like JetBrains [@NotNull](https://github.com/JetBrains/java-annotations/blob/master/java8/src/main/java/org/jetbrains/annotations/NotNull.java), you could.  Lombok would copy it to the getters and setters, but it wouldn't inject any runtime checks.

As of 1.18.4, there doesn't appear to be any way of avoiding runtime checks.  I generally have IntelliJ IDEA configured to [compile runtime null checks](https://www.jetbrains.com/help/idea/compiler.html) for development but omit them from server builds.  Lombok's interjection here is redundant with what my IDE is already doing and unwanted on the server.

I tried `lombok.nonNull.flagUsage` values of `warning` and `allow`, but neither kept the runtime check from being added.  (I'm not using "NonNull", I'm using JetBrains "No**t**Null".)  The new `lombok.copyableAnnotations` property seems to be add-only.  I tried `= []` and it didn't change anything.

In a large code base where nullability annotations are already being used but only for documentation purposes, Lombok's new behavior poses an unwanted risk.  It's now possible that a usage pattern that worked (even if it was ill-advised) could start to actively fail.  There should be a way to configure the previous behavior.

In other words, I want to configure `public @NotNull @Getter @Setter String foo;` (or the `@Data` versions of the same) to produce **only** the following:
```
public @NotNull String getFoo() {
    return foo;
}
public void setFoo(@NotNull String foo) {
    this.foo = foo;
}
```

