Hello, I often need to clone entire objects, except for some fields. This is useful when the POJO goes through some external operation and that update needs to be reflected. I will call this new method `fromObject` throughout. Another way of describing this is adding a `clone` method in the `@Builder`.

Example:

### Immutable Foo
```java
// Example object
@Data
@Builder
public Class Foo{
    private final int id;
    private final String name;
}
```

## Proposed feature

```java
final Foo foo = Foo.builder().name("name").build();

try {
    final int assignedId = doStuff(foo) //foo has somehow changed here, maybe an API call or persistence
    return Foo.builder()
        .fromObject(foo) // magic happens here. All foo fields are copied to the new object
        .id(assignedId) //... except for id, which gets explicitly overwritten by the call to the setter
        .build();
} catch(...){}
```

With current Lombok features:

```java
final Foo foo = Foo.builder().name("name").build();

try {
    final int assignedId = doStuff(foo) //foo has somehow changed here, maybe an API call or persistence
    return Foo.builder()
        .name(foo.getName())
        // more setters here
        .id(assignedId)
        .build();
} catch(...){}
```


If we only need to change `id` we can do the above with `@Wither`, but this becomes tediously repetitive when dealing with multiple setters - both in `Foo` definition and when using it to copy objects. It would also help code clarity if the fact that `foo` is being copied is explicitly specified.

I've seen the following with plain Java (and non-final POJOs):


### Mutable Foo
```java
