**Describe the feature**
Motivation for `@Object`: class design in OO language can range from objects to datastructures. The former hide internal information an expose APIs to manipulate and query internal state whereas the latter expose data "as is" and behave similar to `struct` in the C language. Since datastructures are very well covered in lombok by `@Data` and `@Value` (mutable and immutable) I propose `@Object` for classes that behave like "real" objects.

Essentially objects
- have an internal state which is not queryable
- are comparable/loggable (hc, eq, toString) on that internal state
- expose a well defined API

minimal java sample

```
public class Person {
  
  private String firstName;
  private String lastName;
  private List<String> phoneNumbers;

  public Person(String firstName, String lastName, List<String> phoneNumbers) {
    this.firstName = firstName;
    this.lastName = lastName;
    this.phoneNumbers = phoneNumbers;
  }
  
  public String getDisplayName() {
    return firstName + " " + lastName;
  }
  
  public void changeName(String firstName, String lastName) {
    // ...
  }
  
  public void addPhone(String phone) {
    // some validation
    phoneNumbers.add(phone);
  }
  
  // hc, eq, toString on internal state
  
}
```

Lombok Version

```
@Object
public class Person {

  String firstName;
  String lastName;
  List<String> phoneNumbers;

  public String getDisplayName() {
    return firstName + " " + lastName;
  }

  public void changeName(String firstName, String lastName) {
    // ...
  }

  public void addPhone(String phone) {
    // some validation
    phoneNumbers.add(phone);
  }
}
```

So essentially `@Object` combines
```
@AllArgsConstructor
@FieldDefaults(level = AccessLevel.PRIVATE)
@EqualsAndHashCode
@ToString
```

Furthermore I find myself rewriting the same combination for a certain type of object over and over again:

```
@Slf4j
@AllArgsConstructor // or required args contructor
@FieldDefaults(level = AccessLevel.PRIVATE, makeFinal = true)
```

which is why I would like to see an annotation along the lines of: `@ServiceObject` or `@Component`. the latter is preferred by me, but clashes with springs `@Component` unfortunately, which is why `@ServiceComponent` might be a good compromise.

**Describe the target audience**
Developers who take OO/DDD seriously will greatly benefit from `@Object`. All (Enterprise-)Application Developers will benefit from `@ServiceObject` 

