```

**Describe the target audience**
People wanting to use the  `@JsonSetter` annotation to influence the deserialization of some JSON attributes.

In my project, I wanted to use `@JsonSetter(nulls = Nulls.SKIP)` to force setting a default value if the field is set to `null` in the JSON input. I needed to manually add the annotation to the setters by using an interface, as recommended on [@Builder / With Jackson](https://projectlombok.org/features/Builder). If this annotation was copied to the builder's setter methods, I could avoid the additional code in this interface.

I often convert simple beans to java HashMap and backwards. It's a tedious task.
I've built and used some reflection based tools, but a compile-time option like a Lombok feature could be the very best option IMHO (for performance issues, for example).

The very basic idea:
```java
@MapBuilder(class=java.util.HashMap)
public class Person {
   private String name;
   private String cell;
}
```
generate methods
```java
public Person fromMap(Map<String,Objet> map) {
   Person p = new Person();
   P.name = map.get("name"); 
   P.cell = map.get("cell"); 
  return p;
}

public Map toMap() {
   Map map = new HashMap<String,Object>();
   map.put("name",this.name);
   map.put("cell",this.cell);
  return map;
}
```
This is the very basic idea. It could have more features, like annotations for key customization, and so on.

nodes.body:
What do you plan to use the map for? Presumably for some sort of serialization.

Serialization and deserialization is a hot topic. There are already lots of projects that try to tackle this. Some use reflection (Jackson), some use code generation (MapStruct).

For simple objects, your example might work. But the moment you're allowing more complex objects, it get very complex very fast.

In Lombok, we don't have resolution, so we cannot inspect any other classes.

How can we handle the following example:

```java
class Person {
  private String name;
  private LocalDate born;
  private Address address;
  private List<Person> emergencyContacts;
}
```

1. In your example, fromMap should probably static.
2. Do we put the LocalDate in the map, or an ISO string?
