Hey @Sorrowhs. This is a little late but perhaps I can offer an explanation for you or anyone else seeing this problem. I see two things going on here.

1. The `@Data` annotation works a little different than you might be expecting. `@Data` is short hand for `@Getter @Setter @RequiredArgsConstructor @ToString @EqualsAndHashCode`. The last two annotations in that list have the attribute `callSuper` which [defaults to false](https://projectlombok.org/api/lombok/ToString.html#callSuper()). That means when you apply `@Data` to `Child`, then you get `@ToString(callSuper=false)`. A new `toString` method is being generated in the child class that does not use the `toString` logic from its parent class. The same is true of methods `equals` and `hashCode`.

1. If the BeanUtils class is from Apache's commons-beanutils library, then the [copyProperties](https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-beanutils/apidocs/org/apache/commons/beanutils/BeanUtils.html#copyProperties-java.lang.Object-java.lang.Object-) are backwards. It should be `BeanUtils.copyProperties(destination, original)`; that is `BeanUtils.copyProperties(c, p)`

I believe this snippet of code will give you the results you are expecting. It's a bit wordier, but still less wordy than implementing the methods yourself.

``` java
public class LombokTest {
	@Data
	public static class Person {
		private String name;
	}
	
	@ToString(callSuper = true)
	@EqualsAndHashCode(callSuper = true)
	@RequiredArgsConstructor
	@Getter
	@Setter
	public static class Child extends Person {
	}
	
	public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
		Person p = new Person();
		p.setName("me");
		Child c = new Child();
		BeanUtils.copyProperties(c, p);
		System.out.println(c);
	}
}
```
Output: `LombokTest.Child(super=LombokTest.Person(name=me))`
Hope this helps 😄

