lombok 1.18.10
eclipse 2019-03
java 11 (zulu11.31.11 winx64)
maven 3.6.1, same JDK

### Request

Like `@lombok.Generated`, it is useful if delombok inserts specified comment around generated methods and field, like:

```java
    // parasoft-begin-suppress ALL  "generated with delombok"
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "User(name=" + this.getName() + ", age=" + this.getAge() + ")";
    }
    // parasoft-end-suppress ALL    "generated with delombok"
```
```java
    // parasoft-begin-suppress ALL  "generated with delombok"
    private static final org.slf4j.Logger log = org.slf4j.LoggerFactory.getLogger(SomeClass.class);
    // parasoft-end-suppress ALL    "generated with delombok"
```

The content of comment should be specified with preference like:

```xml
  <format value="generatedCodeBeginningComment:// parasoft-begin-suppress ALL &quot;generated with delombok&quot;" />
  <format value="generatedCodeEndingComment:// parasoft-end-suppress ALL &quot;generated with delombok&quot;" />
```

### Background
Some static code analysis tools, such as Parasoft Jtest, ignore `@lombok.Generated` annotation. But they support suppression comment like `// parasoft-begin-suppress` and `// parasoft-end-suppress`.
cf. https://docs.parasoft.com/display/JTEST1040/Suppressing+the+Reporting+of+Findings

Let's say I have the following class:

```
@Data
public class MyEntity {
  private boolean contactform;
}
```

Lombok then provides the getter method `isContactform()`. In some cases I'd like this method to be called `hasContactform()`.

Could we provide a feature/property which allows changing the prefix for a specific field?

I need something like "NotNullValidation" that provide a method for a class to check whether all the fields of the class are not null. I think this is very useful for domain objects. Thanks.

Example:
```

@NotNullValidation
@Data
public class Admin {
    private String account;
    private String password;
}
```
```
Admin admin = new Admin();
admin.setAccount("123");
admin.allFieldsNotNull(); // false
```

# Description of the issue

As lombok is just using the #toString implementation of an object's fields, this can lead to ambiguities when using String fields:

Provided we have a simple class:

```
  @Data
  class SimpleClass {
    private final String field;
  }
```

Thus `new SimpleClass(null).toString()` and `new SimpleClass("null").toString()` will yield `"SimpleClass(stringField=null)"`.

This is inconvenient, since we cannot determine whether the field is `null` or whether it is the `String` `"null"`. Additionally also empty strings will become more readable.

# Possible solution

When the field would be quoted we would have `stringField=null` and `stringField="null"` respectively. Of course quoting must only apply when the field is actually a `String` instance thus exluding the `null` value.

This is also in line with most code generators. For example IntelliJ IDEA would generate a `#toString()` method like the following (using single quotes):

```
    @Override
    public String toString() {
      return "SimpleClass{" +
          "field='" + field + '\'' +
          '}';
    }
```

Note that, however, this implementation is doing it wrong since it will also quote the `null` value thus making the quoting useless.

To support this an attribute like `boolean quoteStringValues() default false` could be added to the `@ToString` annotation:

```
  @Data
  @ToString(quoteStringValues=true)
  class SimpleClass {
    private final String field;
  }
```

# Alternative solutions

Alternatively we could have a `@ToString.Quoted` annotation that could be placed directly on the field like:

```
  @Data
  class SimpleClass {
    @ToString.Quoted
    private final String field;
  }
```

Additionally a configuration key like `lombok.toString.quoteStringValues` with values `true` or `false` might be neat to configure this on a wider scope.

# Implementation details

It could be argued whether to use single or double quotes for this. This is simply a matter of taste and will not affect the usefulness of the feature.

Note, however that it should not be an issue if the string itself contains quotes, since this would not introduce any ambiguities (using single quotes for easier readability of the code):

* `new SimpleClass("'").toString()` would yield `"SimpleClass(stringField=''')"`
* `new SimpleClass("''").toString()` would yield `"SimpleClass(stringField='''')"`

These are not "easy and nice to read", but definitely unambiguous, since it is clear that the first and last quote are part of the quoting. So I would argue that just "putting quotes around" is enough and we should not escape quote characters in-between.

# Target audience

Any developer having to deal with the occasional `null` value of fields in their data and value objects.

**Describe the feature**
Lazy getter is fine, but it's less useful than expected since it usually requires me to write an initialization method. I just wanted to write
```
	@RequiredArgsConstructor private static final class Data {
		private final Persistor persistor;

		@Getter(lazy=true)
		private final ImmutableList<Order> orders =
				persistor.newQuery(Order.class)....list();
	}
```
but this doesn't work as my `persistor` gets initialized from the constructor, i.e., after the field initializer runs. This forces me to extract the initializer into a method, which makes it much more verbose and diminishes the saving by Lombok.

I guess, the best solution would be
```
		@Getter(lazy=true)
		@InitializerToConstructor
		private final ImmutableList<Order> orders =
				persistor.newQuery(Order.class)....list();
```
where `@InitializerToConstructor` would move the initializer into the generated constructor (actually, into all of them).

---

Something like `@InitializerToMethod` just extracting the initializer into a method like I did manually would work as well.

It might be simpler to implement, however, the `@InitializerToConstructor` annotation would be useful in general, as the default Java behavior (running initializers before constructors) is rarely wanted (if ever).

---

An alternative would be a `@Memoized` annotation like
```
		@Memoized
		ImmutableList<Order> orders() {
			return persistor.newQuery(Order.class)....list();
		}
```
renaming the original method to something else and generating the field and its lazy getter named `orders` . While memoization could have tons of arguments (expiration, dependencies, etc.), this simple variant would be good enough for me.

**Describe the target audience**
Everyone needing a lazy getter depending on a field initialized in the constructor would profit from this.

