@rspilker In this case, I need both: The initializer expression should be "lazily executed" at the end of the constructor - I mean, it should see the `persistor` assigned, but it shouldn't be evaluated - the lazy getter should stay lazy. I'm trying to get a good example, but things are getting complicated.

Let's go the other path: **`@Memoized`**
Using a method instead of the field as the base for the lazy getter would also eliminate a part of the verbosity. Most of the implementation would be shared with lazy getter.

The first attempt
```
	@Getter(lazy=true)
	private final ImmutableList<Order> orders =
		persistor.newQuery(Order.class)....list();
```
doesn't work when `persistor` gets assigned in the constructor, so I had to fix it by extracting a method like
```
	private ImmutableList<Order> ordersBoilerplate() {
		return persistor.newQuery(Order.class)....list();
	}

	@Getter(lazy=true)
	private final ImmutableList<Order> orders =
		ordersBoilerplate();
```
What I'm proposing is a new annotation like 
```
	@Memoized
	ImmutableList<Order> orders() {
		return persistor.newQuery(Order.class)....list();
	}
```
generating *the same code* as the fixed lazy getter(i.e., the user-written method should be removed or renamed, an `Atomic*`-field and a getter should be generated).

I'm not proposing any advanced caching, it's just a memoization of no-args methods as a lazy getter variant.

I came to this feature because I wanted to use a lazy getter yet still have an @With annotation work on the field and I also wanted the field to be present in AllArgsConstructor (but not RequiredArgsConstructor)

More background,

I have something like this:
```
@Value
@With
@Builder(toBuilder = true)
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@AllArgsConstructor
public class HashablePayload  {


    @Getter(lazy = true)
    @With
    byte[] hash = calculateHash();
    Payload payload;

    public byte[] calculateHash()  {
        
        byte[] payloadBytes = payload.toBytes();
        MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
        return digest.digest(payloadBytes);
    }
}
```

HashablePayload represents something I transmit and receive across the network.  So when I am sending it, I want it to calculate the hash to be used when sending.  However when I am receiving it, I want to use the hash sent to me across the network so I can validate later that the calculated hash matches the hash received across the network. 

Currently @Getter(lazy = true) is not compatible with @With and @RequiredArgsConstructor @AllArgsConstructor produce the same constructor which omits hash.  

So you can not do
`HashablePayload payload = new HashablePayload(new byte[0], new Payload())`
nor
`payload.withHash(new byte[0])`


Might be able to support this use case with @Memoized like this:

```
@Value
@With
@Builder(toBuilder = true)
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@AllArgsConstructor
public class HashablePayload  {


    Payload payload;
 
    @With
    @Memoized 
    public byte[] hash()  {
        
        byte[] payloadBytes = payload.toBytes();
        MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
        return digest.digest(payloadBytes);
    }
}
```
Here the @With on the @Memoized method would instruct Lombok to allow the value to be set instead of calculated.  Presumably the @AllArgsConstructor would also include hash as a parameter.

