**Describe the feature**

Currently `@EqualsAndHashcode` works on types only, which is quite rational. But sometimes developer faces a situation when they need to provide and interface to some kind a framework, and then such developer doesn't know how to correctly implement hashcode and equals methods.

That could be addressed by static methods provided by interface itself:

```
interface Entity<E, ID> {
    Class<E> getEntityType();
    ID getEntityId();
    // this is completely unnecessary for hashcode / equals, 
    // but careless developer might not notice this
    Optional<E> getEntity();

    static int hashcode(@NonNull Entity<?, ?> entity) {
        int state = 17;
        state = state * 31 + (entity.getEntityType() == null ? 0 : entity.getEntityType().hashcode());
        state = state * 31 + (entity.getEntityId() == null ? 0 : entity.getEntityId().hashcode());
        return state;
    }

    // wildcards are used on purpose: first, other approach will probably introduce
    // unnecessary and unsafe casting, second, it will allow using raw types
    // without compiler warnings, i.e. EntityReference may be safely cast to
    // EntityReference<?, ?>
    static bool equals(EntityReference<?, ?> left, EntityReference<?, ?> right) {
        if (left == right) {
            return true;
        }

        if (left == null || right == null) {
            return false;
        }

        return Objects.equals(left.getEntityType(), right.getEntityType()) && 
            Objects.equals(left.getEntityId(), right.getEntityId());
    }

    static bool equals(EntityReference<?, ?> left, Object right) {
        return right instanceof EntityReference && equals(left, (EntityReference<?, ?>) right;
    }
}
```

Lombok'd example above would look like this:

```
@EqualsAndHashcode
interface Entity<E, ID> {
    Class<E> getEntityType();
    ID getEntityId();
    @EqualsAndHashcode.Exclude
    Optional<E> getEntity();
}
```

**Describe the target audience**
This would be a great benefit for framework developers. This audience is not massive, but the feature itself shouldn't be very costly in terms of development.

Provide a mechanism for requiring that primitive members were set via the generated builder. 

We would like a way for the code below to throw an exception when run since intField will not have been set.

This is probably not possible anymore for backwards compatibility reasons but in the spirit of ["a builder should always set all fields. No exceptions."](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/issues/1043#issuecomment-405867925) it would be preferable for builders to enforce this by default rather than for developers to have to remember to annotate each primitive field for which they expect a value.

This is similar in nature to the [Option#required in args4j](https://args4j.kohsuke.org/apidocs/org/kohsuke/args4j/Option.html#required()) which works whether or not the annotated property is primitive.

**Sample Code**

    @Builder
    public class Blah {
        @Builder.Required                   // hypothetical new annotation
        private final int intField;
    
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Blah.builder().build();
        }
    }

Generates:

    public class Blah {
        private static final class BlahBuilder {
            private Integer intField = null;

            public BlahBuilder intField(int intField) {
                this.intField = intField;
                return this;
            }

            public Blah build() {
                return new Blah(Objects.requireNonNull(intField));
            }
        }

        private static BlahBuilder builder() {
            return new BlahBuilder();
        }

        private final int intField;

        public Blah(int intField) {
            this.intField = intField;
        }
    
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Blah.builder().build();
        }
    }


**Work arounds**
Force the primitive to be Nullable:

    @Builder
    public class Blah {
        @NonNull
        private final Integer intField;
    
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Blah.builder().build();
        }
    }


Validate the primitive inside the constructor, only works if the primitive's default value is not valid and does not work for SuperBuilder because of duplicate declaration of the constructor:

    @Builder
    public class Blah {
        private final int intField;
    
        public Blah(int intField) {
            if (intField == 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
            this.intField = intField;
        }
    
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Blah.builder().build();
        }
    }


Validate the primitive post construction, only works if the primitive's default value is not valid:

    @Builder
    public class Blah {
        private static final class BlahBuilder {
            public Blah build() {
                final Blah blah = new Blah(this.intField); 
                if (blah.intField == 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();

                return blah;
            }
        }
    
        private final int intField;
            
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Blah.builder().build();
        }
    }

**Target audience**
Any customer who uses @Builder or @SuperBuilder to construct objects which have primitive members would benefit from this proposal.

**Additional context**
I found [much](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/issues/1043) previous [discussion](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/wiki/FEATURE-IDEA:-%22Mandatory%22-fields-with-@Builder) around the right way to make fields mandatory.

I believe this feature request differs from the previous discussion in that we are satisfied with construction time checking and we are interested specifically in validation of primitives.

