So the code that is doing this is:

```
		if (isBoolean && fName.startsWith("is") && fieldName.length() > 2 && !Character.isLowerCase(fieldName.charAt(2))) {
			// The field is for example named 'isRunning'.
			return booleanPrefix + fName.substring(2);
		}
```

So if the field name is `isRunning` or `is_running` or `is3running` then the getter will be `isRunning`, `is_running` or `is3running`. If the field name is `island`, then the getter will be `isIsland`.

The behaviour is kind of documented: "For boolean fields that start with is immediately followed by a title-case letter, nothing is prefixed to generate the getter name."

But it doesn't mention what happens when the next character is `_`... So that behaviour is kind of undefined, but it just so happens that it works as `_` is "not lowercase" so it works out that is leaves the field name alone and makes the accessor `is_running`. This is not JavaBeans compliant but Lombok already is not (and was it designed to be? I don't think).

Suggested workaround, also works in some other similar scenario's: move the initializer code to a private static method:
```java
import lombok.Getter;

public class TestLombokBugLazyGetter {
    
    public static String bar() { return ""; }
    
    @Getter(lazy = true)
    private final Object field = fooInitializer();
    
    private static Object fooInitializer() {
        return foo(bar(), null, () -> { });
    }
    
    public static Object foo(final Object arg0, final Object arg1, final Runnable arg3) { return null; }

}
```

