**Describe the bug**
Using `@Builder` on class level overrides `java.lang.Object.toString`

**To Reproduce**
Use the following class in Eclipse IDE:

```
// eclipse ide shows message: overrides `java.lang.Object.toString` 
@Builder
public class User {
    private int id;
    private String name;
}
```

**Expected behavior**
No need to override `java.lang.Object.toString` for using builder pattern.

**Version info (please complete the following information):**
 - Lombok version: 1.18.10
 - Platform: Eclipse 2019-09 R (4.13.0)

nodes.body:
You can simply use this class in eclipse IDE with lombok integration:
```

import lombok.Builder;
import lombok.Getter;

// eclipse ide shows message: overrides `java.lang.Object.toString`
@Builder
@Getter
public class Item {
    private int id;
    private String name;
}
```

and eclipse shows overriding symbol on the `@Builder:`

![lombok-builder](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3045549/70696596-509f3400-1cc4-11ea-8204-8445e358d084.png)

I can reproduce the behavior, but I don't think we can fix this.

For generated methods, we set the "position" at the annotation. The source position for generated setters will be the `@Setter`. This allows Eclipse to go to the Declaration if you press <kbd>F3</kbd>. 

Similar, if you use `@ToString` on a type, the line containing it will have the same green arrow:
```java
▲ @ToString
  public class User {
    private int id;
    private String name;
  }
```

Now `@Builder`. It generated a Builder class, and in that class it generates a `toString` method. The position of that method is that of the `@Builder annotation`.

You can verify in the following code by putting the caret in `toString` and pressing <kbd>F3</kbd>. You will see that eclipse will highlight `@builder`.
```java
▲ @Builder
  public class User {
      private int id;
      {
    	  builder().toString();
      }
  }
```

In short, this is expected behavior, and we don't plan to change this.

@remal Of course, people make mistakes. If these mistakes happen sufficiently often for you to be worried about it, set up a CI system and/or enforce IDE plugins. Using lombok annotations to generate the nullchecks is worse than those two options and is still forcing something.

kotlin doesn't do any of this stuff. Kotlin tells you _AT WRITE TIME_ that you messed up, by moving the nullity status into the type system. If what you want is 'I want what kotlin has', then this feature request isn't what you want.

I think it's clear this isn't going to go anywhere at this point. I keep tabs on traffic on closed issues, so if anybody has convincing arguments for why this is a worthwhile case, or has a low-maintenance option, by all means...

