Inner classes are fairly common to need instantiation by a method in their nesting class (if for no other reason then because they might be non-static and need a surrounding instance of the class they are nested in).
Often instances of such classes are created in one-liner methods that do nothing else than return the created instance.

It would be nice, if lombok supported an annotation that generated a method calling a no-arg-constructor on a so-annotated inner class.

```java
public class Box extends Container {
    @Creator("createJackInABox")
    public class Jack extends Element {
        // ....
    }
}
```

would then create a method inside `Box`:

```java
public Jack createJackInABox() {
    return new Jack();
}
```

Non-essential additional features could be:
* If `value` (method name) is unset, just generate `create[InnerClassName]`.
* If `accessLevel` is set explicitly, use that, otherwise use the inner class' visibility. (Maybe this is bad, and default should always be public?)
* If a flag (`reuse` ?) is set to `true` an additional field could be created, which keeps the instance once created and returns it, if the method is called on the same outer class instance more than once (Maybe this would require a better name than `@Creator`, since it returns a preexisting instance...?).
* `@CallCreator` - complementary annotation, which creates an (abstract if matching @Creator is missing) method from an annotated field, and calls the creator method to instantiate said field. (I know that lombok can only access one compilation unit and file, so I am explicitly *not* asking for an automatic matching over *different* class files of any sort. It would just be a shortcut to creating an abstract method and calling it:
```java
public class Container {
    private final @Getter @CallCreator("createJackInABox") Element jack;
}
```

would create the getter, plus:

```java
private final Element jack = createJackInABox();

public abstract Element createJackInABox();
```

So if the matching creator is in the same class, the creator would be non-abstract, but if the creator were missing, then the abstract creator would be declared and called. Or in other words: If a matching method is present, nothing needs to be done, but if the method does not exist, the abstract method is implicit.

