**Describe the bug**
Using the delombok tool produces duplicate @NonNull annotations, causing a compile error.

**To Reproduce**

1. Wrote a class annotated with @EqualsAndHashCode(callSuper = true) and @Data 
2. Wrote fields, some of which are @NonNull
3. Selected Refactor > Delombok > All annotations in IntelliJ
4. Setters and getters for @NonNull fields contain duplicate annotations

```java
    @NonNull
    public @NonNull String getName() {
        return this.name;
    }
```

```java
    public void setName(@NonNull @NonNull String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
```

**Version info (please complete the following information):**
 - Lombok version: 1.18
 - Javac version: 1.8.0_202
- IntelliJ version: Ultimate 2019.3.3

Lombok already allows configuring type of the null-check it generates by setting global property:
`lombok.nonNull.exceptionType = [NullPointerException | IllegalArgumentException | JDK | Guava | Assertion] (default: NullPointerException)`

**Feature Description**
Allow overriding this property inline by providing an optional property in annotation `@NonNull`. For example:
```java
@NonNull(exceptionType = "JDK")
```
will generate `Objects.requireNonNull(...)` code despite what is set globally.

**Goal**
In some cases, it makes sense to specify `@NonNull` annotation to improve public API, but the null-check is meaningless and it affects code coverage.

Consider `lombok.nonNull.exceptionType = NullPointerException` and the following code:
```java
class Parent {
  private final String name;
  Parent(@NonNull String name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
}

class Child extends Parent {
  Child(@NonNull String name) {
    super(name);
  }
}
```

Compiled code looks like this:
```java
class Parent {
  private final String name;
  Parent(@NonNull String name) {
    if (name == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("name is marked non-null but is null");
    } else {
      this.name = name;
    }
  }
}

class Child extends Parent {
  Child(@NonNull String name) {
    super(name);
    if (name == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("name is marked non-null but is null");
    }
  }
}
```

When used with code coverage tools, it is impossible to cover line `throw new NullPointerException("name is marked non-null but is null");` in class `Child`.

But a configurable `@NonNull` annotation would solve this problem.

**Additional Notes**
It is useful to have `lombok.nonNull.exceptionType = NullPointerException` because code coverage tools can catch missing tests for null arguments. If `exceptionType` is changed to `JDK`, then the above mentioned issue disappears, but this can lead to missing tests as well.

Maybe there's a more elegant way of improving this.
For example, Lombok could figure out that parent constructor already performs the same check and there's no need to generate it in the child class. Though this seems quite complicated to  implement.

