**Describe the feature**

Given the following class:
```
@Builder
public class FooData {
	@Singular
	@Getter
	private final List<Object> elements;
}
```
lombok will generate the following code:
```
		public FooData build() {
			List<Object> elements;
			switch (this.elements == null ? 0 : this.elements.size()) {
				case 0:
					elements = java.util.Collections.emptyList();
					break;
				case 1:
					elements = java.util.Collections.singletonList(this.elements.get(0));
					break;
				default:
					elements = java.util.Collections.unmodifiableList(new ArrayList<Object>(this.elements));
			}

			return new FooData(elements);
		}
```
This may change the implementation of the list used by the property compared to the one that was actually passed to the builder and may cause surprising behaviour. For example consider this snippet:
```
List<Object> someElements = //...
FooData foo = FooData.builder().elements(someElements).build();
// later on:
foo.getElements().add("some other element");
```
Depending on the initial size of `someElements` this code will throw a `UnsupportedOperationException` if the size was at most 1, otherwise it will just work as expected. 

Also depending on the use case replacing a list implementation may also have an influence on application runtime and performance.

Please add a note to the lombok documentation page of `@Singular` about this behaviour.

**Describe the target audience**

Developers using lombok with `@Singular` lists might become easier aware of the side effects this will have.

**Describe the feature**

I think that two main reasons for using builder is to enhance readability (especially when user wants to construct object that has few fields of same type), and when there is large object, possibly with default values (e.g. configuration classes builders), and user wants to tweak only few options.

In first case, builder does increase the readability of code, but opens possibility that field might get not initialized by accident, or after adding to class new field.

My proposition is to add possibility to generate builder which requires on type level that all fields are initialized. Below, I present an example how it could be done.

```java
@Getter
@Setter
@NoArgsConstructor
public class Point {
  private double x;
  private double y;

  public static PointBuilderX builder() {
    return new PointBuilder();
  }

  interface PointBuilderX {
    PointBuilderY x(double x);
  }

  interface PointBuilderY {
    PointBuilderBuild y(double y);
  }

  interface PointBuilderBuild {
    Point build();
  }
}
```

```java
public class PointBuilder implements PointBuilderX, PointBuilderY, PointBuilderBuild {
  private final Point object;
  public PointBuilder() {
    object = new Point();
  }

  @Override
  public PointBuilderY x(double x) {
    object.setX(x);
    return this;
  }

  @Override
  public PointBuilderBuild y(double y) {
    object.setY(y);
    return this;
  }

  @Override
  public Point build() {
    return object;
  }
}
```

Conceptually, builder implements interfaces that restrict which methods can be called on it. For example calling `Point.builder().y(1.0).build()` is not possible, since `build()` methods returns interface that only allows calling `x` method.

After calling `Point.builder()` only possibility is to call `x` method, then `y`, and then `build`. This way object will always have all fields initialized by the user. If afterwards `z` field is added, code will stop to compile.

This could be extended to default arguments. If I had `@Builder.Default` on `x` field, generated `PointBuilderX` should allow user to either call `x` or `y` method, and if latter one was called, then `x` of course should have been initialized with default value.

**Describe the target audience**
Everyone who wants to initialize all fields in the object, and have guarantee that builder invocation will always meet that requirement.

