`lombok.eclipse.handlers.HandleFieldNameConstants`, in `createInnerTypeFieldNameConstants(..)`, [injects the class initializer method (<clinit>)](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/blob/7dfbe4323c15cbd88983380b27a250d2a381d148/src/core/lombok/eclipse/handlers/HandleFieldNameConstants.java#L176), [generates the method's body](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/blob/7dfbe4323c15cbd88983380b27a250d2a381d148/src/core/lombok/eclipse/handlers/HandleFieldNameConstants.java#L177), and only then [injects the field members](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/blob/7dfbe4323c15cbd88983380b27a250d2a381d148/src/core/lombok/eclipse/handlers/HandleFieldNameConstants.java#L180). The generated class intializer method body contains the code to initialize and populate the synthetic enum values array - but since no field members have been injected at the time it is generated, the array is initialized to be of size 0/empty.

**To Reproduce**
```
@FieldNameConstants(asEnum = true)
public class Example {

    String aField;


    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(Example.Fields.values().length);
    }
}
```

Compiled under Eclipse, this application prints out "0" instead of "1".

**Expected behavior**
The class initializer method's body should only be generated after the field members have been injected.

**Version info:**
 - Lombok version: 1.18.12
 - Platform: Eclipse 2020-06 (4.16.0) - Lombok v1.18.12

**Feature description**
Suppose I have the following classes:

```java
@Getter
@Builder
public class User {
    private final String username;
    private final String email;
    private final List<Phone> phones;
    private final String status;
}

@Getter
@Builder
public class Phone {
    private final PhoneType type;
    private final String countryCode;
    private final String areaCode;
    private final String number;
}

public enum PhoneType {
    HOME,
    MOBILE,
    WORK,
    OTHER,
    ;
}
```

It would be great If I could do the following:

```java
User.builder()
    .username("JoeDoe")
    .email("joe.doe@gmail.com")
    .phone() // This returns a Phone builder
        .type(PhoneType.HOME)
        .countryCode("+54")
        .areaCode("11")
        .number("1234-5678")
        .continueUser() // This returns the previous user builder (the Phone builder must hold a reference to it)
    .phone() // This returns a new Phone builder
        .type(PhoneType.OTHER)
        .countryCode("+598")
        .areaCode("42")
        .number("123-4567")
        .continueUser() // Again, this returns the user builder
    .status("SomeStatus")
    .build();
```

The annotation to allow this could be `@Builder.Composition`, or something of the sort. Must be placed in the field you want to allow this. Obviously, the sub-builder (in the example, this is the `Phone.PhoneBuilder`) class must allow holding an "owner" reference. Maybe this could be achieved by subclassing the sub-builder for each use of `@Builder.Composition`, adding in the subclass the owner's field. Or maybe allowing any builder hold an owners reference (with generics).

Note that in the given example, to get the `Phone` builder, I use the `Phone.PhoneBuilder#phone()` method (same name but different arguments as the generated by the `@Singular` annotation). So there should not be any naming conflicts.

I think this allows not explicitly creating a builder each time you want to add an element to the phones list. The method chain is cleaner.

**Target audience**
Any java programmer

