**Describe the feature**
A new `@NoConstructor` annotation that allows for the creation of what I'll be calling here a "disallowed constructor" - basically a constructor that throws an `UnsupportedOperationException` when it is called. This is great for utility classes to prevent instantiation, and all you have to do is use an annotation instead of having to copy-paste it out. The `@NoConstructor` annotation will optionally take a string message. 

<details>
  <summary>Potential implementation of the interface</summary>

```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.SOURCE)
public @interface NoConstructor {
	/**
	 * If set, this message will be passed to the created {@code RuntimeException}.
	 * Otherwise (or if null), the exception will have no message.
	 * 
	 * @return Message for the generated exception, or blank/null for none.
	 */
	String value() default null;
	
	/**
	 * Any annotations listed here are put on the generated constructor.
	 * The syntax for this feature depends on JDK version (nothing we can do about that; it's to work around javac bugs).<br>
	 * up to JDK7:<br>
	 *  {@code @NoArgsConstructor(onConstructor=@__({@AnnotationsGoHere}))}<br>
	 * from JDK8:<br>
	 *  {@code @NoArgsConstructor(onConstructor_={@AnnotationsGohere})} // note the underscore after {@code onConstructor}.
	 * 
	 * @return List of annotations to apply to the generated constructor.
	 */
	AnyAnnotation[] onConstructor() default {};
	
	/**
	  * Placeholder annotation to enable the placement of annotations on the generated code.
	  * @deprecated Don't use this annotation, ever - Read the documentation.
	  */
	@Deprecated
	@Retention(RetentionPolicy.SOURCE)
	@Target({})
	@interface AnyAnnotation {}
}

```
</details>

