However if a copy constructor annotation is supported (which would require any parent class to have a copy constructor or fail due to compiler error) then we can switch the developers code to the following `new B(new A(1), 2)` it is more ugly, but it would work.  And yes if you had another class called `C` with similar pattern you would end up with `new C(new B(new A(1), 2), 3)` uhg, but it works.  

This would need to be an optional addition on the subclass.  Something like:
```
@Data
@AllArgsConstructor(callCopyCon=true)
class B extends A
   int b; 
}
```

Which would then generate code that looked something like
```
B(A a, b) {
super(a);
this.b = b
}
```

As mentioned before at this point you have a compiler error (as designed), so A would need to be updated to have a CopyConstructor

```
@Data
@CopyConstructor
class A
  int a;
}
```

This would need to generate a constructor like the following
```
A(A copyFrom) {
this.a = copyFrom.A
}
```

You can likewise require the parent class to either be Object, or have its own CopyConstructor (failing to compiler error as designed).  For example adding the @CopyConstructor to the B class would generate the following constructor in B
```
B(B copyFrom) {
super(copyFrom);
this.b = copyFrom.b;
```

Of course you have the readily available alternative of @SuperBuilder.

