**Describe the feature**

Defensively copying collections is a good practice in many contexts, including lombok `@Value` classes. Lombok's annotations that generate constructors, getters, or setters could be extended to defensively copy members of `List`, `Set`, or `Map` type.

*Example*

```java
@Getter(copy = Copy.IMMUTABLE)
@Setter(copy = Copy.MUTABLE)
@AllArgsConstructor(copy = Copy.MUTABLE)
public class Student {

    private String name;
    private List<Course> schedule;

}
```

*Generated*

```java
public class Student {

    private String name;
    private List<Course> schedule;

    public Student(String name, List<Course> schedule) {
        this.name = name;
        this.schedule = new ArrayList<>(schedule);
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public List<Course> getSchedule() {
        return List.copyOf(schedule);
    }

    public void setSchedule(List<Course> schedule) {
        this.schedule = new ArrayList<>(schedule);
    }

}
```

**Describe the target audience**

This feature should be generally useful to any developers who tend to use defensive copies. It is particularly useful alongside `@Value`, since value classes are intended to be immutable, but this requires manual effort when the class contains collections.

**Additional context**

This `copy` parameter would be available on `@Getter`, `@Setter`, `@AllArgsConstructor`, `@RequiredArgsConstructor`, and `@Value` (which applies it to the constructor). It has four options:

```java
public enum Copy {

    // No defensive copy
    NONE,

    // Uses the ArrayList, HashSet, or HashMap constructor
    MUTABLE,

    // Uses List#copyOf, Set#copyOf, or Map#copyOf
    IMMUTABLE,

    // Uses Collections#unmodifiableList and friends
    UNMODIFIABLE,

}
```

These defensive copies look specifically for fields whose raw type is `List`, `Set`, or `Map`. If `copy` is explicitly set on a `@Getter` or `@Setter` annotation for a field whose raw type is none of those, lombok fails with a compile-time error.

To preserve backwards compatibility, each annotation's version of the `copy` parameter defaults to `Copy.NONE`, unless overridden in `lombok.config`.

