> But if @Builder is placed on the class and the user is actually creating a method that conflicts with what what @Builder itself is trying to create, then it's appropriate to enforce that parameter names match. 

It's tricky here; if you put Builder on a method, then the names do matter: It's the only thing lombok gets in order to name the 'setter' methods of the builder; as `@Builder` on a class is just syntax sugar for putting it on the default AllArgsConstructor, which is normally derived from fieldnames, all is well. Toss in a handwritten constructor instead, and lombok will silently use the fields in order to figure out what to do, but then use the constructor you wrote to actually instantiate the object.

This change would be backwards incompatible. But, reconsidering the fact that builder elevates param names by its very nature, and that a mismatch between your fields and an explicitly written constructor effectively dooms you anyway... maybe?

It gets real complicated though. Imagine you have this:

```
@Builder
class Example {
    String a, b;

    Example(Object a, String b) { ... }
}
```

This currently works fine with builder - builder-on-a-class passes each and every field, in that order, and typed the same way the field is, to the constructor, without checking if it even exists. It does in this snippet, even though the types don't match.

Should lombok warn about the above? If we go with a warning, what's the solution to get rid of the warning? I guess the only way out is to go ahead and rename your parameter, because I rather doubt `@Builder(allowWonkyNames = true)` is a good idea, and we can't add 'keys' to `@SuppressWarnings` easily or even piggy-back on existing ones.

What about:

```
    @Builder class Example {
        String a, b;
        Example(String... a) {}
    }
```

should we warn there?

