1) Equals and canEquals are using **instanceof** to check argument type, when you extends a class in JPA it is not ok, because different entities with same super class return true if EqualsAndHashCode is used only on super class.
2) When using cached hashCode give the option to use it on equals too. in some situations it can improve performance.

### With Lombok
```java
@EqualsAndHashCode(cacheStrategy = LAZY_EQUALS, instanceOfStrategy = IS_INSTANCE)
public class Foo {
    private HeavyHashCodeObject heavy;
}
```

### Vanila Java
```java
public final class Foo {
    private transient int $hashCodeCache = 0;

    private final HeavyHashCodeObject heavy;

    public Foo(HeavyHashCodeObject heavy) {
        this.heavy = heavy;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!getClass().isInstance(o)) return false;
        return hashCode() == o.hashCode();
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        if ($hashCodeCache != 0) return $hashCodeCache;

        int result = 0;
        result = 31 * result + Objects.hashCode(heavy);

        $hashCodeCache = result;
        return result;
    }
}
```

