The Builder Singular annotation generates two "adder" methods and a "clear" method to manipulate the annotated collection. However, there is a case with the **toBuilder** parameter where the developer may want to remove an entry from an existing collection without having to clear it completely. A "remove" method that can call the underlying collection/map's "remove" method would make this feature more useful for cases in which we want to create a new instance of an immutable object with changes that involve removing entries from the affected collections.

Consider the following scenario:

```
@Builder(toBuilder = true)
public class MyClass {
    String id;
    @Singular Map<String, Integer> items;
}

public void doStuff() {
    MyClass foo = MyClass.builder()
        .id("foo")
        .item("x", 1)
        .item("y", 2)
        .build();

    // I now want to remove "x" and add "z".
    MyClass bar = foo.toBuilder()
         .removeItem("x")   // This method does not exist.
         .item("z", 3)
         .build();   // bar should now contain "y" and "z" only.
}
```

To do this currently, I either need to remove the Singular annotation and just replace or modify the map directly, or I have to make a mutable copy of the original object's map, make whatever changes I need to that, clear the builder's map using the "clearItems" method, then use "items" to copy my modified map's contents back into the builder. Both of these strategies could be avoided if Singular had a "remove" method.

The target audience for this feature request is any developer that writes or maintains code that can add, modify and delete entries from collections or maps, who wishes to keep those collections immutable outside of change contexts, and who wants to minimize allocations for extra copies of the affected collections. This is especially important for live services that have to be able to process thousands of requests per second with these kinds of operations.

