Without even getting into issues related to inheritance, it's easy to see why this cannot be done safely. Collections and other mutable objects are an example. See the two examples below where `@Value` will make all fields final, but this is not enough to really enforce the immutability of the object:

```java
@Value
public class Foo {
    List<String> stringList;
}
```

```java
// This is mutable
@Data
public final class Bar {
    private String aString;
    private int aNumber;
}

@Value
public class Foo {
    Bar aBar;
}
```

As you can see, neither of the 'Foo' classes annotated with `@Value` are immutable. If you mutate the list for the first one and the `Bar` object for the second one it'll also mutate the `Foo` one.

