**Describe the feature**
Getter and Setter methods created by lombok are not final by deafult, and it suits most of the time, but there are cases when we want the methods become final so those who extend the class can not override them.

**Lombok**
```
public class MyClass {
  @Getter
  @Setter
  private String name;

  @Getter(makeFinal = true)
  @Setter(makeFinal = true)
  private String code;
}
```

**Vanilla Java**
```
public class MyClass {
  private String name;
  private String code;

  public String getName() { return name; }
  public final void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }

  public final String getCode() { return code; }
  public final void setCode(String code) { this.code = code; }
}

```

As you can see the change is obvious, and for not breaking backward compatibility, the default value of annotation argument must be false. 

**makeFinal** was the name came to my mind (as final itself is reserved word and cannot be used), better name is appreciated.

I have created a minimal example in Eclipse 2020-12 with Lombok enabled:

```java
package lomboktest;

import lombok.Getter;

public class OnSetter {
	@Deprecated
	@Getter(onMethod_ = @Deprecated)
	private boolean dirty;
}
```

And it gives me this error:
![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/10684/108684828-fa638d00-7525-11eb-8885-3c98ee811a64.png)

~~Eclipse 2020-03 works fine, so I'll roll back to it for now.~~ It seems that issues with Lombok began with Eclipse 2020-06 onwards.

This occurs with both JDK 8 and JDK 11 selected as the project JDK. Eclipse itself runs under JDK 11.

**Description**
At the moment you can use a delegate like this

```java
interface CollectionAdders {
    boolean add(Object e);
    boolean addAll(Collection<? extends Object> c);
}
class A {
	@Delegate{types=CollectionAdders.class)
	Set<Object> objects;
}
```

This is nice and all, but if you have 2 `Set`s in class A, then you can't use it for both.

That is why I propose adding a suffix parameter to the `@Delegate` annotation (and optionally add a `@Plural` annotation for cases where this suffix needs to be pluralized).

```java
interface Adders {
    boolean add(Object e);

	@Plural
    boolean addAll(Collection<? extends Object> c);
}
class A {
	@Delegate{types=Adders.class, suffix="cat")
	Set<Object> cats;

	@Delegate{types=Adders.class, suffix="dog")
	Set<Object> dogs;
}
```

Or (without `@Plural`):

```java
interface Adder {
    boolean add(Object e);
}
interface AllAdder {
    boolean addAll(Collection<? extends Object> c);
}
class A {
	@Delegate{types=Adder.class, suffix="cat")
	@Delegate{types=AllAdder.class, suffix="cats")
	Set<Object> cats;

	@Delegate{types=Adder.class, suffix="dog")
	@Delegate{types=AllAdder.class, suffix="dogs")
	Set<Object> dogs;
}
```

This would generate the `A.addCat(...)`, `A.addDog(...)`, `A.addAllCats(...)` and `A.addAllDogs(...)`

**Describe the target audience**
While I am using a Set as an example, this could be widely applicable for delegating methods from more than one field. The example of a `Set` was purely used to also explain the `@Plural` concept.

**Additional context**
To implement the `@Plural` functionality, it might be possible to reuse parts of the code that is used for creating a singular form for the `@Signular` annotation.

This suffix feature has been mentioned by me before in #1905, but I felt that this feature was more generally applicable and deserved a feature request of its own.

**Describe the feature**
Right now when you use `@Delegate` without parameters, it works for generic types, like `Set<E>`, but if you want to specify the types parameter on `@Delegate`, the methods in this type don't support generics. Example:

```java
interface CollectionAdders<E> {
    boolean add(E e);
    boolean addAll(Collection<? extends E> c);
}

class Application {
    @Delegate(types = { CollectionAdders.class })
    private Set<User> users = new HashSet<>();
}
```
This throws the following error: 
```
java: incompatible types: java.lang.Object cannot be converted to some.package.domain.model.User
```
I have previously thought of how to fix this, but my previous suggestion [here](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/issues/1905#issuecomment-565736057) doesn't seem satisfactory. 

It would be nicest to keep the interface as defined above. That, together with seeing how `@JoinTable`s are implemented in JPA, gave me the following idea:


```java
interface CollectionAdders<E> {
    boolean add(E e);
    boolean addAll(Collection<? extends E> c);
}

class Application {
    @Delegate(genericTypes = { @GenericType(
			type = CollectionAdders.class, 
			parameters = { User.class }
	) })
    private Set<User> users = new HashSet<>();
}
```

This way you have all the type information that is needed, without the problem of type erasure.

Here is another example, with multiple generic parameters: 
```java
interface MapPutters<K, V> {
    V put(K key, V value);
    void putAll(Map<? extends K, ? extends V> m);
}

class Application {
    @Delegate(genericTypes = { @GenericType(
			type = MapPutters.class, 
			parameters = { Integer.class, User.class }
	) })
    private Map<Integer, User> userMap = new HashMap<>();
}
```

**Describe the target audience**
Everyone who already wants to use `@Delegate` right now, but who is unable to do so without copying the interface for every concrete usecase of a generic type.

For example: right now you'd have to make one interface for every concrete use, like `Collection<String>`, `Collection<Integer>`, `Collection<User>`, etc, whereas after implementing this feature you can make one reusable interface for all `Collection<E>` uses.

**Additional context**
If this is implemented, together with #2763, you can finally silence everyone who is asking for the `@Adder` annotation, because `@Delegate` will be able to provide what you would have wanted out of the `@Adder` annotation, without being `Collection` specific.

