**Describe the bug**

Getters/setters should not be generated for `Optional` fields.

**To reproduce**

```java
@Getter
@Setter
private Optional<String> foo;
```

**Expected behavior**

```java
private Optional<String> foo;
```

**Actual behavior**

```java
private Optional<String> foo;

public Optional<String> getFoo() {
    return foo;
}

public void setFoo(Optional<String> foo) {
    this.foo = foo;
}
```

**Version info**

- Lombok version 1.18.11 (or any)
- Java 11 (or any)

**Additional context**

Project Lombok has taken [a strong stance](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/wiki/Language-Design:-Null-vs.-Optional#lomboks-position-on-null-and-optional) against helping developers who use `Optional` as intended—as a return type. Its most popular feature request (a `@Getter` variation that wraps nullable fields in `Optional`) has been rejected repeatedly: #1214, #1957, #2264.

For consistency with its own position, Project Lombok shouldn’t help developers who use `Optional` as a field type. That pattern is [discouraged](https://stackoverflow.com/a/26328555/140743) even by Brian Goetz, who helped add `Optional` to Java:

> You should almost never use it as a field of something or a method parameter.

If Project Lombok is trying to help the Java community by discouraging the use of `Optional`, it should be consistent in that policy. Its current behavior discourages _intended_ uses of `Optional` and promotes _discouraged_ uses of `Optional` as a workaround. This behavior is a disservice to the Java community.

