**Describe the feature**
Improve the performance of apps using `@Value` annotated objects (https://projectlombok.org/features/Value) and `hashCode` intensively (e.g. by using `HashMap`'s). Since the fields of those objects never change, we can precompute/cache the `hashCode` value, as it will also never change (due to immutability). Hence Lombok can precompute that value when creating the immutable object, and always return that value when `hashCode` is called. Or we can lazily compute that value and cache it so that we can return that in future calls.

With Lombok
```
@Value public class ValueExample {
 String name;
}
```

Vanilla Java (precompute when instantiating object)
```
public final class ValueExample {
  private final String name;
  private final int $hashCode;
  
  @java.beans.ConstructorProperties({"name"})
  public ValueExample(String name) {
    this.name = name;

    this.$hashCode = $hashCode();
  }
  
  public String getName() {
    return this.name;
  }

  private int $hashCode() {
    final int $PRIME = 59;
    int $result = 1;
    final Object $name = this.getName();
    $result = $result * $PRIME + ($name == null ? 43 : $name.hashCode());
    return $result;
  }
  
  @java.lang.Override
  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (o == this) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof ValueExample)) return false;
    final ValueExample other = (ValueExample)o;
    final Object this$name = this.getName();
    final Object other$name = other.getName();
    if (this$name == null ? other$name != null : !this$name.equals(other$name)) return false;
    return true;
  }
  
  @java.lang.Override
  public int hashCode() {
    return $hashCode;
  }
  
  @java.lang.Override
  public String toString() {
    return "ValueExample(name=" + getName() + ")";
  }
}
```

Vanilla Java (lazy computing and caching)
```
public final class ValueExample {
  private final String name;
  private Integer $hashCode;
  
  @java.beans.ConstructorProperties({"name"})
  public ValueExample(String name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
  
  public String getName() {
    return this.name;
  }

  private int $hashCode() {
    final int $PRIME = 59;
    int $result = 1;
    final Object $name = this.getName();
    $result = $result * $PRIME + ($name == null ? 43 : $name.hashCode());
    return $result;
  }
  
  @java.lang.Override
  public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (o == this) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof ValueExample)) return false;
    final ValueExample other = (ValueExample)o;
    final Object this$name = this.getName();
    final Object other$name = other.getName();
    if (this$name == null ? other$name != null : !this$name.equals(other$name)) return false;
    return true;
  }
  
  @java.lang.Override
  public int hashCode() {
    // the result will be the same even if it's called from several threads at the same time when $hashCode is null
    if ($hashCode == null) {
      $hashCode = $hashCode();
    }

    return $hashCode;
  }
  
  @java.lang.Override
  public String toString() {
    return "ValueExample(name=" + getName() + ")";
  }
}
```

**Describe the target audience**
Any app with `@Value` annotated objects and using `hashCode` intensively.

**Additional context**
In our use case, we have a CPU intensive app that is heavily using hash maps. By using a profiler, we saw that calling `hashCode` in big objects (which were immutable) was taking a considerable amount of time, so we had to do this performance tuning by ourselves. It's is not a big improvement in case of small objects, but we noticed a big difference when using relatively big objects as key in the hash maps.

**Before submitting**
Please check our [wiki](https://github.com/rzwitserloot/lombok/wiki) because some feature requests are asked _a lot_ and there are good reasons (as explained on the wiki) why we can't (yet) implement them or why we won't implement them.

