What about something similar to `Builder.Default`?

```java
@RequiredArgsConstructor(staticName = "create")
private static class ConstructorDefaults {
    @RequiredArgsConstructor.Default
    private final String value = "default-value";
}

```

Generated code:

```java
private static class ConstructorDefaults {
    private final String value;

    public ConstructorDefaults() {
        this("default-value");
    }

    public ConstructorDefaults(final String value) {
        this.value = value;
    }
    
    public static ConstructorDefaults create() {
        return new ConstructorDefaults();
    }
    
    public static ConstructorDefaults create(final String value) {
        return new ConstructorDefaults(value);
    }
}
```

I think this would be awesome! I definitely find myself having to write multiple constructors and static constructors because I want to have defaults. Problem with `Builder` is that it is not clear what is defaultable versus required when building the object.

Edit: One caveat that comes to mind is when you have multiple default fields of the same type. We could not create a single-param default constructor for just either or of these two defaultable fields because we cannot differentiate on type. In any case, even creating one constructor that omits all default fields and another that has all fields would still be quite useful.

This would work well with `@With` - folks that want to create a default object and override only the fields they care about could use the fully-defaulted constructor then `.withX` whatever they need to override.

We've discussed putting quotes around strings. We might do that. Or allow users to configure doing that. 

But the same problem already exists in standard libraries.
```java
public class ToStringExample {
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		System.out.println(Arrays.toString(new String[] {null}));
		System.out.println(Arrays.asList(new String[] {null}));
		
		System.out.println(Arrays.toString(new String[] {"null"}));
		System.out.println(Arrays.asList(new String[] {"null"}));
	}
}
```

Output:
```cmd
[null]
[null]
[null]
[null]
```

Adding quotes in lombok generated toString methods would not fix that problem. Also, in practice it has never been that much of a problem for me.

Adding quotes to strings or having alternative rendering for null values increases the maintenance load, lombok learning curve or both. It might/would also affect existing programs or cause a split in the output.

So I am currently leaning against implementing improved null string rendering.

