Consider this code
```java
@EqualsAndHashCode
class Foo {
  long value;
}
```
After compilation this turns into
```java
class Foo {
    long value;

    public boolean equals(final Object o) {
      //...
    }

    public int hashCode() {
        int PRIME = true;
        int result = 1;
        long $value = this.value;
        int resultx = result * 59 + (int)($value >>> 32 ^ $value);
        return resultx;
    }
}
```
Here instead of generating the code manually Lombok should just delegate to `Long.hashCode(long)`. Same is applicable for some other primitives, e.g. `boolean`, `float`, `double`. This allows both code reuse and JDK-compatible behaviour.

Currently, when using `@Builder`, values are assigned directly to fields without going through setters.

Consider the following example:

```java
@Builder
public static class Foo {
    private Integer value;
    private Boolean even;

    public void setValue(Integer value) {
        this.value = value;
        this.even = (value & 1) == 0;
    }
}
```

And here are my expectation:
```java
@Test
public void testBuilderCallToSetter() {
    Foo result = Foo.builder().value(4).build();
    Assertions.assertTrue(result.even);
}
```

I think we can add a property to `@Builder` to indicate which fields are need to call setter. Or create a new annotation to annotate on the setter function.

Thanks.

