Keep in mind that `@NonNull` doesn't guarantee the nullness of a field, only that a null check will be performed when attempting to set the value of that field.

Consider:

```java
import lombok.Getter;
import lombok.NonNull;
import lombok.Setter;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertNotNull;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertNull;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows;

public class NonNullTest {
    private static class TestFixture {
        @Getter
        @Setter
        @NonNull
        private String value;
    }
    @Test
    public void can_NonNull_be_null() {
        final TestFixture subject = new TestFixture();
        assertNull(subject.getValue());
        subject.setValue("some-value");
        assertNotNull(subject.getValue());
        assertThrows(NullPointerException.class, () -> subject.setValue(null));
   }
}

```

Hum, I see what you mean.
Given that Lombok is not creating "frozen" classes but reducing the boilerplate of Java code, one cannot control how attributes are used.
Even with a `@Data`, it is not possible to be sure that the field is not set to null somehow.

Looking at the decompiled source, I see the annotation removing all `@SuppressWarnings("all")`, that must help with some warnings from javac, but not for external tools.
Reasonably, it is not possible to include annotations for _every health code checker_. Would it be possible to introduce an `onMethod` field for the `EqualsAndHashCode` annotation, similar to what is done for `@Getter`?
