&lt;ranty context&gt;
That's a common shading strategy and yet also seems befuddlingly stupid. Why in the blazes would you want the exact same class file (byte for byte equivalent) to exist in 85 differently named but otherwise identical flavours? That wastes memory, interop ability, load times, and build times. It's also utterly unneccessary; "we" (developers) invented hashes, what, 50 years ago? Shading tools should mangle the names by including versions or hashes, not by project name. If a combination of build tools and JVM can't handle this, I think you should report bugs with them, then. We're developers and use source control like git. Concepts like 'the hash identifies the content' should not be rocket science. It's disconcerting that a process commonly engaged in by a great many java developers evidently was set up and written by folks who have no idea. It's further disconcerting that the java module system yet again fails to take the realities of how java projects are set up in account, but it sort of got lucky in that a commonly engaged in bit of lunacy (project-based name-mangling in shaders) is hiding this oversight. And all this weirdness now lands on our plate as a 'bug'. Oof.
&lt;/ranty context&gt;

&lt;explanatory context&gt;
Lombok's shader works differently from both what common shaders do (the dumb thing; stripe in the project's name), and what they ought to do (the version). Instead, lombok modifies _the extension_ (check it out; run `jar tvf` on lombok.jar and you'll notice that almost all class files contained inside do not end in `.class`), because lombok, intentionally, wants to be invisible. At any time and in any phase of the process. Lombok's deps are for lombok only. Lombok is running as agent or AP and in that sense is 'outside the universe', or should be. That's why we've set it up that way. We do not want our ASM to ever show up in your auto-complete dialogs. As such, we're already shaded, at least in terms of file names.
&lt;/explanatory context&gt;

The immediate problem, then, cannot possibly be explained by how the files are stored in the jar. They are stored in a way that could not possibly cause this problem.

However, we do _load_ them. This presumably is the source of the issue. At the time of invoking `.defineClass`, passing it the bytes we loaded from the 'class file from the jar but it has a different extension'. However, the solution to isolation at a class loader level is customized loaders. Not name mangling.

Given that name mangling is self-evidently silly, let's follow the crowd off of that particular cliff. That implies that lombok's ASM is loaded in a context where it could in theory affect other code and we don't want that to happen. The proper solution, which also isolates us far better than name mangling can, is class loaders, or possibly detect that we're in the Java Module System loading architecture and add a layer.

Step 1 is a proper reproduction and preferably a test case. Once that's there, the fix probably involves lombok making a classloader first, and then loading at least ASM, and possibly itself, within that. We already do this when running as an agent in eclipse, but I'm pretty sure we don't do this when running as AP in javac and javac-derived tooling.

