**Additional context**
Skipping through lombok source I found this: https://github.com/projectlombok/lombok/blob/master/src/core/lombok/core/handlers/HandlerUtil.java#L445 It might be enough to add it there?
(Reached via https://github.com/projectlombok/lombok/blob/5120abe4741c78d19d7e65404f407cfe57074a47/src/core/lombok/javac/handlers/JavacHandlerUtil.java#L1570 and https://github.com/projectlombok/lombok/blob/5120abe4741c78d19d7e65404f407cfe57074a47/src/core/lombok/javac/handlers/HandleBuilder.java#L257)

Thank you!

**Describe the feature**

Lombok

```
@SneakyThrows(exceptionClass=SQLException.class, runtimeExceptionClass=PersistenceException.class)
@SneakyThrows(exceptionClass=IOException.class, runtimeExceptionClass=UncheckedException.class)
foo() {
    ... something that throws SQLException }
}
```

delombok

```
foo() {
  try {
    ... something that throws SQLException and IOException
  } catch (SQLException e) {
    throw new PersistenceException(e);
  } catch (IOException e) {
    throw new UncheckedIOException(e);
  }
}
```

**Describe the target audience**

Those that deal with low level JDBC that don't want to deal with SQLException, BUT want to know that it is a persistence error rather than unwrapping.  Same with those using IO.


**Additional context**
I should be allowed to handle multiple BUT for older JDKs that don't support repeated ones it can be
```
@SneakyThrows(mappings={
  @SneakilyThrow(exceptionClass=SQLException.class, runtimeExceptionClass=PersistenceException.class)
  @SneakilyThrow(exceptionClass=IOException.class, runtimeExceptionClass=UncheckedException.class)
})
```

That being the case we can add `@SneakilyThrow` as a new annotation instead.

**Describe the feature**
My idea would be to add an annotation `@SuperConstructor` which, on classes, creates a constructor that is equal to and calls the super-constructor.

Lomboked:
```
public class LombokIdeaSuperConstructor{

    @RequiredArgsConstructor
    public class FirstClass {

        private final String string;
        
    }

    @SuperConstructor
    public class SecondClass extends FirstClass {
        
    }

}
```

Plain Java:
```
public class LombokIdeaSuperConstructor{
    
    public class FirstClass {
        
        private final String string;

        public FirstClass(String string) {
            this.string = string;
        }
    }
    
    public class SecondClass extends FirstClass {

        public SecondClass(String string) {
            super(string);
        }
        
    }
    
}
```

**Describe the target audience**
Any developer working with class hierarchies would benefit from this project.

**Describe the feature**
Add a possibility to the annotations @Setter, @NoArgsConstructor, @RequiredArgsConstructor und @AllArgsConstructor to call a method after the code to e.g. B. to recalculate other variables.

Lomboked:
```
@RequiredArgsConstructor(afterMethod = "calculateB")
public class AferMethod {
    
    @Setter(afterMethod = "calculateB")
    private String a;
    private String b;
    
    public void calculateB() {
        this.b = this.a.replace("A", "C");
    }
    
}
```

Plain Java:
```
public class AferMethod {

    private String a;
    private String b;

    public Lombok(String a) {
        this.a = a;
        this.calculateB();
    }

    public void setA(String a) {
        this.a = a;
        this.calculateB();
    }

    public void calculateB() {
        this.b = this.a.replace("A", "C");
    }

}
```

**Describe the target audience**
It often happens to me that I would need such a feature and then cannot use Lombok because the method is not called. I think it will be the same for many other developers.

