Currently it is required to manually define that you want to use another builder for creating a child object that is also annotated with a builder. It would be easier for usage to have this done inside the builder itself.
### classes
```java
@Builder
class A {
    @Getter
    B b;
}
@Builder
class B{
    @Getter
    String field;
}
```

### current usage
```java
A.builder()
  .b(B.builder()
      .field("Hello world")
      .build())
  .build();
```

### suggested usage
```java
A.builder()
  .b()
      .field("Hello world")
      .end()
  .build();
```

### sidenote
Another advantage would be that you can use suppliers to separate the knowledge of how the builder is retrieved while still being able to separate the building of A from B. In production you could use `a::b` while in testing you could use `b::builder`.

Edit: updated so not reference `@SuperBuilder` or not having `@Setter` annotations

**Describe the feature**
[As discussed here](https://github.com/projectlombok/lombok/issues/746#issuecomment-1143599637), it would be nice to either have a new command `valToVar` or mode of `delombok` to replace existing usage of `lombok.val` with `final var` in the source code. This is intended to move away from `lombok.val` specifically, leaving all other code, comments and lombok features intact.

```diff
public String example() {
-   val example = new ArrayList<String>();
+   final var example = new ArrayList<String>();
    example.add("Hello, World!");
-   val foo = example.get(0);
+   final var foo = example.get(0);
    return foo.toLowerCase();
}
```

**Describe the target audience**
Anyone using Java 10/11+, and looking to move away from `lombok.val` specifically, towards `final var`, while leaving all other usage of lombok features intact.

**Additional context**
`lombok.val` was added to reduce boilerplate, and up to Java 10 that was indeed true. With the arrival of `final var` there is less of a need to use `lombok.val`, and  it can be [troublesome for other tools](https://github.com/openrewrite/rewrite/issues/1297) to work with.

To keep things simple support could be limited to Java 11+; that way there's no need to replace `val` with the actual type on Java 8.

At present delombok produces the code to a separate target folder; instructions can be provided to move the altered files back into the source folder to complete the phase out of `lombok.val`. Even better if replacement could be done in place, but that's optional.

**Describe the bug**
[@Annotations](https://projectlombok.org/features/experimental/Accessors) API indicates that "getters/setters/with-ers will look at the annotation on the field first, on the type the field is in second". With v2.24 a change was introduced requiring annotation properties to be explicitly set which broke existing code where `@Annotations` with defaults on field level were used to override a behavior set on type level.

**To Reproduce**
```java
@Accessors(fluent = true)
@Getter
@Setter
public class AccessorsTest {
    private int intValue;
    @Accessors
    private boolean done;
}
```

In v2.24 the compilation produces warning:
```
AccessorsTest.java:12: warning: Accessors on its own does nothing. Set at least one parameter
    @Accessors
    ^
1 warning
```

Default `#fluent = false` for `@Annotation` on field level is ignored. Getters and setters are generated with  `#fluent = true`:
```java
public class AccessorsTest {
    private int intValue;
    private boolean done;

    public AccessorsTest() {
    }

    public int intValue() {
        return this.intValue;
    }

    public boolean done() {
        return this.done;
    }

    public AccessorsTest intValue(int var1) {
        this.intValue = var1;
        return this;
    }

    public AccessorsTest done(boolean var1) {
        this.done = var1;
        return this;
    }
}
```


**Expected behavior**
`@Accessors` on the field has default `#fluent = false` and according to API should be read first. Hence should override the type level annotation and create getters/setters according to bean specification:
```java
    public boolean isDone() {
        return this.done;
    }

    public void setDone(boolean var1) {
        this.done = var1;
    }
```
This is how it worked with pre-v2.24 versions.

**Version info (please complete the following information):**
 - Lombok version: 2.24
 - Platform: javac 
```
java version "11.0.4" 2019-07-16 LTS
Java(TM) SE Runtime Environment 18.9 (build 11.0.4+10-LTS)
Java HotSpot(TM) 64-Bit Server VM 18.9 (build 11.0.4+10-LTS, mixed mode)
```
 
**Additional context**
If `#fluent = false` is set explicitly on field level, getters/setters are created as expected.

```
@SuperBuilder
class A {
    private int id;
    protected int type;
    
    public A() {
        type = 1;
    }
}

@SuperBuilder
class B extends A {
    public B() {
        type = 2;
    }
}
```

I need to init type in subclass when using SuperBuilder, how to do that?

**Describe the feature**
It is common approach to code against interface and to invoke the required implementation of it in certain times. But in some cases we need to filter all of the implementations and find the one that we need then use it (e.g. strategy pattern).
The feature would be an annotation that can expose the implementing class type. E.g.:
Having the following simplified classes:

interface Common \<T\>;

class Type1 {};

class Type2 {};

class Service1 implements Common<Type1> {};

class Service2 implements Common<Type2> {};

-Lomboked version:
@AnnotationName (e.g. exposeImlementingClass)
class Service1 implements Common<Type1>{};

@AnnotationName (e.g. exposeImlementingClass)
class Service2 implements Common<Type2>{};

-What lombok should do:
class Service1 implementCommon<Type1> {
      // Lombok generated
   public Class<Type1> getImplementingType() {
      return Type1.class;
   }
}
 And for the second service:
class Service2 implementCommon<Type2> {
   // Lombok generated
   public Class<Type2> getImplementingType() {
      return Type1.class;
   }
}

