I consider suppressBaseMethods = true a reasonable default, as it prevents "surprising" behaviour if a class that I "extended" gets new methods (maybe by an updated dependency), that formerly existed only as extension methods. suppressBaseMethods = true results in my own extension methods still being used.

Another use case I like is the ability to create null-safe variants e.g. for string:
```
class MyStringExtension {
  public static void isEmpty(Sting s) {
      return s==null || s.isEmpty();
  }
}
```

allows code like:
```
@ExtensionMethod(MyStringExtension.class)
...
String s = ...;
if (s.isEmpty()) {
    // Do something if string is not present
}
```

I find it rather handy and use it a lot.

Anyhow: the scenario you describes seems to be more related to creating extension methods specifically for `Object` or any superclass that might have a declared subclass, that gives a conflicting alternative implementation. In that situation the extension method and the subclass-method are in a competing state, and I wouldn't know which one I'd use by intuition. Thus an error message seems fine for me.

I never had a problem like yours, but most likely because it never occurred to me to use extension methods with `Object` ;-) ... So far I always wanted to extend some specific class or interface, so the default behavior worked well for me.

Interesting new perspectives you bring! :-)

