**Describe the bug**
Calling an extension method on `this` does not work. Assigning `this` to a local variable first, and then calling the extension method works fine.

In this example, `.hello()` is defined as an extension method on `MyClass`, and `this` is also an instance of that class:
```
// This fails with: error: cannot find symbol
this.hello();
//  ^
//  symbol: method hello()
// 1 error

// but works perfectly fine, when I assign "this" to a local variable first
MyClass a = this;
a.hello();

// even "val" is no problem
val b = this;
b.hello();
```
This only happens with `javac`, Eclipse has no problem with it.

**To Reproduce**
See here for an example project:
https://github.com/marc-guenther/lombok-problems

Example is in the file [ProblemWithJavacButNotEclipse2.java](https://github.com/marc-guenther/lombok-problems/blob/master/prob/lems/ProblemWithJavacButNotEclipse2.java). Run `make problem2` to see the output of `javac`.

**Expected behavior**
Two things:
- javac and Eclipse should show the same behaviour
- `this.someExtensionMethod()` of course should work.

**Version info (please complete the following information):**
- Lombok version: 1.18.24
- Platform: javac 1.8.0_282, Eclipse 2019-03 (4.11.0)

I would like to have a copy constructor as described in https://github.com/projectlombok/lombok/issues/908

It would be VERY usefull to enforce the usage of a SPECIFIC type (and not it's subclasses) in some scenarios.

**Additional context**
We're using Lombok on all our models, however, there's a very usefull feature missing: a copy (deep or shallow) constructor. I know that there's a toBuilder feature, however, it will create a instance of the same type of the original one, however, there are scenarios when we need to cast it to a SPECIFIC superclass. Example:

Let's say we've two objects:

```
@SuperBuilder -and all other needed annotations-
public class DataForFront {
    private String name;
}

@SuperBuilder -and all other needed annotations-
public class InternalData extends DataForFront {
    private String someDataNotMeantForFrontEnd;
}
```

I want someDataNotMeantForFrontEnd to be serialized for other reasons when using InternalData , however, for DataForFront  i don't want it to be serialized. If i directy pass InternalData  to the front, I would reveal someDataNotMeantForFrontEnd, so we usually create a copy constructor in DataForFront and just use it to ensure the info is removed from the object (or using the Converter pattern, it really depends on the project: we try to be consistent with the existing project).

This methodology has lead to some issues when a developer that doesn't have a full understanding of the code or our methodology adds some data and doesn't create proper JUnit tests.

Just to be clear, this is an example of the differente between the copy constructor and toBuilder approaches.

In this case, copy is actually a instance of InternalData  with all the data inside
```
InternalData data = <blah>;
DataForFront copy = data.toBuilter().build();
```
In this case, copy is an instance of DataForFront that only contains the fields declared in that specific class
```
InternalData data = <blah>;
DataForFront copy = new DataForFront(data);
```

**For our specific scenarios, a shallow copy constructor would be sufficient**, however, deep copy constructors can "easilly" be achieved by nesting this feature (by requiring that every mutable object has a copy constructor itself) <- I've once tried to automatically generate deep copy constructors / cloners myself in the past, and I know it's not an easy task if you want to cover all scenarios, but I'm pretty sure that a compromise solution can be achieved by forcing the user to annotate every parameter with instructions about how to behave in the copy constructor 

**I'm aware that a deep-copy constructor is a very complex feature that is very hard to promote to production without concerns, so let's start with a shallow copy constructor and see if people finds the use case usefull. I'm more than happy to study the repo and write a PR myself if you're interested in the feature but don't have time for it.**

If you are interested in this feature and find it MAY be usefull, I'll write down my ideas about how to cover most common scenarios with field-level annotations to allow the core to create a working constructor. Primitives are easy to deep-copy, so they don't need annotations. Subclasses will depend on superclass copy constructor, and use annotations in order to get instructions of how to perform the copy (use getter or private property? / rely on a copy constructor or use a toBuilter() on the property? etc.

Some colleagues consider copy-constructors as an anti-pattern in object oriented scenarios and Converters as the way to go. I'd probably agreed with them before knowing Lombok, but the ENORMOUS amount of work saved during the maintenance cycle of a project by using this pattern with lombok overweighs all the possible caveats: I know Dogs are also Animals, but we work with REST services and we only want the consumer to know the Animal properties, but the intermediate operations performed in backend may need to know about the Dog properties, and we've very serious reasons to do so (from network perfomance to privacy, depending the specific use-case).

