TL;dr: A problem when compiling with Maven and IDEA (which might not occur with Eclipse) causes a class-level Lombok `@Builder` annotation to produce incorrect code; a workaround exists.

**Description**
I have a POJO class with several fields, and a Lombok `@Builder` annotation at the class level and also on the all-argument constructor. The fields are declared in one order, and the all-argument constructor declares the corresponding parameters in a different order.

When compiling or running, the behavior indicates that it's attempting to call the constructor with the values in the field order, instead of the parameter order.

**To Reproduce**
For example, this code does not compile with Maven or in IDEA:

```
package com.ringdna.testutilities;

import lombok.Builder;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;


public class BugReport {

    @Test
    public void demonstrateBug() {

        var bugDemo = BugDemo.builder().id(4L).account(2345L).name("Fred").active(true).build();

        assertThat(bugDemo).isNotNull();
        assertThat(bugDemo.id()).isEqualTo(4L);
    }


    @Builder
    public static class BugDemo {

        private final Long id;
        private final Long account;
        private final String name;
        private final Boolean active;


        @Builder
        public BugDemo(Long id, String name, Long account, Boolean active) {

            this.id = id;
            this.account = account;
            this.name = name;
            this.active = active;
        }


        public Long id() {

            return id;
        }
    }
}

```
When I try to run the unit test, I see this error as it attempts to build:
`incompatible types: java.lang.Long cannot be converted to java.lang.String`

If I instead change the order of the order of the constructor arguments to id, account, name, active (same order as field declaration), it compiles and runs, passing the test as expected.

If I change the order of the constructor arguments to account, id, name, active (swapping account and id from field declaration order), it compiles and runs, but fails the second assertion with the id being 2345; debugging shows that id is 2345 and account is 4. This strongly suggests that the arguments were presented in the order of the fields instead of the order of constructor parameters.

Removing the constructor-level `@Builder` annotation has no effect and the problem remains.

Removing the class-level `@Builder` annotation causes the problem to be solved. I am using this as a workaround.

I see the same behavior using maven to run the test, or using IntelliJ IDEA to run the test. My colleague using Eclipse doesn't seem to see the error.

**Expected Behavior**
The `@Builder` annotation, at class level, should call the constructor correctly.

**Version Info**
Lombok version: 1.18.24
```
