Hi,

this time no direct PR because I have no idea how to proceed with that. I've noticed that every compilation unit is checked on the fact if Lombok is disabled or not in `JavacTransformer`.

```
public void transform(long priority, Context context, List<JCCompilationUnit> compilationUnits, CleanupRegistry cleanup) {
	for (JCCompilationUnit unit : compilationUnits) {
		if (!Boolean.TRUE.equals(LombokConfiguration.read(ConfigurationKeys.LOMBOK_DISABLE, JavacAST.getAbsoluteFileLocation(unit)))) {
			// ... 
		}
	}
}
```

There are two things that pop out here.
- The purple highlight of reading the actual configuration. Which is currently at around ~1% (might be less when my other PRs are merged though)
- Getting the absolute file location of the compilation unit, which ultimately leads to the `stat64` syscall (for checking if it's a directory or not), which is at ~1.5% currently

<img width="860" alt="image" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/6304496/221117700-bfa1dc9e-3749-4b2d-bd0c-ee6831cbfb5e.png">

Overall this accounts for 2-3% at the moment. Which is unfortunate, because in 99% of the cases you don't have Lombok disabled  I'd say.
Moreover, most of the time you only have no `lombok.config` files at all, maybe one, sometimes a few in your project. It seems like an overhead to check if there is a `lombok.config` alongside of every compilation unit.

I'm wondering if it would make sense to refactor the config loading overall and check for the `lombok.config` files already upfront and pass them to the transformer. Or given that `ConfigurationKeys.LOMBOK_DISABLE` is rarely used, doing a check on all these upfront if the key is present somewhere and if not skipping this check alltogether.

I really have no clue how to tackle this best. Maybe you have ideas, hence me sharing this.

Cheers,
Christoph

