**Description of the feature :**
Each method in Java should have an entry and exit trace log, that can be useful to trace the flow of program execution. Having a new annotation (or attributes to the current logger family) on the class level should insert the trace logs automatically for all the methods.
Currently, it is possible to achieve such logging by using Aspect Oriented Programming, but Lombok has the power to make it much easier and cleaner.
Furthermore an option can also be provided to print the time taken for the method execution, though it can be ignored if it adds too much overhead.

With Lombok:
```
@Slf4j(traceEntry=true, traceExit=true, traceTime=true)
public class TraceTestClass{
    
    //Variables, Constructors etc

    public void method1(){
        //Implement method1 functionality
    }
    public void method2(){
        //Implement method2 functionality
    }
}
```
Without  Lombok:
```
public class TraceTestClass{
    
    //Variables, Constructors etc
    Logger log; //Initialize log variable with Log4j, Slf4j, Logback etc

    public void method1(){
        log.trace("Entry method1");
        long entryTimeMillis = System.currentTimeMillis();
        //Implement method1 functionality
        long exitTimeMillis = System.currentTimeMillis();
        log.trace("Exit method1");
        log.trace("Time taken for method1 execution :"+(exitTimeMillis - entryTimeMillis));
    }
    public void method2(){
        log.trace("Entry method2");
        long entryTimeMillis = System.currentTimeMillis();
        //Implement method2 functionality
        long exitTimeMillis = System.currentTimeMillis();
        log.trace("Exit method2");
        log.trace("Time taken for method2 execution :"+(exitTimeMillis - entryTimeMillis));
    }
}
```


**Target audience :**
Anyone who is developing Java program. The major benefit is in the development phase and not really useful for Production.

