**Describe the bug**
`@Singular` is sensitive to race conditions for `Map`s. When trying to set a value on a builder that uses `@Singular` on a map from multiple threads, and then calling `.build()`, it can happen that the `.build()` will crash with an `IndexOutOfBoundsException`.

**To Reproduce**
Since it's a race condition it's tricky to reproduce, but I've found that retrying enough times with a decent concurrency results in pretty consistent failure. I've used the following code to trigger this:

```java
public class SingularRaceTest {

	@Value
	@Builder
	public static class TagContainer {
		@Singular
		Map<String, Long> tags;
	}

	public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
		ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
		for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
			System.out.println("line " + i);
			TagContainer.TagContainerBuilder test = TagContainer.builder();
			List<Callable<Long>> tasks = LongStream.range(0, 10)
					.mapToObj(j -> (Callable<Long>) () -> {
						test.tag("key-" + j, j);
						return j;
					})
					.toList();
			executorService.invokeAll(tasks);
			test.build();
		}
	}
}
```

This will at some point throw the following exception:

```
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.IndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 9 out of bounds for length 9
	at java.base/jdk.internal.util.Preconditions.outOfBounds(Preconditions.java:64)
	at java.base/jdk.internal.util.Preconditions.outOfBoundsCheckIndex(Preconditions.java:70)
	at java.base/jdk.internal.util.Preconditions.checkIndex(Preconditions.java:266)
	at java.base/java.util.Objects.checkIndex(Objects.java:359)
	at java.base/java.util.ArrayList.get(ArrayList.java:427)
	at me.magnet.explore.SingularRaceTest$TagContainer$TagContainerBuilder.build(SingularRaceTest.java:17)
	at me.magnet.explore.SingularRaceTest.main(SingularRaceTest.java:35)
```

**Expected behavior**
It should build the object from the builder with the correct values set in the map.


**Version info (please complete the following information):**
 - Lombok version: 1.18.28
 - Platform: javac 17.0.6

**Additional context**
Looking at the generated code:

```java
            public SingularRaceTest.TagContainer.TagContainerBuilder tag(String tagKey, Long tagValue) {
                if (this.tags$key == null) {
                    this.tags$key = new ArrayList();
                    this.tags$value = new ArrayList();
                }

                this.tags$key.add(tagKey);
                this.tags$value.add(tagValue);
                return this;
            }
```

In case two threads call this method at the same time while `tags$key` is `null`, I suspect it can happen that `tags$key` and `tags$value` get initialized by different threads, thus resulting in the behavior we're seeing.

