**Describe the feature**
Many businesses use enumerations to describe the meaning of a set field
And the structure and methods of the following enumeration are very common. Previously I used @Getter and @AllArgsConstructor to simplify the sample code somewhat.
But there are still a lot of cumbersome and consistent methods that need to be written. 
For example, to determine whether it is an enumeration value, for example, to determine whether the code is legal or illegal.

**Describe the target audience**
For anyone who regularly writes business code, this use of enumerations to represent the meaning of setting items is a very standard and elegant way, just in my opinion, with my shallow knowledge.

**Additional context**
`import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public enum SettingDescEnum {

    ON(10, "switch on"),
    OFF(20, "switch off");

    private static final Map<Integer, SettingDescEnum> map = Arrays.stream(SettingDescEnum
            .values()).collect(Collectors.toMap(SettingDescEnum::getCode, Function.identity()));

    private final Integer code;
    private final String desc;

    SettingDescEnum(Integer code, String desc) {
        this.code = code;
        this.desc = desc;
    }

    public static boolean isValid(Integer code) {
        return map.containsKey(code);
    }

    public static boolean isNotValid(Integer code) {
        return !map.containsKey(code);
    }

    public Integer getCode() {
        return code;
    }

    public String getDesc() {
        return desc;
    }

    public boolean isOn(Integer code) {
        return ON.code.equals(code);
    }

    public boolean isOff(Integer code) {
        return OFF.code.equals(code);
    }
}
`

Context:
When generation of nullity annotations is switched on, Lobok is expected to generate appropriate nullity annotations for the flavor chosen. In my case, I used the Eclipse flavor (`lombok.addNullAnnotations = eclipse`) but the issue quite probably applies to the more general case. 

What is happening:
The solution for the builder pattern is not complete. Nullity of the `build()` method in the abstract builder is not defined, making the rest effectively unsuable.

Details (and how to reproduce):

I simplified the generated builder pattern to the bare bones to illustrate the issue. Lombok thus generates something like this:
```
import org.eclipse.jdt.annotation.NonNull;

public class Bar {
    public static @NonNull BarBuilder<?, ?> builder() {
        return new BarBuilderImpl();
    }
    
    public void doIt() {
    }

    public static abstract class BarBuilder<C extends Bar, B extends Bar.BarBuilder<C, B>> {
        public abstract C build();
    }

    private static class BarBuilderImpl extends BarBuilder<Bar, BarBuilderImpl> {
        public @NonNull Bar build() {
            return new Bar();
        }
    }
}
```

Now, assume that another code accesses the builder:
```
class Foo {
    void aMethod() {
        var bar = Bar.builder().build();
        bar.doIt();
    }
}
```

When the compiler dereferences the local variable `bar`, it has no way of saying if the value may be `null` or not. In the concrete class `BarBuilderImpl`, Lombok correctly annotates the `build>()` method with `@NonNull` but the compiler sees only its superclass when accessing the builder. In the abstract superclass, the method has the signature `public abstract C build();` without a nullity annotation. There aren`t any nullity constraints on C either.

Expected behavior:
There are two possible solutions:

Either the abstract build method must also be annotated:
```
import org.eclipse.jdt.annotation.NonNull;

public class Bar {
    public static @NonNull BarBuilder<?, ?> builder() {
