In 99,99% of the use cases you write:
`var person = new Person()`
Only 0.01% of the use cases requires immutability where you write `final var`.

And there are better frameworks for immutability, e.g. [Immutables](https://immutables.github.io/).

Because `val` does not guarantee immutability, see the example below: 
```
public static void main(string[] args]) {
   val person = createPerson();
   person.setName("Jane Dow"); // mutated
}

public static final createPerson() {
   val person = new Person("John Dow");
   return person;
}
```
This is something you cannot do with  [Immutables](https://immutables.github.io/).

In conclusion, whats the purpose of writing `val` instead of  `var` (when `final` does not guarantee immutability)?

The answer: nothing, because `val` does not fit into the bigger picture of immutability.

