nodes.body:
The Eclipse handler (and the javac handler, too) adds the log-line before the static block, resulting in the following code:
```
class LoggerSlf4jWithStaticInitializer {
  private static final org.slf4j.Logger log = org.slf4j.LoggerFactory.getLogger(LoggerSlf4jWithStaticInitializer.class);
  static {
    log.debug("test");
  }
}
```
The [JLS spec describes in section 12.4.2](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se17/html/jls-12.html#jls-12.4.2) how the initialization process works:
> `6.` [...] Then, initialize the static fields of C which are constant variables [...]
> `9.` Next, execute [...] the class variable initializers and static initializers of the class [...], in textual order, as though they were a single block.

So `log` cannot be `null` in the static initializer code, unless `org.slf4j.LoggerFactory.getLogger()` returns `null`. However, as this method from SLF4J is not annotated with a `@NonNull` annotation, the Eclipse analyzer is technically correct to claim it may return `null`.

This is not an issue with lombok, this is an issue with SLF4J not having the return type necessary to pass the Non-Null analysis. Lombok must not mess around with the type system.

