**Describe the feature**
Add a parameter ("interfaces" ?) in the @Builder and @SuperBuilder annotations, so that the generated builders declares implementing these interfaces.

When handling a lot of DTO, they likely contain data (or methods) of domains ("domain A", "domain B", "error domain"...) that start crossing each others : it is very likely there will be DTO for "errors" in "domain A", DTO that merges A and B domains, etc. 
Adding interfaces to those DTO helps keeping the code tidy by having classes that initialize/handle them depending on their domain. Adding interfaces to theirs builders is uneasy though, as it requires declaring the empty Builder class (which is complex for a @SuperBuilder). 

Thus, the following code
 ```
@SuperBuilder(interfaces={DomainABuilderInterface, ErrorBuilderInterface}
class DomainAErrorMessage extends X implements DomainAInterface, ErrorInterface {
    int idDomainA;
    String contentDomainA;
    String errorCode;
    String errorMessage
}
```
Would generate :
 ```
class DomainAErrorMessage extends X  implements DomainAInterface, ErrorInterface {
    int idDomainA;
    String contentDomainA;
    String errorCode;
    String errorMessage


    public static abstract class DomainAErrorMessageBuilder
            <
                   C extends DomainAErrorMessage, 
                   B extends DomainAErrorMessageBuilder<C, B>
            >
            extends XBuilder<C, B>
            implements DomainABuilderInterface, ErrorBuilderInterface {
            ...
    }
}
```

**Describe the target audience**
Any programmer using a lot of DTO (for messaging, HTTP requests/responses...), who wants to factorize their handling.

**Additional context**
I am not a lombok expert, but I tend to think this is a simple change, that should not hide traps in corner cases.

Add a @SuperConstructor annotation so that instead of 
```java
public class A extends B { 
    public A(int i) {
        super(i);
    }
}
```
we can just have
 ```java
@SuperConstructor
public class A extends B { 
}
```

A proof of concept for this being possible is [here](https://pastecord.com/pehabekusa.php)

