Came here to raise this exact same issue :) 

Did get as far as writing a smaller test case for it, so will share that in case it's useful.

```java
    import org.springframework.lang.NonNull;

    @Test
    void checkNonNull() {

        assertThatThrownBy(() -> TestClass.builder().build())
            .describedAs("Checking class has non-null fields")
            // passes
            .isInstanceOf(NullPointerException.class);

        assertThatThrownBy(() -> TestRecord.builder().build())
            .describedAs("Checking record has non-null fields")
            // fails with Expecting code to raise a throwable.  
            .isInstanceOf(NullPointerException.class);

    }

    @Builder
    public static class TestClass {
        @org.springframework.lang.NonNull
        private final String value;
    }

    @Builder
    record TestRecord(@org.springframework.lang.NonNull String value) {

    }
```

Lombok already does this if tagged `@lombok.NonNull`. The current lack of inconsistency is IMHO extremely confusing. Consider the following four essentially identical classes/records:
```java
    @lombok.Builder
    public static class TestLombokClass {
        @lombok.NonNull
        private final String value;
    }

    @lombok.Builder
    public static class TestSpringClass {
        @org.springframework.lang.NonNull
        private final String value;
    }

    @lombok.Builder
    record TestLombokRecord(@lombok.NonNull String value) {
    }

    @lombok.Builder
    record TestSpringRecord(@org.springframework.lang.NonNull String value) {
    }
```
At attempt to invoke 
* `TestLombokClass.builder.build()` fails, because value is null.
* `TestSpringClass.builder.build()` fails, because value is null.
* `TestLombokRecord.builder.build()` fails, because value is null.

An attempt to invoke
* `TestSpringRecord.builder().build()` succeeds, creating a record with a `null` value.

In your `@Data`-examples lombok generates the constructor, in your `record`-examples they are already there. So you want that lombok handles record constructors different than normal class constructors?

Possible, but this might lead to some confusion. For example this
```java
record A(@spring.NonNull String a) {
}
```
will generate no null-check but this
```java
@Builder
record A(@spring.NonNull String a) {
}
```
will, even if you do not use the generated builder. 

`@lombok.NonNull` always works because it is not only a marker annotation like the other ones but also it modifies the annotated method and adds a null-check. It also works with existing normal methods like `public void test(@lombok.NonNull) { ... } `.

A possible solution would be to allow @AllArgsConstructor on records, then it becomes clear that the constructor is generade by lombok.

